[[entries]]
name = "heap_allocation_patterns"
description = "How to structure heap-allocated data in a language without structs-on-heap"
category = "cyrius"
---
Cyrius has structs, but for heap data you typically use raw alloc + store64/load64.
The pattern that emerged across both projects:

1. POINTER + ACCESSOR FUNCTIONS:

   var map_nodes = 0;
   fn map_alloc() { map_nodes = alloc(1024 * 112); }
   fn map_node_x(idx) { return load64(map_nodes + idx * 112); }
   fn map_node_y(idx) { return load64(map_nodes + idx * 112 + 8); }

   This is essentially manual vtable-free OOP. The accessor functions ARE
   your type safety. Name them consistently: module_field(index).

2. ENUM FOR LAYOUT CONSTANTS:

   enum BspNode {
       BSP_NODE_SIZE = 112;
       BSP_NODE_X = 0;
       BSP_NODE_Y = 8;
       BSP_NODE_DX = 16;
   }

   Enums cost zero gvar_toks. Use them for every struct layout.

3. LAZY INIT GUARD:

   fn framebuf_set_palette(data) {
       if (palette == 0) { palette = alloc(768); }
       memcpy(palette, data, 768);
   }

   We found a real segfault from this: framebuf_set_palette() was called
   before framebuf_init() allocated the buffer. The lazy guard fixed it.
   Always guard heap pointers that might be called before init.

4. PACK SMALL STATE:

   # Instead of 3 globals:
   var player_start = 0;
   fn map_find_player_start() {
       if (player_start == 0) { player_start = alloc(24); }
       store64(player_start, x);
       store64(player_start + 8, y);
       store64(player_start + 16, angle);
   }
   fn player_start_x() { return load64(player_start); }

[[entries]]
name = "enum_as_namespace"
description = "Using enums as zero-cost namespaced constants"
category = "cyrius"
---
Enums in Cyrius are compile-time integer constants. They don't consume
gvar_toks slots — they const-fold, and only NON-literal top-level
`var X = expr;` inits take a gvar_toks slot at all. (This line used to
say "a separate initialization path in cc2"; `cc2` has not been the
compiler since the v3.x era — the current one is `cycc`. See
`diagnostics_caps.cyml::the_gvar_wall` for the live table.)

This makes them perfect for constants that would otherwise eat your
global variable budget:

  # BAD: 8 gvar_toks slots
  var MAP_MAX_VERTEXES = 2048;
  var MAP_MAX_LINEDEFS = 2048;
  var MAP_MAX_SIDEDEFS = 2048;
  ...

  # GOOD: 0 gvar_toks slots
  enum MapMax {
      MAP_MAX_VERTEXES = 2048;
      MAP_MAX_LINEDEFS = 2048;
      MAP_MAX_SIDEDEFS = 2048;
      ...
  }

Both produce identical code — the values are inlined at use sites.

WARNING: Enum VALUES must be non-negative integers — `enum E { NEG = -1; }`
still errors 'expected number, got -' (verified v6.4.2). For a negative
constant, declare it as a top-level `var FIXED_NEG_ONE = 0 - 65536;` or
`var FIXED_NEG_ONE = -65536;` — negative literals DO work in general
expressions now; it is only the enum-value grammar position that rejects
the leading `-`.

In cyrius-doom, converting constants to enums saved 30+ gvar_toks slots,
which made the difference between compiling and not compiling.

[[entries]]
name = "var_name_bracket_is_bytes_not_elements"
description = "`var name[N]` in a FUNCTION is N bytes (rounded up to 8); the SAME `var name[N]` at TOP LEVEL is N i64 SLOTS = N*8 bytes. Two different sizes for one syntax. Use `var a: i64[N]` (v6.2.1) to mean slots in both positions."
category = "cyrius"
---
THE TWO MEANINGS — this is the part that bites hardest, and it is
NOT what the original note below said:

    fn f() {
        var buf[16];       # 16 BYTES (rounded up to a multiple of 8)
    }
    var buf[16];           # TOP LEVEL: 16 i64 SLOTS = 128 BYTES

Same three tokens, an 8× difference, decided by whether you are inside
a function. The top-level form was fixed to N*8 at v6.4.10 (it had been
inconsistent); the fn-local form has always been bytes.

WHERE THE TOP-LEVEL FORM BIT US: **the long-HOME overflow bug** (fixed v6.4.81). A
compiler-internal `var _cyrius_lib[256]` is 2048 bytes, not 256 — and
the code that copied into it had been reasoned about as if it were 256.
Reading a bare `var x[N]` and mentally assigning it N bytes at top
level is a live source of both over- and under-estimates.

THE FIX — SAY WHICH YOU MEAN (v6.2.1):

    var a: i64[N];     # N i64 slots = N*8 bytes — SAME in fn and top level
    var a: u8[N];      # N bytes explicitly — SAME in both
                       # widths i8..i64 / u8..u64, u128

The element width is registered into the var-size table, so every
backend (x86 / aarch64 / macho / pe / cx) inherits it. Prefer the
annotated form in new code; it removes the position dependence entirely.

THE ORIGINAL BITE (fn-local, still accurate):

    var buf[16];       # 16 bytes — OK for a 2-u64 pair
    var state[25];     # 25 BYTES — NOT 25 u64 lanes!
    var lanes[200];    # 200 bytes = 25 × u64 lanes

Surfaced v5.4.15 writing `lib/keccak.cyr` — `var state[25]` gave
25 bytes (should have been 200). First `memset(state, 0, 200)`
overwrote 175 bytes of adjacent stack locals including the
caller's return-address region. SIGSEGV on the first absorb.

RULE OF THUMB for the unannotated form: inside a fn, size it as
`N * 8` (or `N * sizeof(thing)`) yourself; at top level, do NOT — the
compiler already multiplied.

A BARE ARRAY TAKES NO SUBSCRIPT. `b[i]` on a `var b[N]` is refused by
name — since 6.7.8 "cannot subscript 'b': a bare `var a[N]` states no
element width - declare `var a: T[N]` …" — because the element width
is exactly what the bare form does not say. Annotate it to index it;
`load*` / `store*` at a byte offset stay valid. Under the opt-in
bounds-checked mode (6.7.8) an annotated `u8[5]` is FIVE elements, not
its 8-byte slot: `a[5]` exits 134 even inside the rounded storage.

⚠ 6.7.11 — cyrlint now catches the CONSTANT overrun. `var vals[5]` in a fn holds ONE i64 (8 bytes); four `store64`s at
+0..+24 overrun the frame silently (the check driver itself did exactly that until 6.7.11). cyrlint warns on a
literal-offset `load*/store*(&a + K)` past a bare local; mark a deliberate offset `# lint: offset-ok` (on the call's
name line). Since 6.7.12 (TL-21) N is sized as the compiler sizes it — a literal, a `const NAME = <literal>;`, an enum
constant with a literal value (bare or `E.X`), or `+ - *` over those — and a TOP-LEVEL bare `var g[N]` is checked too,
at N × 8 bytes, in every fn that does not shadow it; the warning names the spelling and its value. Use
`var vals: i64[5]` for five slots.

⚠ 6.7.14 — THE OVERRUN CAN STAY INVISIBLE UNTIL THE FN GROWS (a consumer crash, closed: not a cyrius bug). `var
argv2[8];` in a fn is ONE 8-byte slot, so `store64(&argv2 + 8, ..)` writes the PREVIOUS local's home. While the fn has
at most 256 local slots the register allocator keeps that local in a register, and the clobbered home is never read;
past the picker's cap the fn is not register-allocated, the local is read from its clobbered slot, and an EARLIER call
faults once the fn grows (a block adding 14 local slots, 244 → 258, moved a SIGSEGV into group 3 of a test suite).
`CYRIUS_REGALLOC_PICKER_CAP=0` unmasks such an overrun at once. Size the array as slots (`var a: i64[2]`), and run
`cyrius lint` from a 6.7.11-or-later toolchain: it names `store64(&a + 8)` past `var a[8]`.

[[entries]]
name = "stack_var_keyword_for_struct_locals"
description = "v5.5.36 `stack var p: Point;` forces a named struct onto the frame; `var p: Point;` also now parses"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.5.36 added the `stack` storage-class keyword for named struct
locals, plus `var p: Point;` (no RHS) as a parse-level way to
declare a struct-typed local. Both allocate on the frame:

    fn demo() {
        stack var p: Point;       // explicit (preferred when
                                  // you want readers to notice)
        p.x = 1;
        p.y = 2;
    }

    fn demo2() {
        var q: Point;             // same effect, shorter form
        q.x = 3;
    }

`stack` is reserved (TOK=111). Variable and field names can't
use `stack` any more — rename to `stk`/`sp` if you collide.

Struct locals that previously needed anonymous filler `var`
slots to land at a known rbp disp can now just declare at the
struct type directly.

[[entries]]
name = "fn_main_is_not_the_entry_point"
description = "⛔ CORRECTED v6.6.5, RE-MEASURED v6.6.14 — CYCC AUTO-CALLS `main()` (since v5.9.37), and this entry said the opposite for eighteen minors. The live trap is the reverse: a source that defines `fn main` AND calls it at top level without exiting runs the body TWICE and exits with the SECOND run's value — a trailing `var r = assert_summary();` is dead for the exit code. Top-level statements still run first; `main` is called after them."
category = "cyrius"
---
⛔ **CORRECTED v6.6.5, RE-MEASURED v6.6.14.** At the end of the entry code `src/main.cyr` looks
`main` up in the fn table (`_find_fn_by_name(S, "main", 4)`) and emits `call main` when it has a
body; the program then exits with main's return value. This entry said the opposite for eighteen
minors, and `language/agents.cyml` kept saying it until 6.6.14.

Measured on cycc 6.6.14 (`./build/cycc < p.cyr > p && ./p`):

| source | stdout | exit |
|---|---|---|
| `fn main(): i64 { return 42; }` | — | 42 |
| `fn main() { syscall(60, 42); return 0; }` | — | 42 |
| no `fn main`; top level ends `var r = helper();` (helper returns 5) | — | 5 |
| `fn main` (prints, returns `count + 10`), then `var ec = main(); var r = 7;` | body TWICE | **12** — the second run; `r` ignored |
| the same, then `syscall(60, ec);` after the call | body once | 11 |
| `fn main` with a failing `assert_eq`, then `var ec = main(); var r = assert_summary();` | FAIL twice, `0 passed, 1 failed` | **0** |

⭐ **THE LIVE TRAP fails SILENTLY GREEN.** The auto-call is unconditional — it does not know you
already called `main` — so the last row reports a failure and exits 0. At 6.6.5 a control build
of `tests/tcyr/crossos/syscall_shm_fd_passing.tcyr` printed `19 passed, 30 failed` and exited 0;
at 6.6.14 a draft TLS test in the same shape hid 42 failed assertions.

THREE SHAPES ARE CORRECT:
  (a) no `fn main` at all — top level ends on the value to exit with (`var r = assert_summary();`
      then `syscall(SYS_EXIT, r);`);
  (b) `fn main` that RETURNS the value and is NOT called at top level — the auto-call runs it once;
  (c) `fn main` called at top level FOLLOWED BY an explicit `syscall(SYS_EXIT, r);`, which exits
      before the auto-call is reached. This is what the `tests/tcyr/crossos/` syscall tests use.

THE MECHANISM (from the v5.6.33 entry, which predates the auto-call): top-level statements ARE
the program. The entry branches over every fn body, runs enum inits, global inits and the
top-level statements in source order, then — since v5.9.37 — `call main` if one exists, and exits
with main's return, else with the last top-level `var`'s value. Before v5.9.37 a source holding
only `fn main() { … }` exited with whatever was in rax / x0 (argc on Mach-O arm64, so 1), which
is how a "macOS arm64 runtime regression" was filed at v5.6.33 against a fixture whose body never
ran. Two wrong conclusions, both from reading the doc instead of running the compiler.

6.7.11: THE AUTO-CALL IS ON EVERY TARGET, cx INCLUDED. cx never called `main` before 6.7.11 (its exit was the last
global's value), so a cx test that kept its asserts in `main` passed vacuously — and a cx program that defines `main`
AND calls it at top level without an explicit exit now runs it twice, like everywhere else. The three correct shapes
above are unchanged.

[[entries]]
name = "var_buf_in_library_functions"
description = "`var buf[N]` inside a library function is static data — every call shares the SAME buffer, and any Str borrowing from it dangles after the next call"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE TRAP:

    fn parse_file(path) {
        var buf[262144];                       # NOT stack — static data
        var n = file_read_all(path, &buf, 262144);
        return parse(str_new(&buf, n));        # Str's data ptr → static buf
    }

This is the compounded form of "var buf[N] is BYTES not ELEMENTS."
NOTE (v6.3.15 default-on stack arrays): a fn-local `var buf[N]` now
behaves TWO ways by size — SMALL arrays route to per-thread STACK
slots (so a returned `&buf` DANGLES after the fn returns, reused by
the next call), while OVERSIZED arrays (over the per-fn stack budget,
as with `var buf[262144]`) STILL fall back to one shared global
buffer (the compiler emits `note: oversized array local kept in
shared global ... use alloc()` plus the `large static data` warning).
Either way the borrow is unsafe across calls. Sized properly, the
first call works; the second call clobbers the first — every Str the
first call returned now points at the second file's bytes (shared
global) or at reused stack (small).

You see this as parsed values bleeding across calls — IDs become
suffixes/prefixes of later IDs, descriptions become fragments of
the most-recently-read file. Nothing crashes; everything is just
wrong.

The build emits a hint:

    warning: large static data (262144 bytes) — consider alloc()
    for buffers >4K

Heed it. Library helpers that hand out heap pointers must heap-
allocate the backing memory:

    fn parse_file(path) {
        var buf = alloc(262144);               # fresh per call
        var n = file_read_all(path, buf, 262144);
        return parse(str_new(buf, n));
    }

Now the Str's data ptr aliases an alloc that lives until process
exit; consecutive calls keep their backing memory.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

vidya 2.3.0 — `lib/toml.cyr::toml_parse_file` shipped with
`var buf[262144]`. `vidya stats` segfaulted on the first
content-loading command after the cyrius 4.5.0 → 5.7.0 upgrade
because every concept's parsed Str pointed into the *last*
concept.toml's bytes; `concept_id` returned 0/garbage and
`path_join(0, fname)` faulted at NULL+offset. Workaround: read
the file into `alloc(N)` in the caller and call `toml_parse(...)`
directly, bypassing the leaky helper. Permanent fix belongs
upstream in cyrius stdlib.

This trap is invisible at single-call test scope. Every stdlib
helper that returns Str/buf-borrowing values must be exercised
with at least two consecutive calls in tests, otherwise the bug
sleeps until the second consumer arrives.

[[entries]]
name = "global_init_order_silent_zero"
description = "✅ FIXED in v5.7.32 (cyrlint warning) + v5.7.36 (string-literal awareness) — original bug behavior + workaround pattern preserved below for pre-v5.7.32 toolchain users"
category = "cyrius"
---
⭐ **6.6.16 — WHAT IS A CONSTANT INITIALIZER NOW.** An initializer that names an enum
constant (`var x = A;`, `E.A`, `A + 1`, `var x: u8 = A;`), the enum declared above or below,
is a constant: baked into the image like `var x = 42;`. The name resolves as a read of it
does — a later global that hides the enum constant wins. So is a struct literal whose every
field is a constant, and an array list (`var t: i64[3] = {1, 2, E.A};`). Through 6.7.13 the
startup store still ran (see 6.7.14 below); the visible difference was an EARLIER initializer
reading it, which saw the value instead of 0. KERNEL BUILDS: an x86 `kernel;` build runs every OTHER initializer after
the top-level program (the multiboot shim must run first), so a kernel that never returns
never runs them; the compiler warns once per such declaration (not for a host build, an
aarch64 kernel, or an EFI application with `efi_main`). Compiler side: field_notes/compiler/
gotchas.cyml `a_guard_in_the_loop_implies_a_second_pass_somebody_has_to_write`.

⭐ **6.7.14 — A CONSTANT INITIALIZER RUNS NOTHING AT STARTUP (D11 (b), FE-15, FE-17).** A
declaration-zone initializer that folds to an integer constant (a const / enum name, an
expression of them, `= 0`), and a bare float literal (`var G: f64 = 1.5;`, `: f32`, an untyped
`var K = 1.5;`), is the global's value FROM PROGRAM START — the replayed startup store is gone.
What that changes:
  - `var R = INT_MIN % -1;` (and an enum-named `INT_MIN / -1`) starts at 0 / INT_MIN on every
    target; through 6.7.13 the replay re-ran the division and trapped at startup on x86_64, cx
    and PE (136 / the divide exception).
  - An EARLIER initializer's fn call that writes the global KEEPS its write: `fn setg() { G = 9; }
    var r = setg(); var G = C;` (or `= 0`, or `var G: f64 = 1.5;`) leaves G at 9, where the
    startup store reset it — what `var G = 5;` (a static init) always did. The same after an
    earlier NONZERO literal declaration of the name (`var G = 3; …; var G = C;`).
  - A redeclared name whose earlier declaration is computed, `= 0` or a NAMED constant of
    another value kept its order through 6.7.14 (`var a = D; var b = a; var a = C;` gave
    b == D). Since 6.7.15 every constant redeclaration is the value from program start (6.7.15's
    D2 (a), FE-13: b == C) — a_zone_redeclaration_is_its_last_constant_from_program_start_v6715.
  - An x86 `kernel;` build reads a float-literal global (it read 0 and warned); since 6.7.15 a
    float global whose initializer is a constant f64 EXPRESSION (`= CF`, `(1.5)`, `1.5 + 1.0`) is
    baked too (FE-16; 6.7.15's D46 kept the bake). Since 6.7.16 f64 arithmetic with an integer or
    enum RIGHT operand (`1.5 + 1`, `1.5 * E.A`) is baked as well, computed as the run time computes
    it (6.7.16's D15 (a): a_zone_redeclaration_is_its_last_constant_from_program_start_v6715); a
    call, a NaN and an integer LEFT operand (`1 + 1.5`) are still left to the late replay.
  - Since 6.7.15 an all-constant zone STRUCT / TUPLE literal (`var p = P { 1, 2 };`, `(1, 2)`) is
    not stored again at startup either (FE-15): an earlier initializer's call that writes it keeps
    its write.
CHANGELOG [6.7.14] FE-15, FE-15r, FE-17; [6.7.15] FE-13, FE-15, FE-16; [6.7.16] ZN-4.

⚠ **RE-MEASURED at v6.6.5 — the example below no longer reads 0, and the rule now sees
wrapped initializers.** Since v6.4.74 a global whose initializer is a CONSTANT is static-
folded, so `var COMPUTED = FLAG_A | FLAG_B | FLAG_C;` over later `var FLAG_A = 0x1;` …
evaluates to 7 (measured). The silent zero is real for a later global with a COMPUTED
initializer: `var C = FLAG_A | 2;` over a later `var FLAG_A = f(1);` exits 2, not 3.
cyrlint warns on both shapes. Before v6.6.5 it scanned only the declaration's own line, so
a WRAPPED initializer — `var A = 1 +` / `    B;` over a later `var B = g();` — drew no
warning while the binary read B as 0 (exit 1, the reordered program exits 3); it now scans
to the statement's `;` across lines, ignores an `=` inside a trailing comment, and checks a
second `var` after a `;` on the same line. A `}` inside a multi-line string no longer takes
a real top-level declaration out of its sight. CHANGELOG [6.6.5].

✅ FIXED in cyrius v5.7.32. `cyrlint`'s `lint_globals_init_order`
walks the file twice (pass 1 collects every top-level `var IDENT =
...;` with line, pass 2 walks every `var X = expr;` initializer
and emits a warning if any IDENT in the expr was declared at a
line > X's line). v5.7.36 added string-literal awareness so
identifier-shaped substrings inside `"..."` literals don't
false-positive. Tracked at:
`cyrius/docs/development/issues/archived/2026-04-28-global-init-order-forward-ref.md`
(v5.7.32 fix; archived 2026-05-03 during the v5.8.41 cyrlint
cleanup pass). Body below preserved as the v5.8.47 vidya audit
cross-referenced this entry as the canonical example of the
"description was stale; body had the correct upstream status"
pattern.

PRE-v5.7.32 BUG (kept for historical context):

Cyrius initializes top-level `var X = expr;` declarations in source
declaration order. If `expr` references a constant declared LATER in
the same file, that reference resolves to `0` (the default
zero-initialized value) at the time `X` is evaluated. No warning,
no error — `X` ends up holding the wrong value, and downstream
consumers silently see the wrong value too.

    # In src/foo.cyr (single file, declaration order matters):

    var COMPUTED = FLAG_A | FLAG_B | FLAG_C;   # → 0, NOT 7

    # ... 200 lines later ...

    var FLAG_A = 0x1;
    var FLAG_B = 0x2;
    var FLAG_C = 0x4;

    # COMPUTED reads as 0 throughout the program.

A trivial assertion surfaces it instantly:

    fn test() {
        assert_eq(COMPUTED, FLAG_A | FLAG_B | FLAG_C, "computed = OR");
        # → fails with "got 0, expected 7"
    }

WHERE THIS BIT US:

mabda v3 Step 4f.ii (BO page-perms tightening). Added named
perm-bitmask constants near the top of `src/backend_native.cyr`:

    var _NATIVE_PERM_FULL = AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_READABLE
                          | AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_WRITEABLE
                          | AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_EXECUTABLE;

at line 117. The `AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_*` constants lived at line 391+ in
the same file. Result: `_NATIVE_PERM_FULL` evaluated to `0` at load
time. Every BO got mapped with `perms = 0` — no read, no write, no
execute. Every dispatch TDR'd at the 10-second AMDGPU timeout with
"post-dispatch marker stale" + "output unchanged."

Visible failure mode looked like a wedged GPU — ~30 minutes spent
on hardware-iteration hypotheses (firmware near-permanent-wedge
rule, ring corruption, bad VAs) before a CPU regression test
`assert_eq(_NATIVE_PERM_FULL, R|W|X)` returned `got 0, expected 14`
and pinned the actual cause.

Fix: one-line move. `_NATIVE_PERM_*` block relocated below the
`AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_*` defs. Code that was "wrong since the moment I
typed it" started working immediately on hardware.

WORKAROUND PATTERN until the lint lands:

  1. For computed-from-other-constants globals, place the
     definition AFTER its dependencies (forced reordering).
  2. Add CPU regression tests that assert the *value* of every
     computed perm/flag/size constant. These are 5-line tests
     that catch this class of bug before any hardware
     iteration.
  3. When refactoring top-level declarations, re-run the value
     assertions. Reordering is a top-level change with non-local
     consequences in Cyrius.

UPSTREAM STATUS:

**✅ Shipped at v5.7.32 + v5.7.36** — picked option (a):
`cyrlint` rule `lint_globals_init_order` walks the file
twice. Pass 1 collects every top-level `var IDENT = ...;`
and records `(name, line)`. Pass 2 walks every initializer
expression, scans IDENT tokens, and emits a warning if any
IDENT was declared at a line LATER than the current decl:

    warn line N: global var init refs 'NAME' declared at line M
                 (silent zero at init)

v5.7.36 extended the Pass-2 scanner with **string-literal
awareness** — IDENTs nested inside `"..."` strings or `'...'`
chars (with `\\` / `\"` / `\'` escapes) no longer trigger
spurious warnings. So `var MSG = "FLAG_LATER not yet
defined"; var FLAG_LATER = 1;` is clean.

The compile-time error path (option b) was rejected because
existing stdlib shapes rely on declaration order in benign
ways; promoting to error would break green builds across
the ecosystem. Lint warning is the right level — surfaces
the foot-gun without forcing churn.

Affects ANY computed-from-other-constants global: size
calculations, page perms, bit-packed enum compositions,
refactors that reorder top-level declarations, the common
"define module constants near the top of the file for grep-
ability" pattern.

[[entries]]
name = "bump_allocator_exhaustion_in_tests"
description = "The stdlib bump allocator never frees during process lifetime — but since v6.1.19 it CHAINS fresh 256 MB chunks instead of failing, so the DOOM/mabda-era 'exhausted at the 9th alloc(112)' symptom is retired. alloc() returns 0 only for size<=0, size>ALLOC_MAX (2 GiB), or a failed mmap. The no-free property is still real; use an arena when you need to reclaim."
category = "cyrius"
---
⚠ **CORRECTED 2026-08-07 (v6.5.10), by running it.** The MECHANISM
below is out of date and the numbers with it. `lib/alloc.cyr` has
been a CHUNKED bump allocator since **v6.1.19**: it bumps inside the
current chunk and, on overflow, `mmap`s a FRESH 256 MB chunk
(`_LINUX_CHUNK = 0x10000000`) and continues there. Old chunks stay
mapped and valid; a single allocation never spans two. Measured on
this box: 4 × `alloc(100000000)` = 400 MB, crossing the first chunk,
all non-zero.

So today `alloc(N)` returns 0 in exactly three cases:

    N <= 0                       # rejected
    N  > ALLOC_MAX (0x80000000)  # 2 GiB single-allocation cap
    the mmap for a new chunk failed

"Ran out of heap after nine 112-byte contexts" is no longer reachable.
If you are chasing a null from `alloc` today, check the SIZE first —
a negative or overflowed size argument is the live cause, not
cumulative pressure.

⛔ **What IS still true, and is the reason to keep reading:** nothing
is ever freed. `alloc` has no `free`. A long-running process that
allocates per request grows without bound, and the only batch release
is `alloc_reset()`, which **invalidates EVERY pointer the allocator
has ever handed out** — including ones the stdlib itself is holding.
Any `Str`, `Vec`, `HashMap`, arena or struct built before the reset is
dangling afterwards, and the span is zeroed AND re-issuable, so a
stale pointer reads zeros until something else is allocated over it
and then reads that. v6.5.7 fixed one instance of this *inside the
stdlib* — `default_alloc()` memoized its vtable in the very arena it
describes, so `alloc_reset()` invalidated the allocator itself and
every later `default_alloc()` returned a dangling pointer
(reproducible SIGSEGV, hisab-filed). The vtable now lives in a
dedicated `_default_allocator_storage` global outside any resettable
region. **Consumer-held pointers are still the caller's problem.**
Reset only when nothing from the previous epoch will be read again.

For per-request memory you actually want reclaimed, use an ARENA
(`arena_new` / `arena_new_growable` / `arena_reset`) rather than
leaning on the global bump — and read
`arena_exhaustion_is_silent_because_a_zero_str_looks_valid` in this
file before you size one.

ORIGINAL (DOOM/mabda-era, kept for historical context — the WRITE
IDIOM at the bottom is still good advice, the diagnosis is not):

The Cyrius stdlib `alloc` is a bump allocator: each `alloc(N)`
moves a global pointer forward by N bytes; allocations are
NEVER freed during process lifetime. This is fine for short-
running programs (test suites, benchmarks, smoke programs) but
breaks down when a single process accumulates enough
allocations to exhaust the underlying heap segment.

**Symptom.** `alloc(N)` silently returns 0. Subsequent `store*`
to the returned pointer dereferences null → segfault. The
crash looks like an unrelated bug in test code that seemed to
work before.

**Where it bites.** Test files that grow over time and run all
their tests in one `main()` invocation. Each test that calls
`alloc(N)` permanently consumes that N. After enough tests,
the cumulative consumption exceeds the heap segment.

**Diagnostic.** If a test fails with exit 139 (SIGSEGV) and
inserting `assert(ctx != 0, "alloc returned non-null")`
catches it, the bump allocator is exhausted. mabda hit this
during Step 7.7 — adding the 9th `alloc(112)` ctx in
`tests/tcyr/mabda_v3_phase_d.tcyr` made `alloc` start
returning 0.

**The fix.** Stack-local arrays for test-scoped buffers:

```cyrius
# Before — heap-allocated, accumulates:
fn test_thing() {
    var ctx = alloc(112);
    memset(ctx, 0, 112);
    # ...
}

# After — stack-allocated, freed at fn return:
fn test_thing() {
    var ctx[112];
    memset(&ctx, 0, 112);
    # ...
}
```

The `var name[N]` form allocates on the stack, gets reclaimed
at fn exit. Identical observable behavior for test code; zero
heap pressure.

**When you MUST use alloc.** When a value needs to outlive the
fn (heap-allocated structs returned from constructors,
caller-owned buffers passed across abstraction boundaries).
Production code generally needs alloc; test code generally
doesn't.

**Why not fix the bump allocator.** The bump-allocator design
is intentional — its simplicity is the load-bearing property
for AGNOS's "no GC, no malloc" stance. Adding a heap-replacing
allocator would change the contract for every consumer.
Better to teach the test-write idiom: stack-local for
test-scoped buffers.

**Cross-pollination.** Every AGNOS test suite hits this
eventually. Teach the pattern at the start; don't wait for
the segfault. samvada's `tests/samvada.tcyr` already uses
stack-local idiom; mabda's older test files retrofitted as
they grew.

[[entries]]
name = "unwrap_is_option_only_result_silently_corrupts"
description = "unwrap()/unwrap_or() operate on Option (Some/None); on a Result they silently return the raw error code instead of aborting — use result_unwrap/is_ok for Results. Still true under the v6.6.0 VALUE FORM; only the spelling changed (payload() is DELETED, unwrap takes (t, v))"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Updated at v6.6.1 for the value form. The HAZARD IS UNCHANGED — only the
spelling is.** That is the point worth carrying: the flip re-aritied every helper
in this entry and did not close the hole, because the hole is the overlapping tag
values, not the representation.

`unwrap(t, v)` (lib/tagged.cyr) checks ONLY `is_none()` (tag == None == 0) before
returning `v`. A `Result` (lib/result.cyr) has Ok tag=0, Err tag=1 — tag values that
OVERLAP Option's None=0/Some=1. So calling `unwrap()` on a Result is a category
error with NO diagnostic:

- an **Err** (tag=1, != None) sails past the `is_none` check → `unwrap` hands back
  the payload = the RAW ERROR CODE as if it were a valid value (e.g. a negative
  fd fed straight into a syscall);
- an **Ok** (tag=0 == None) makes `unwrap` ABORT with "unwrap called on None".

Because the tags overlap, the mismatch never faults at the unwrap site — it
CORRUPTS DOWNSTREAM. This bit the async net client: `lib/net.cyr` fns
(`tcp_socket`, `sock_accept`, `sock_connect`, `sock_send/recv`) return a **Result**,
but the reactor glue reached for the familiar `unwrap()`.

**Correct spelling since v6.6.0** — `Result`, `Option` and `Either` are
`enum ...: stack`, so a payload variant returns `(tag, payload)` in a REGISTER PAIR
and construction allocates zero bytes. Bind both halves:

    var t, v = sock_send(fd, buf, n);
    if (is_err_result(t) == 1) { return err_code_of(t, v); }
    # v is the value

- ⛔ **`payload()` is DELETED (6.6.0) and has no 1-argument replacement** — rdx never
  reaches a parameter. Under the value form the payload is already a plain variable,
  so `payload(r)` becomes `r`. And so is `tag()` (6.6.2; a call is `undefined function
  'tag'`). `tagged_new()` was deleted at 6.6.0 and RESTORED at 6.6.2 in lib/boxed.cyr:
  read its box with `boxed_tag` / `boxed_payload`, never with the value-form helpers.
- Helpers that need the payload gained the tag: `unwrap(t, v)`,
  `unwrap_or(t, v, fallback)`, `option_print(t, v)`, `result_unwrap(t, v)`,
  `result_unwrap_or(t, v, fallback)`, `err_code_of(t, v)`, `result_print(t, v)`.
- Tag-only predicates keep **arity 1** — argument 1 receives rax, which for a pair
  IS the tag: `is_ok`, `is_err_result`, `is_none`, `is_some`, `is_left`, `is_right`.
  `is_tag(t, expected)` takes two.

⛔ **The migration rule has its own silent fail-open, and it cost 19 sites in yukti
alone.** `return res;` on an Err path returns the PAYLOAD by itself, so `Err(77)`
reads downstream as `tag=77, is_err=0` — **an error that reads as success.** Both
halves must be returned: `return (t, v);`. The compiler warns on a mixed-return
function since v6.6.0, but the warning is not a hard error, so read it.

Rule of thumb, unchanged across the flip: `unwrap` is Option-only; if a fn returns
`Result<T,E>`, never `unwrap` it.


[[entries]]
name = "sockaddr_ipv4_is_network_order_bytes_packed_as_int"
description = "async_connect/async_resolve pass IPv4 as the 4 network-order octets packed low-byte-first into an int (127.0.0.1 = 0x0100007F), NOT the host-order a.b.c.d value; resolve→connect round-trips untouched"
category = "cyrius"
---
The `addr` argument to `async_connect(rt, addr, port)` (and the nameserver addr to
`async_resolve`) is the four IPv4 octets in **network byte order, packed low-byte-
first into an integer** — e.g. 127.0.0.1 is `0x0100007F` (b0=127 in the LOW byte),
matching how it is stored raw with `store32(&sa+4, addr)` into `sockaddr_in`. The
`port` is HOST order and byte-swapped inline to network order at the store site.
`async_resolve` returns its resolved A record in the IDENTICAL packed form
(`_async_dns_parse_a` reassembles the 4 answer octets as
`b0 | (b1<<8) | (b2<<16) | (b3<<24)`), so a resolve result feeds STRAIGHT into
`async_connect` with no conversion. The trap: this int is NOT the human-readable
a.b.c.d and NOT host-order — passing `0x7F000001` (host-order 127.0.0.1), or
`htonl`-ing the resolve output before connect, silently connects to the wrong host
with no error. Same convention across net.cyr's sockaddr builders.

[[entries]]
name = "string_literal_to_str_coercion_is_annotation_only"
description = "A bare `\"...\"` coerces to a `Str` struct ONLY when the receiving param is annotated `: Str`. Into an UNTYPED param it arrives as a raw cstr pointer, and any str_* call then reads `load64(ptr+8)` as garbage — a silent empty/zero result, never an error. Shipped undetected for ~a year across 11 lib/fs.cyr fns; fixed v6.4.72."
category = "cyrius"
---
THE RULE: the compiler's string-literal → `Str` auto-coercion
(`parse_fn.cyr`) fires only when BOTH hold —

    1. the argument is a LITERAL token, and
    2. the receiving parameter is annotated `: Str`.

Miss either and you get the raw null-terminated `cstr` pointer.

THE SILENT FAILURE:

    fn find_files(path, ext) {          # UNTYPED params
        ...
        if (str_ends_with(name, ext)) { ... }
    }

    find_files("src", ".cyr");          # returns 0 matches. No error.

  `".cyr"` enters as a cstr pointer, flows unchanged through the
  untyped `ext`, and reaches `str_ends_with`, whose `suffix: Str` IS
  typed — but coercion happens at the CALL BOUNDARY, and by then the
  value is an i64, not a literal token. `str_len(suffix)` does
  `load64(ptr+8)` and reads whatever sits 8 bytes past the string data.
  Every comparison fails. You get an empty result set, a zero count, a
  never-taken branch. **Never a diagnostic.**

WHY THE OBVIOUS TESTS PASS ANYWAY:

    str_ends_with(name, ".cyr")         # WORKS  — literal + `: Str` param
    find_files(path, str_from(".cyr"))  # WORKS  — real Str value
    find_files(path, ".cyr")            # BROKEN — literal + untyped param

  So a direct probe of the leaf function works, and any caller that
  already wrapped in `str_from` works. Only the bare-literal call into
  the untyped API is wrong, which is why it survived ~a year in
  `lib/fs.cyr` (`find_files`, `find_files_with_prunes`, `path_has_ext`
  and 8 siblings) and was found only when `cyrius coverage` reported
  `0/1097` and someone chased it. The working hypothesis at the time
  was allocator aliasing — it was not.

THE FIX IS AT THE API BOUNDARY, NOT THE CALL SITE:

    fn find_files(path: Str, ext: Str) { ... }   # annotate — done

  An `IDENT` argument is never coerced, so `str_from`-wrapped callers
  are byte-identical after the annotation. Annotating is free.

THIS IS NOT A COMPILER BUG. Literal-only coercion is deliberate: a
runtime `i64` cannot be known to be a `Str`. The library simply failed
to express its contract. The lesson is a review rule:

  **Any public fn that takes a string MUST annotate the param `: Str`.**
  An unannotated string param is an API defect, and it fails silently
  rather than loudly — which is the worst possible failure mode for a
  stdlib. Grep your own modules for `fn <name>(...)` with a
  string-shaped parameter and no `: Str`.

CROSS-REF: CHANGELOG [6.4.72]; the same release fixed `cyrius coverage`,
which had been working AROUND this bug with an inline `dir_walk` +
`str_ends_with` filter.

[[entries]]
name = "str_int_cstr_are_reserved_fn_name_suffixes_that_rewrite_your_call_sites"
description = "**`_str`, `_int` and `_cstr` are RESERVED FUNCTION-NAME SUFFIXES in cyrius, not free-form naming.** If `X` and `X_str` both exist, the compiler REWRITES a call `X(a, ...)` into `X_str(a, ...)` whenever argument 1 is `Str`-typed — likewise `X_int` for an i64-returning call argument. **What \"Str-typed\" means, since 6.6.16:** the WHOLE first argument is a Str — a Str local, param or closure capture, a `: Str` or inferred-Str global (`var g = str_from(..)`), a `: Str` field one step past a name or a call (`h.name`, `mkh().name`), or a call or method declared `: Str` (`mk()`, `g<T>(..)`, `s.cat(t)`, `h.getname()`, `mk().clone()`). A call or method result is typed by its OWN declared return, never by its receiver: `s.len()` and `s.data()` are not Str. Two steps (`a.b.name`, `h.name.clone()`) and a generic whose return is `T` (`id<Str>(s)`) are not typed. Before 6.6.16 only a Str LOCAL or param routed, so `println(gs)` on a Str global printed the header's pointer bytes and `strlen(gs)` answered 3; and `x.data`, `x.len`, `x.len()` and `x + 8` on a Str local were routed by the base's type into `X_str`, which read the value as a header (memory-unsafe). An untyped local, param or capture named like a Str global is NOT a Str. The I11 warning (\"passing Str-typed X … which expects a cstring\") uses the same classifier, so a call that can be routed is never warned about. Registration is bidirectional at `REGFN`, so include order does not save you. The mechanism exists for genuine type variants (`println(cstr)` / `println_str(Str)`) and it is invisible until it misfires. **THE RULE: `X_str` must be the Str-taking variant of `X` — a cstr+len form may NEVER occupy that name.** Violating it means calls to `X` silently execute `X_str`. This is not hypothetical: bayan named its cstr+len entries `bayan_json_v_parse_str(buf, len)` alongside `bayan_json_v_parse(src)`, so every `bayan_json_v_parse(someStr)` in the ecosystem became a **1-argument call to a 2-argument function** — `len` bound to garbage, parse returned 0 for every input including valid documents — and the only outward sign was one `expects 2 arguments, got 1` warning line lost in build output. It cost bayan a BREAKING public-API rename (1.3.0, `_str` -> `_buf`) to escape, and sakshi carried the same collision latently (`_sk_write_str/5` shadowing the unrelated `_sk_write/2`). **As of cyrius 6.5.1 the compiler is arity-aware** — it only redirects when the target's parameter count matches the arguments you actually supplied — and a wrong argument count is now a hard ERROR that withholds the binary rather than a warning. **`_int` IS DIFFERENT FROM `_str`, and as of 6.5.2 it has an explicit condition.** The `_int` route only ever fired for a bare FN-CALL first argument — never a literal, never a variable — so `take(v)` and `take(42)` called the base while `take(mk())` ran `take_int`: the same value dispatching differently depending on whether it had passed through a variable. From 6.5.2 the route requires the base's parameter 0 to be explicitly annotated **`: cstring`**, which is the condition it was always approximating (\"this param wants a POINTER, so convert the number for it\"). Practical rule: if you want `X`/`X_int` auto-dispatch, annotate X's first parameter `: cstring`; if you do NOT want it, leave it unannotated and the pair is inert. **Also 6.5.2: passing an integer LITERAL to a `: cstring` parameter is now a hard error** — it used to compile silently and SIGSEGV, since the callee dereferences the number as a `char*` (`println(42)` printed nothing and died on signal 11). `0` remains legal as NULL. An i64 VARIABLE or a fn-call result passed to a `: cstring` param is still only a warning, and always will be: under ADR-002's i64-everywhere tenet those are genuinely indistinguishable from a pointer. A literal is the one case the compiler can actually judge. So the trap is defused on 6.5.1+, but the NAMING rule still stands: pick `_buf` (or anything that is not `_str` / `_int` / `_cstr`) for a pointer+length form, because a suffix that lies about a function's type is confusing even when it is no longer dangerous. See CHANGELOG [6.5.1], bayan CHANGELOG [1.3.0]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "async_await_readable_ms_bounded_wait"
description = "`async_await_readable(fd)` parks on epoll with a hardcoded -1 and returns a constant 0 — a cooperative server parked there cannot be woken to STOP. Use `async_await_readable_ms(fd, ms)` (v6.5.6): 1 = readable, 0 = timed out."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.6** — filed by sandhi/agnosai while building a graceful-shutdown facility. `async_await_readable(fd)` builds its own epfd, registers `fd`, and calls `sys_epoll_wait(epfd, &revents, 1, 0 - 1)`. Two separate problems live in that one line:

1. **The `-1` is unbounded.** A caller parked there is unwakeable by anything except data on `fd` — no flag, no deadline, no cancellation. Fine for a server whose only job is to serve; fatal for one that must also **stop**. An idle accept loop never observes a shutdown flag set by another thread, because control never leaves the call. A signal handler can fire perfectly and change nothing.
2. **The return value was discarded.** The helper returned a constant `0` regardless of outcome, so even *with* a timeout a caller could not distinguish "the fd is readable" from "the wait expired". A bounded wait that cannot report which happened is not usable.

`async_await_readable_ms(fd, ms)` fixes both: **1** = readable, **0** = timed out, negative `ms` blocks forever. `async_await_readable` remains as a wrapper returning `0`, so no existing call site moves.

WHAT DOESN'T COVER THIS. `async_with_timeout(rt, handle, ms)` looks like the answer and is not — it races a *spawned task* against a deadline sentinel inside a runtime, whereas this is a bare fd-readiness wait taken on the accept path, outside any task, before a runtime is meaningfully in play.

EINTR. A signal-interrupted wait reports `0`, the same as a timeout. That is deliberate: the caller's loop re-checks its own condition and waits again, which is the correct response to both.

SCOPE. `lib/async.cyr`'s epoll body is gated NOT-agnos AND NOT-win, so this pair is Linux-path only by construction (Windows has `lib/async_win.cyr`, agnos `lib/async_agnos.cyr`). On the **cx** target no syscall peer is included at all, so the body does not even compile there — no cross-target work is owed.

THE TESTING LESSON. Both directions need pinning. A variant hardwired to `1` passes an "it returned instead of hanging" test; a variant hardwired to `0` — the *old* contract — passes that one too. `tests/async_await_readable_ms.sh` asserts an idle fd reports 0 **and** a readable fd reports 1; each mutation fails exactly one axis.

[[entries]]
name = "i64_min_negation_is_a_noop_and_division_truncates_toward_zero"
description = "`n = 0 - n` is a NO-OP at i64::MIN (two's-complement abs overflows back to itself), so `n == 0` and `n > 0` are BOTH false and any `while (n > 0)` digit loop runs zero times. That is why every decimal formatter in the stdlib emitted a bare `-` for i64::MIN until v6.5.8. The fix is a sign FLAG + a `!= 0` loop + per-digit negation — which is safe because `/` and `%` truncate toward zero on x86, aarch64 AND cx."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.8** — 12 sites, not the 7 the roadmap named.

THE ARITHMETIC (all four probed by running the compiler at v6.5.10):

    var m  = 1 << 63;        # i64::MIN
    0 - m  == m              # TRUE  — negation is a NO-OP here
    m > 0                    # FALSE — so a `while (n > 0)` loop never runs
    (0 - 7) / 2 == 0 - 3     # TRUE  — truncates TOWARD ZERO, not floor
    (0 - 7) % 2 == 0 - 1     # TRUE  — remainder takes the DIVIDEND's sign

i64 is two's complement, so the magnitude of `i64::MIN` is one larger
than `i64::MAX` and has no positive representation. `0 - n` overflows
straight back to `n`. There is no error, no trap, no diagnostic.

THE SHAPE THAT BREAKS. Every hand-rolled decimal formatter is written
this way, in every language:

    if (n < 0) { write('-'); n = 0 - n; }    # ← NO-OP at i64::MIN
    if (n == 0) { write('0'); }              # ← skipped, n is not 0
    while (n > 0) { ... }                    # ← skipped, n is not > 0

At `i64::MIN` the sign byte is written and then NOTHING. `fmt_int`
printed `-`. `str_from_int(i64::MIN)` was the one-character string
`"-"`, and bayan then serialised `{"n":-}` — not JSON — with no error
raised at any layer.

THE FIX — keep the sign as a FLAG, never negate `n`:

    var neg: i64 = 0;
    if (n < 0) { neg = 1; }
    if (n == 0) { store8(&buf + bi, 48); bi = bi - 1; }
    while (n != 0) {                     # `!= 0`, NOT `> 0`
        var d = n % 10;
        if (d < 0) { d = 0 - d; }        # negate the DIGIT, not n
        store8(&buf + bi, 48 + d); bi = bi - 1; n = n / 10;
    }
    if (neg == 1) { store8(&buf + bi, 45); bi = bi - 1; }

`!= 0` is the load-bearing half. Extracting digits from a negative
value only works because `/` and `%` truncate toward zero on **all
three** cyrius backends — that was verified on x86_64, aarch64 and cx
before the fix relied on it, not assumed. (Under floor division
`(0-7)/2` would be `-4` and the digits would come out wrong.)

WHERE IT WAS. Four in `lib/fmt.cyr` (`fmt_int`, `fmt_int_fd`,
`fmt_int_buf`, and a sibling), plus `lib/string.cyr`, `lib/log.cyr`,
sakshi (fixed upstream at 2.4.8 and re-vendored), **two inside the
compiler itself** (`PRNUM`, `_emit_decimal`) that no roadmap entry
listed, and three shipped demos.

⭐ **THE LESSON IS THE HALF-FIX.** `fmt_hex`, sitting directly below
`fmt_int` in the same file, had already been corrected for this exact
`> 0` vs `!= 0` reason at **v6.4.69**. The decimal siblings were never
brought along. When you fix a loop-bound predicate in one formatter,
grep the file for every other `while (n > 0)` before you close it.

REVIEW RULE for your own code: any `while (x > 0)` over an i64 that
came from user data is suspect, and any `x = 0 - x` "take the absolute
value" is a bug at exactly one input. Test formatters and parsers at
`1 << 63`, not just at `-1`.

[[entries]]
name = "arena_exhaustion_is_silent_because_a_zero_str_looks_valid"
description = "An exhausted arena returns 0 from `arena_alloc` / `str_from_a` — correct behaviour — but a `Str` of 0 is INDISTINGUISHABLE from a valid `Str`, there is no option type and no error channel through the `_a` families, so it flows on and faults layers away. v6.5.9 adds a per-arena exhaustion POLICY (ARENA_FULL_NULL / GROW / SPILL / ABORT) precisely because returning 0 correctly was never enough."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.9** — filed from agnosai.

THE PRIMITIVES WERE ALREADY RIGHT, AND THAT WAS THE PROBLEM.
`arena_alloc` returns 0 when the chunk cannot serve the request,
`str_from_a` returns 0, `vec_push_a` returns -1. None of that is the
bug. The bug is what the 0 does next: **a `Str` of 0 is
indistinguishable from a valid `Str`** — there is no option type, and
the `_a` (allocator-threaded) families have no error channel — so it
flows onward and the first thing that dereferences it faults. "Arena
too small" was observable as a SIGSEGV several layers away, in a JSON
walker, with no indication which allocator ran out.

⭐ **THE REGRESSION DIRECTION IS WHAT MAKES IT SERIOUS.** Before
threading a route onto an arena, an oversized response was a *leak* on
the no-free global bump. After threading — a change whose entire
purpose is to stop leaking — the same response is a *crash*. Adopting
the feature correctly made the failure worse and quieter. A
5,500-assertion suite missed it because allocation tests are
small-fixture by nature: they assert `bytes == 0` over a handful of
items, and nothing about that shape probes the ceiling.

THE POLICY (one field on the arena header, set at creation or later):

    ARENA_FULL_NULL  = 0    return 0 — TODAY'S BEHAVIOUR, still the
                            default, so no existing arena moves
    ARENA_FULL_GROW  = 1    chain another chunk
    ARENA_FULL_SPILL = 2    serve overflow from the global allocator
                            (weaker — spilled bytes are never reclaimed
                            by reset — but the crash becomes the
                            pre-arena cost)
    ARENA_FULL_ABORT = 3    die loudly AT the allocation instead of
                            faulting three layers away

    var a  = arena_new_growable(65536);        # policy = GROW
    var al = arena_allocator_growable(65536);  # the allocator-interface form
    arena_set_on_full(a, ARENA_FULL_ABORT);    # or change it later
    arena_on_full(a)                           # read it back, -1 if a == 0

⚠ **GROW RETAINS ITS CHUNKS ACROSS `arena_reset`.** The bump allocator
underneath has no `free()`, so releasing them is not expressible — and
it is not what an arena wants anyway. Reset rewinds to the FIRST chunk
and re-uses the chain, so a request loop converges on its high-water
mark and then allocates nothing. Use `arena_capacity_total(a)` for the
whole chain; `arena_used(a)` cannot show it once the arena has grown.

⚠ `arena_free(a)` used to clear only base/ptr/end and LEAVE THE POLICY
SET, so a "freed" growable arena would take the grow path and succeed.
Fixed in the same release — but if you are on an older toolchain, do
not reuse a freed arena handle.

PRACTICAL RULE: if you cannot bound the allocation from the input,
either use `arena_new_growable` and stop guessing a capacity, or set
`ARENA_FULL_ABORT` so a wrong guess is a diagnosable death rather than
a mystery segfault in whatever code touched the pointer next. Sizing an
arena and hoping is the shape this entry exists to stop.

[[entries]]
name = "thread_is_done_is_valid_only_before_thread_join"
description = "`thread_is_done(t)` (v6.5.8) is a non-blocking poll of the handle `thread_create` returned — valid ONLY BEFORE `thread_join`, because join CONSUMES the handle (on Windows it CloseHandle()s it) and a post-join query reports \"not done\" forever. A detached thread has no handle at all, by design."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.8** — the reaper primitives.

    var t = thread_create(&worker, arg);
    while (thread_is_done(t) == 0) { ... keep serving ... }
    thread_join(t);                     # then join once — and STOP asking

`thread_join` blocks unconditionally, which made a reaper — "collect
whichever workers have finished, keep serving the rest" — impossible to
write without a detached thread plus a side channel. `thread_is_done`
reads the same `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` slot `thread_join`'s loop already
tests, so it adds no new state: **1** = done, **0** = still running,
**-1** = null handle.

⛔ **VALID ONLY BEFORE `thread_join`.** Join CONSUMES the handle — on
Windows it `CloseHandle()`s it — so a post-join query inspects a closed
handle and reports "not done", forever. That is not a bug to work
around; after joining, the thread is finished by definition and there is
nothing left to ask. Caught on real cass hardware, by a test that
asserted the wrong contract.

⛔ **A DETACHED THREAD HAS NO HANDLE.** `thread_create_detached(fp,
arg)` returns 1/0 (spawned or not), not a handle, and there is nothing
to poll or join — the child unmaps its own stack in the trampoline tail
and the TLS block is carved from the top of that same mapping so one
`munmap` frees both. Use it for fire-and-forget work; use
`thread_create` + `thread_is_done` + `thread_join` when you need to know
it finished. (Before .8 the only fire-and-forget shape was an unjoined
`thread_create`, which leaked **2,101,248 bytes of VA per thread** —
100 unjoined threads = 210,124,800 B, now 0.)

⚠ RELATED, SAME RELEASE: `thread_join` itself had a lost-wakeup
deadlock — it read the tid word TWICE per iteration, once for the loop
condition and once as the `FUTEX_WAIT` expected value, so a worker
exiting between the two loads let the kernel's one-shot wake fire with
nobody parked and the joiner then parked on an address that would never
be woken again. Permanent, silent, log-less, ~1 join in 150k, SIGKILL
the only recovery. Fixed in v6.5.8. If you are on ≤6.5.7 and a
long-running server wedges in a join, this is it.

[[entries]]
name = "vector_value_return_binding_forms"
description = "A value-form vector return (f32v4/f64v2/f64v4) used to survive only `var r: <exact type> = f(...)`; every other binding form silently read back zero"
category = "cyrius"
---
Until v6.5.61, a function returning a SIMD vector by value was stored correctly by
exactly ONE spelling. The others compiled clean, exited 0, and produced wrong numbers.

    var r: f64v2 = f64v2_add(a, b);    # worked
    var r        = f64v2_add(a, b);    # lanes read back 0   (no annotation)
    var r: f64v2;  r = f64v2_add(a,b); # lanes read back 0   (assignment form)
    var r: f64v4 = f64v2_add(a, b);    # lanes read back 0   (mismatched type)

Every width was affected — f32v4 returned garbage, f64v2 and f64v4 returned 0 — and
it was never confined to lib/simd.cyr: a user-defined `fn mk(): f64v2` had the same
problem. The most visible symptom was `f64v2_scale(a, f64_from(3))` coming back as
`lo=0, hi=3`, with the SCALE FACTOR sitting in lane 1.

WHY. The 16/32-byte receive paths in PARSE_VAR were gated on the variable's own type
annotation, so a variable without one fell through to the ordinary scalar
initialiser. The assignment path had no vector receive at all and used the generic
8-byte store. Since v6.5.61 the type is inferred from the callee's recorded return
type when the variable declares none, the assignment form has its own receive, and an
annotation that DISAGREES with the callee is a hard error rather than a silent
truncation:

    error: vector return type does not match the declared type
    error: vector return type does not match the assigned variable

WHAT TO TAKE FROM IT. If you are writing SIMD code on a cyrius older than 6.5.61,
annotate every vector binding with its exact type and never use the assignment form.
On 6.5.61+ all forms work, and a wrong annotation is refused instead of silently
dropping lanes.

The reason this survived for so long is worth more than the bug: every call site in
the tests, the guide and the stdlib spelled the annotation, so the API's most natural
spelling was exercised by nothing. The differential corpus was 406/407 byte-identical
across the fix — which measures the corpus, not the fix. When you add a receive path
for a type, test the binding forms a CONSUMER writes, not the one the stdlib writes;
and note that a declaration receive and an assignment receive are two halves of one
statement, not two features (v6.5.57 fixed the same split for structs).

6.7.11 — A VALUE-FORM VECTOR LOCAL TAKES A VECTOR, OR NOTHING. `var v: f64v2 = 0;` inside a fn is refused (it zeroed
one lane and left the other stale) — declare it bare; a vector GLOBAL still takes `= 0`. `callptr(&f, ..)`,
`fncallN(&f, ..)` and `(f(..))` receive whole (the old "no working spelling for a vector-returning callptr" is
history); a callee in a variable, a word (`load64(..)`, `= 5`), `&w`, a method call and `*p` are refused by name.
A `p: *f64v2` PARAMETER is a pointer since 6.7.11 (call_arguments_and_fn_pointer_results_since_6_7_11).
On Windows a vector-returning call nothing receives — a statement, an argument to an untyped parameter, a method, an
operator fn — no longer faults (6.7.12, FX-2): it gets its hidden result pointer like a receive; its value, where one
is read, is lane 0.
6.7.13 — MORE SOURCES BIND (D29, D34, VC-3, VC-4, VC-10): a METHOD declared `: V` (`var v: f64v2 = s.mv(..);`), an
EXPLICIT INSTANCE (`= mkg<i64>(..)`) and a DEREFERENCE `*p` of a `*V` (`var v: f64v2 = *p;` / `v = *p;` copy the 16 /
32 bytes) now bind into a vector local, and an UNANNOTATED `var v = callptr(&f, ..)` / `fncallN(&f, ..)` /
`mkg<i64>(..)` is the vector — it was one word, so code that used it as a word now reads a vector (its lane 0 as a
value). An unannotated `var v = s.mv();` and `var v = *p;` still bound ONE WORD silently through 6.7.13; since
6.7.14 they are the vector too (6.7.14's D36 / D37). A missing fn in a vector initialiser is reported as missing
(`undefined function 'f64v2_splat'` — add `include "lib/simd.cyr"`), not as a refused source (VC-12; at every vector
position since 6.7.14). Through 6.7.14 three more stayed one word, silently — a WRAPPED `var x = (mk2(..));`, a
TOP-LEVEL `var w = mkv(..);` and a chained receiver `var v = w.s.mv();`; since 6.7.15 the wrapped and the chained one
are the vector and the top-level one is refused (6.7.15's D28 / D26; VC-4, VC-7, VC-9). vector_values_since_6_7_14,
vector_values_since_6_7_15.

[[entries]]
name = "public_marker_leaks_onto_next_declaration_v664"
description = "A `public` on a struct/union/enum/impl/use in a private file re-exposed the NEXT fn or var (v6.5.0–6.6.3); the marker is now armed only for items that can carry it (v6.6.4)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYMPTOM. A `private` file declares `public enum AdPowLimit { … }` and, right after
it, `fn _ad_pow()`. From another file `_ad_pow()` compiles and runs; every other
`_`-named item in the file is refused. hisab's public-surface gate found exactly ONE
leak among 457 probes (456 refused), and the one was the declaration after the enum.

MECHANISM. `public` set a running marker (`_pub_pending`) that was consumed by the
next fn or global-var DEFINITION — and by nothing else. An enum records no visibility
(enum constants are public by design), so it never touched the marker; the marker
outlived the enum and the next fn or var was stamped public. The same for a bare
`public struct` / `union` / `impl` / `use`, and for `public var a[N]` (the array path
never called the stamp helper). The fix is a POSITIVE list: `public` arms the marker
only when the next token is `var`/`secret`, `fn`/`async`, or a fn-attribute directive;
otherwise it is consumed on the spot.

THE SECOND LAYER. The compiler makes two passes over top-level tokens. Pass 2 skips
`var` inline without consuming the marker and stamps only what pass 1 could not — impl
methods and fns after top-level code — so `public var V = 7;` followed by an impl block
re-exposed the impl's FIRST method even with pass 1 correct. Now PARSE_IMPL and the
relaxed-ordering fn path clear a stale marker on entry. Consequence: `public impl`
marks NO method; write `public fn` per method.

THREE THINGS THE FIX EXPOSED. (1) `public var a, b = f();` stamped `b` private (the
helper cleared the marker after `a`) — one `public` now covers every name. (2) Derived
codecs (`T_to_json`, `T_from_json`, `T_from_json_str`, enum codecs) were emitted
without the type's `public`; only the v6.6.3 accessors carried it, so closing the leak
would have turned "one codec reachable by accident" into "none" — they inherit it
now. (3) The once-per-translation-unit `_cy_enum_name_eq` helper was private to
whichever file derived an enum first; a second private file's enum codec was refused.

THE PATTERN. A "next item" marker that only some declaration kinds consume is an
armed trap; every kind that does NOT consume it is a leak site. Enumerate the kinds
(struct, union, enum, impl, use, mod, array var, destructure var, the pass-2 skip)
and gate each by name — the filing's matrix had tested struct only through
`#derive(accessors)`, whose generated `public fn` happened to consume the marker,
so the struct row read green for the wrong reason.

[[entries]]
name = "private_applies_to_the_whole_file_and_forward_references_are_checked"
description = "`private` scopes to the FILE it appears in, wherever in that file the marker sits — including definitions written above it, and including globals — and a reference that comes earlier in the concatenated stream than the definition it names is enforced exactly like one that comes after. Before 6.6.5 both halves were false, and the consequences ran in BOTH directions: reachable private methods on one side, refused public ones on the other."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["language", "visibility", "private", "include-order", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
Two rules, both true since 6.6.5 and neither true before it:

**1. The marker's POSITION in the file does not matter.** `private`
alone on a line flips the whole file. A fn declared ABOVE that line is
file-private like any other, and so is a global. Until 6.6.5 the flip
was recorded as the compiler's first pass walked past the marker, so
anything declared earlier was stamped as if the file were public — and
a global above the line was never private in EITHER include order.

**2. A FORWARD reference is checked.** If the call is parsed before the
definition — the library included BELOW the code that uses it — the
boundary still applies: `q.m()` and `Q_m(&q)` on an impl method,
`a + b` through an operator `impl`, a `mod`-scoped fn, a fn defined
after the first top-level statement. Until 6.6.5 every one of those
compiled and RAN (exit 42 on the filed repro).

⚠ **THE MIRROR IMAGE IS THE PART WORTH KNOWING, because it bit code
that did nothing unusual.** The same root made the compiler keep one
symbol-table entry per NAME during that first pass, so:

* Two files that each declare `private` and each define a helper of the
  same name got a FALSE `'_h' is private to its file` — and a false
  `'_h' expects 2 arguments, got 1` when the arities differed.
* A public fn could inherit a private stamp from an unrelated file's
  same-named private one. The language guide's own two-file `_helper`
  example was REFUSED when the public file was included first.
* A forward call passing a plain struct larger than 8 bytes SIGSEGV'd —
  silently, with no diagnostic, on Linux, macOS/aarch64 and Windows.

**If you are on a toolchain before 6.6.5, the workaround for the
fail-open is the normal include order (library first) — but note that
the SAME order is what triggers the false-refusal half, so there is no
ordering that avoids both. Pin 6.6.5 or later.**

There is also no per-item `private`: `private fn h()` is a hard error
naming the file-level form (v6.5.56). ⚠ Until 6.6.5 that error printed
TWICE and privatised the file anyway, so a legitimate sibling fn in the
same file was then reported `is private to its file` at ITS caller —
the diagnostic pointed at correct code.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_struct_declared_inside_a_fn_is_per_call_and_per_thread"
description = "Until v6.6.5 a struct LITERAL written inside a fn (`var p = T { .. }`) was compiled as a GLOBAL: shared across recursion, shared across threads, and visible to every later reference of that name in ANY file of the program. From v6.6.5 it is a per-call frame object like `var p: T;` has always been. The rules a consumer needs: a struct declared inside a fn is per call and per thread; a struct declared at top level is one shared object; and `var p: T = <expr>` where the expression yields an ADDRESS is a POINTER to someone else's struct, not a copy."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["structs", "storage", "locals", "recursion", "threads", "scoping", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**What changed, in consumer terms.** These three now mean what they look like:

```cyrius
fn f(n) {
    var a = P { n, n * 2 };   # per call, per thread (was a GLOBAL before v6.6.5)
    var b: P;                 # per call, per thread (always was)
    var c: P = mk();          # a POINTER to whatever mk() returned — NOT a copy
    return a.x + b.y;
}
```

**Symptoms you would have seen before v6.6.5**, all silent: a recursive fn building a literal
returned the innermost call's value; N threads running the same fn scribbled over one another;
and a `var cfg = P { 1, 2 };` inside any fn of any file captured a top-level `var cfg = 5;`
elsewhere in the program. The last one needed no `private`, no second file and no collision the
author could see — just the same identifier twice.

**Two behaviours that follow, and are now consistent.** Operator overloading dispatches for a
struct VALUE wherever it lives — literal local, typed local, annotated local, global — and does
NOT dispatch for a pointer-mode local, where `+` is pointer arithmetic. And a closure that
references an enclosing struct or array captures it BY VALUE, including a stack array through
`&buf`, which previously failed to compile.

**If you relied on the old behaviour** — a literal that persisted across calls — declare it at
top level. That is what it actually was.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "an_over_budget_array_local_is_static_storage_and_says_so"
description = "`var buf[N]` inside a fn is per-thread STACK storage, but past the per-fn frame budget (~120 KB of slots) it falls back to ONE shared static buffer. As of v6.6.5 the compiler says so with a `warning:` rather than a `note:`, and the name is scoped to its fn so it can no longer collide with a global elsewhere in the program. `CYRIUS_STACK_ARRAYS=0` puts EVERY array local on that path."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["arrays", "storage", "threads", "secret", "diagnostics", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
```
warning: array local over the per-fn frame budget gets STATIC storage: one buffer shared by
all calls and all threads; use alloc() for per-call storage
```

**Read it as a concurrency warning, not a size warning.** The buffer works; it is simply not
per call and not per thread. If the fn can be re-entered or run on two threads, move the
buffer to `alloc()`. Two known consumers (a 256 KB crypto scratch and a test server buffer)
bank theirs deliberately and are correct as they stand.

**The budget is per FN, so it can meet two buffers (6.7.12).** Two 64 KB array locals in ONE fn pass the frame budget
(15,360 slots): the first is stack storage and the SECOND silently becomes the static, shared-by-every-thread buffer
(the warning names it). Give each large buffer its own fn — `lib/fs_win.cyr`'s `_fs_handle_under_path` was split out
for exactly this (WN-3) — or `alloc()` it.

**Before v6.6.5** the same declaration ALSO took a program-wide global name, so it captured
every later reference to that identifier in any file (measured: 7 where 3 was right) and, from
v6.6.4, produced a visibility error naming the CONSUMER's variable. It was invisible to the
local table too, so an enclosing local of the same name won inside its own fn and `&buf` wrote
into that scalar. And `secret var buf[N]` over the budget was refused outright — or, worse,
compiled and never zeroised. All four are closed.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_top_level_block_scopes_its_vars_since_6_6_6"
description = "A `var` declared inside a top-level block (an `if`/`while`/`for`/`switch` body outside every fn) belongs to that block and is gone at its `}` — the same rule as a fn body, since 6.6.6. Before that it registered a GLOBAL: the name stayed visible after the block AND an inner declaration of an outer name overwrote the outer global (measured 2 where 1 is correct). Reading one after its block is now an error that names the variable."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["scope", "globals", "top-level", "semantics", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The rule.** These two are now the same language:

    fn f(): i64 { var c = 1; if (c == 1) { var t = 5; } return t; }   # undefined variable 't'
    var c = 1; var x = 0; x = 1;
    if (c == 1) { var t = 5; }
    syscall(60, t);                                                   # undefined variable 't'

Before 6.6.6 the second one compiled and exited 5. A top-level `var` inside a block went
through `PARSE_VAR`'s `GINFN == 0` arm and registered a global, and the global var table had
no scope mechanism at all — `SCOPE_POP` cleared only the frame-local table.

**The part that was worse than visibility.** With 6.6.6's one-global-per-name fold, an inner
declaration of an OUTER name did not shadow it, it *was* it:

    var limit = 1;
    var go = 0; go = 1;
    if (go == 1) { var limit = 2; }
    syscall(60, limit);       # 2 on x86 before 6.6.6; 1 now

cx and aarch64 already answered 1, so the self-host fixpoint and both cross-target legs were
green over an x86-only wrong answer. When two targets disagree about a *frontend* rule, the
frontend is where to look.

**Writing code that needs the value after the block.** Declare above, assign inside —
assignment to an outer global from inside a block is unchanged, only DECLARATIONS are scoped:

    var t = 0;
    if (go == 1) { t = 5; }
    syscall(60, t);           # 5

**Recognising it.** The error names the variable and says where to put it:

    note: 't' was declared inside a top-level block and goes out of scope at its '}'
          (since 6.6.6 a top-level block scopes its `var`s like a fn body does)
          declare it at top level, before the block, to use it after the block

A survey of 12,604 `.cyr` sources across the ecosystem when the change landed found no file
that relied on the leak, so nothing downstream had to change — but code written against
pre-6.6.6 cyrius that does rely on it fails loudly rather than quietly.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "println_dispatches_on_the_declared_return_type"
description = "A helper declared `: i64` that returns a string pointer gets println() rerouted to the NUMERIC printer — silently"
category = "cyrius"
---
`println` is an overload-dispatch target (lib/string.cyr, v5.10.5): PARSE_FNCALL routes
`println(x)` to `println_int` whenever the argument is i64-TYPED — an `: i64`-annotated
local or global, or the result of an `: i64`-annotated fn call. The dispatch reads the
DECLARATION, not what the value actually points at.

So a perfectly ordinary helper:

    fn _suite_row(idx, run): i64 {        # every helper in this codebase is `: i64`
        if (idx == 0) { return "selfhost"; }
        ...
    }
    println(_suite_row(i, 0));            # prints 6551721, not "selfhost"

prints nine pool ADDRESSES where you wanted nine names. No diagnostic, exit 0 — the
program is type-correct as far as the compiler is concerned, because `: i64` is exactly
what you told it. Measured 6.6.6 (bite 27a) on the check driver's `--list-suites`.

WHAT TO DO. Either annotate the helper honestly (`: cstring`), or print through a
cstring-only primitive rather than the overloaded name — the check driver already has
`_p(cstr)` for exactly this and the fix was `_p(name); _p("\n");`.

⚠ THE SHAPE, not the symbol: any overload-dispatched stdlib name behaves this way, and
`: i64` is this codebase's default annotation for *everything* (ADR-002), so the two
collide constantly. When a stdlib call prints or formats the wrong KIND of thing, suspect
the declared return type of whatever produced the argument before you suspect the callee.

ONE MORE, IN cyrius's OWN CLI (6.7.12, TL-14): `println(vec_get(v, i))` prints the POINTER — `vec_get` returns i64, so
the dispatch picks `println_int`; `cyrius lib sync --dry-run` printed `would sync: 140207630426008` for each file
through 6.7.11. The rule above covers it: print a cstring held in a vec through a cstring-only primitive (or a
`: cstring`-typed local).

[[entries]]
name = "float_negation_is_a_sign_flip_since_6_6_8"
description = "Before cycc 6.6.8, `-1.0` evaluated to -4.0 and `-x` on an f64 negated the BIT PATTERN; f64_to disagreed across targets"
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc < 6.6.8 compiled unary minus on a float LITERAL or an f64-typed operand as INTEGER
negation of its bit pattern: `-1.0` is `0 - 0x3FF0000000000000`, which reads back as
-4.0, and `-0.0` came out +0. x86 `f64_neg(+0)` also gave +0. No warning. Consumers carried
workaround rules (dhvani, ganita) or, like ranga, wrote `-4.0` meaning `-1.0`.

SINCE 6.6.8: unary minus on a float literal folds to `bits ^ (1 << 63)`, on an F64-typed
operand it emits a sign flip, and x86 `f64_neg` is `btc rax, 63`. `f64_to` agrees on every
target: NaN -> 0, out-of-range saturates (x86 used to return 0x8000000000000000).

STILL INTEGER, BY DESIGN (ADR-002 — values are i64 unless typed): unary minus on an
UNTYPED variable that happens to hold float bits. Type it (`var x: f64`) or call
`f64_neg`. A kind-2 warning now flags `int_expr OP f64_expr` mixes, which is how ranga's
`-4.0` was found. ⚠ Open at 6.6.8: f64 struct fields and f64 builtin RESULTS are not yet
F64-typed, so arithmetic and unary minus on them are still integer (6.6.8 review finds).

[[entries]]
name = "exp_ln_family_one_ulp_contract_since_6_6_8"
description = "f64_exp/exp2/ln/log2 are <= 1 ulp with IEEE specials on every target since cycc 6.6.8 — not bit-identical across targets"
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc < 6.6.8's aarch64 polyfills (no x87 there) returned finite values for NaN / negative /
zero inputs to f64_ln and drifted by many ulp (tyche's golden stream caught it); PE's x87
f64_exp ran at 53-bit precision control. SINCE 6.6.8: fdlibm ports on aarch64 and cx, range
guards BEFORE x*log2e, every negative input -> NaN, +0 -> -inf, and PE saves/sets/restores
the x87 control word around exp/exp2. The PROMISE is <= 1 ulp with IEEE specials on every
target — NOT bit-identical results across targets; compare with a ulp bar, never `==`.
sin/cos/atan get the same treatment in 6.6.9.

[[entries]]
name = "trig_one_ulp_and_x86_off_fsin_since_6_6_9"
description = "f64_sin/cos/atan are <= 1 ulp with IEEE specials on every target since cycc 6.6.9; x86 no longer uses bare x87 fsin/fcos"
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc < 6.6.9: the aarch64/cx polyfills carried a 1e-9 ABSOLUTE tolerance (hundreds of ulp near
zeros of sin/cos), and x86 used bare x87 `fsin`/`fcos`, whose argument reduction uses a 66-bit pi
and loses every bit of accuracy past ~2^63 (and is visibly wrong near k*pi/2 well before that).
SINCE 6.6.9: fdlibm ports (Cody-Waite + __kernel_rem_pio2, k_sin/k_cos with y_lo, s_atan with an
exact +/-pi/4) on every target, and x86 / PE / x86-Mach-O call that same software routine — at a
DSP-speed cost. The promise matches exp/ln's: <= 1 ulp with IEEE specials, NOT bit-identical across
targets; compare with a ulp bar.

[[entries]]
name = "undefined_call_refused_whatever_its_shape_since_6_6_9"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.9 a REACHABLE call to an undefined fn fails the build on every target and in every position — including a tail call on aarch64"
category = "cyrius"
---
Before 6.6.9 the x86 forks refused a reachable undefined call, but an aarch64 build let one through
when it sat in TAIL position (`return missing(x);`), shipping a binary that crashed there; four of
the seven forks also skipped the pre-pass that WARNS about undefined calls. Since 6.6.9 one top-level
directive dispatcher serves all seven forks, every fork runs the pre-pass, and a reachable undefined
call is refused whatever its shape. A call from a fn DCE proves dead is still only a warning.
Related 6.6.9 diagnostics: a struct/union re-declared with a DIFFERENT layout warns (the first
layout used to win silently), an enum member and a top-level var of the same name with different
values warn, and bare `#deprecated` needs its message. Since 6.6.11 a `#derive`d struct
re-declared with different field names, order or size is an ERROR, because its generated
accessors would address the first layout's bytes.


[[entries]]
name = "struct_field_types_checked_and_f64_fields_typed_since_6_6_10"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.10 an f64/f32 struct field loads typed, a struct value assigned into a struct-typed field copies the whole struct, and an unknown / forward / self field type is a compile error; u8..u64 and f32 fields were 8 bytes until 6.7.10 (u8 / u16 / u32 / f32 are packed at their widths since)"
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc < 6.6.10, all silent: (1) `x: f64` was not in the field-type ladder, so it registered as
an i64 and every operator on `p.x` was an INTEGER op on the bits (`p.x + p.y` = NaN for
1.5 + 2.0, `p.x * p.y` = 0, `p.x < p.y` wrong for negatives), through a local, a parameter, a
global, a chain, a union, a method's self and Pair<f64>; correct code (`2.0 * p.x`) drew a false
kind-1 warning. (2) `b.v = p` into a struct-typed field stored ONE word (from an address-passed
struct parameter, the pointer). (3) A field type naming nothing (`a: Nonexist`), a struct used
above its declaration (`struct A { b: B; }` before B — A got a 16-byte layout instead of 24),
`v: i16v8` (registered as a 2-byte i16) all compiled; `struct Node { next: Node; }` crashed cycc.
SINCE 6.6.10: the field load types F64/F32 (a per-field float-kind side table, no layout change);
the field store copies the whole struct from a name, a call / method / operator result or another
field, and refuses a different struct type by name — since 6.6.11 also a <= 8 B method /
operator result, which arrives in rax untyped and was not checked at 6.6.10 (the reverse
direction, `q = b.v` / `var p: P3 = b.v`, copies whole since 6.6.11 too: see
language/features.cyml `struct_value_copies_and_result_type_checks_v6611`); an unknown type is
refused by name, a forward one with "declare it before 'A'", a vector and a self-reference by
name.
⚠ AN ODD-SIZED FIELD (3/5/6/7 B, e.g. `struct Odd { a: i8; b: i16; }`) took the store ladder's
8-byte arm for any value in rax — a <= 8 B method / operator result, a top-level call, a plain
integer — and silently overwrote the fields AFTER it (on every backend and real host). Since
6.6.10 such a store writes exactly the field's bytes (4/2/1-byte chunks, shifted into place).
Width ladders that fall to "else: 8 bytes" are the recurring shape: an odd size is not 8.
⚠ WIDTHS CHANGED IN 6.7.10: through 6.7.9 u8/u16/u32/u64 and f32 fields were 8 bytes each (only
i8/i16/i32 were narrow — `struct { a: u8; b: u8; }` was 16 bytes). Since 6.7.10 u8/u16/u32 are 1/2/4
bytes, ZERO-extended, and f32 is 4 (its binary32 bits), all PACKED (that struct is 2 bytes) — a layout
and ABI change; u64 / bool stay 8. See struct_handles_and_values_since_6_7_10 and language/features.cyml
struct_field_widths.
⚠ `#derive(accessors)` getters are `load64` and stay UNTYPED on purpose (bit-exact getter
compares in svara/samay): `p.x + p.y` is a float add, `P_x(&p) + P_y(&p)` an integer one.


[[entries]]
name = "float_builtin_results_are_float_operands_since_6_6_10"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.10 + - * / on a float builtin's result is float arithmetic (option D: a result flag, not a type); compares on builtin results stay integer; unary minus on an untyped float-initialised var and an integer constant into an f64/f32 slot warn"
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc < 6.6.10, silent: a float-returning builtin (f64_sqrt, f64_add, f64_sin, f32_from, ...)
left the estype of its LAST ARGUMENT, 0 under the i64-boxed idiom, so every + - * / on its
result was INTEGER arithmetic on the bits: `f64_sqrt(u) * f64_sqrt(u)` = 0,
`f64_sin(z) - f64_cos(z)` = -4.0, `f64_mul(u, v) / v` = 1, `-(f64_sqrt(v))` the integer
negation, `-f64_sqrt(u) * 2.0` an integer multiply — and `f64_add(p, u) * 2.0` differed from
`f64_add(u, p) * 2.0` because the argument ORDER decided the type.
SINCE 6.6.10: the result is a float operand of + - * / on either side, directly, in parens
and after unary minus, whatever the arguments were typed. It is a transient flag (code pointer
+ token index at the end of the call), NOT a type: ⚠ `==` / `<` on builtin results are still
INTEGER compares of the bits (41 ecosystem sites rely on that — hex constants, hisab's -0
check, ganita's `f64_floor(v) == v`); `f64_neg(a) < f64_neg(b)` is wrong for negatives — use
f64_lt/f64_gt/f64_eq. `var x = f64_sqrt(u);` is still UNTYPED (declare `: f64`). Code that
wants the integer ulp distance of two results must go through an untyped temp first:
`var a = f64_atan(x); d = a - b;` (cyrius's own exp2_atan_bigtrig.tcyr had to).
New warnings (CYRIUS_TYPE_CHECK=0 silences them): `var c = 1.5; -c` is integer negation
(-3.0; `-z` of 0.0 is +0, `-n` of -2.5 is 1.75) → "unary minus on an untyped variable holding
a float is integer negation (declare it f64)" — judged at the DECLARATION (float literal, f64
value, float builtin, or a copy of such a var) and, since 6.6.11, RE-JUDGED by every plain
assignment: `var g = 0; g = 1.5; -g` warns and `var h = 1.5; h = 7; -h` does not, for locals
and globals alike (a compound `h += ..` keeps the judgement). Not flow-sensitive: the last
assignment the compiler READ wins — source order in a fn, and for a global source order across
fns. (6.6.11 also TYPES a pure copy of a declared float, `var t = p.y;` / `var u = y;`, so
that one is a float, not a warned untyped var — see language/features.cyml
`f64_named_type_and_operators`.) And
`var t: f64 = 1;` / `t = 1;` / `p.x = 1;` / `P { 1, 2 }` store 0x1 (a subnormal) → "an
integer stored into an f64/f32 slot keeps its integer bits"; only compile-time integer
constants are judged, 0 and hex bit patterns >= 2^52 are exempt, a runtime untyped value
(`var x: f64 = load64(p)`) is the legal boxed idiom and is not.
Ecosystem at landing: 0 binary or diagnostic changes over 6,226 non-vendored files.

6.7.11 — THE KIND-4 WARNING REACHES ARGUMENTS. An integer constant into an `f64` / `f32` keeps its BITS (ADR-002) —
`f(3)` passes a subnormal, about 1.5e-323 — and since 6.7.11 it warns at an argument too (a call, a method, a tail
call, `#inline`, a named argument, an operator's right operand). Write `3.0`, or `f64_from(n)`. One integer + f64
mix warns ONCE however far its result is carried.


[[entries]]
name = "generic_using_t_as_a_struct_has_no_i64_instance"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.10 a generic that uses a T-typed value as a struct (`p.y`) has no i64 or other scalar instance: every scalar call, tail call, &g and forwarding generic is a compile error naming it; struct inference binds the struct instance on every call path"
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc < 6.6.10, silent: `fn g<T>(p: T) { .. p.y .. }` has no valid i64 form, so its BASE (the
i64 instance) is emitted as a `rax = 0` dead stub — and that decision was made only while the
base was being emitted, recorded nowhere. So `g<i64>(5)`, `g(5)`, `return g(5);`, `&g` (then
fncall1) and `outer<i64>(5)` with `fn outer<T>(p: T) { return g<T>(p) + 2; }` all compiled
and returned 0 (outer: 2); an inlinable body said "no struct type in scope for 'p'" instead.
Worse, the STRUCT calls ran the stub too: struct inference existed only in the inline replay
(positionally, re-typing the param i64), so `var r = g(p)`, `return g(p)`, `g(mk(2))`,
`g(2, p)` (T on param 1), `g(q)` with `q: Pt` a parameter and `outer<Pt>(p)` forwarding
`g(p)` all returned 0 — only the explicit `g<Pt>(p)` worked.
SINCE 6.6.10: pass 1 records, BY NAME, each generic's T-typed names (params + `var q: T`
locals), whether one is used as a struct (`x.f`; `n..m` is a range), and which param carries
each type parameter; a fixpoint marks a generic that forwards a T-typed value or T itself into
such a generic. Every scalar use of one is refused: "generic 'g' has no i64 (or other scalar)
instance: it uses its type parameter as a struct ...". A struct argument (local, param,
global, struct-returning call) instantiates `g$Pt` exactly like `g<Pt>(p)` on every path —
expression, var init, tail (`return g(p)` leaves the tail path ONLY for such a callee: a
scalar generic keeps its tail call, a 20M-deep `cnt<T>` must not overflow), argument, struct
receive, `r = g(p);`, `b.v = g(p);`. Only a WHOLE argument (a bare name, one whole call)
binds T — reading the argument's first token made `f(p.y)` bind Pt and fail on an undefined
`Pt_add`; a `pp: *Pt` parameter is a pointer and binds i64. Two inferred struct type args
(or a struct beside a scalar) are refused like the explicit `g<Pt, Q>`, on the paths that
emit their OWN call too (the resolver used to swallow the failure and run the base). A
signature naming `Box<Pt>` / `Box<T>` is the instance, not the base, and an instance's `: T`
return with T a struct returns that struct (it returned its address).
⚠ Every path that emits its own call must resolve the callee (`_gen_resolve_call`), not
FINDFN the base — the first 6.6.10 cut missed the register-pair return check and the
assignment paths, and each miss was silent or a regression.
⚠ The stub scan is lexical, so it errs toward MORE stubs — a loud refusal, never a silent 0.
⚠ OPEN AT 6.7.8, FIXED IN 6.7.11 (the paragraph below; filed as
issues/archived/2026-10-10-generic-base-reads-carrier-field-as-struct.md): the scan saw only T-typed
NAMES, so a generic that reads its struct PARAMETER's T field as a struct — `fn gy<T>(b: Box<T>):
i64 { return b.v.y; }` — failed at its own definition ("expected ';', got '.'"), called or not.
On 6.7.8 – 6.7.10 bind the field first: `var t: T = b.v; return t.y;` (then `gy(b)` with `b:
Box<Pt>` infers T = Pt, 6.7.8, and runs).

6.7.11 — MORE STUB MAKERS, AND THE METHOD SPELLING. A generic that reads its CARRIER's T field as a struct (`b.v.y` with
`b: Box<T>` / `*Box<T>`) has only the dead-stub base too — it compiles now (it did not), `gy(b)` calls `gy$Pt`, and a
scalar binding is refused. A generic METHOD whose body uses T as a struct is refused for a scalar exactly as the free
call is: `p.pick(9)` like `P_pick(p, 9)` (it returned 0 silently through 6.7.10), and a generic that forwards T into
such a method — through a name, a field chain or a call's result — is itself a stub.

6.7.14 — WHAT COUNTS AS A STRUCT USE, AND WHAT BINDS T (GN-12, GN-14, GN-15, GN-17):
- A T forwarded INSIDE a struct carrier into a stub generic (`gx(mk1(x))`, `gx(b)` with `b: Box<T>`, `q.pick(b)`) makes
  the forwarder a stub too; a bare T-typed value passed whole into a carrier position does too since 6.7.15 (GN-17).
- A forward of T to a BOUNDED generic whose i64 base the bound refuses (`fn o<T>(x: T) { return gb(x); }`, `gb<T:
  Show>`, no `impl Show for i64`) is a bound refusal, not a struct stub: `o(h)` with `h: f64` calls `o$f64`, and only
  the i64 call is refused — "generic 'o' has no i64 instance: it forwards its type parameter to 'gb'", with a note
  naming the impl the chain's end needs. A chain of more than 64 such forwards was refused at a definition through
  6.7.14; since 6.7.15 it is judged at any depth, the CALL that instantiates it held to the nesting bound (about 120
  forwards; GN-20, GN-20r) — since 6.7.16 to the compiler's stack, ~300 links (GB-2).
- A bounded method call on a carrier's T field (`b.v.show()`, `b: Box<T>`, `T: Show`) is a TRAIT use, not a struct use:
  it is the bound's call, as on a T-typed name.
- A struct-using generic given a `*S` LOCAL through `x: T` has no S to bind: a pointer through `x: T` is an i64 now (it
  bound S since 6.6.17, against the guide), so `g(q)` with `q: *S` is refused — write `g<S>(q)`. Through `p: *T` every
  struct pointer binds its pointee.
6.7.16 — TWO MORE STUB LINES (6.7.16's D1, GB-19): `return &n` of a T value in a fn returning a struct, and a struct
result over 8 B stored in a T-typed name (`var y: T = mkp();`, `y = ..`, `p = ..` into `*T` / `**T`, and through a `*T`,
D99) make a generic struct-only too — a scalar call is refused at the call, its note naming the line
(generics_since_6_7_16).
generics_since_6_7_14, generics_since_6_7_15, generics_since_6_7_16. CHANGELOG [6.7.14] GN-12, GN-14, GN-14r, GN-15,
GN-17; [6.7.15] GN-17, GN-20; [6.7.16] GB-2, GB-19, SS-46.

[[entries]]
name = "closure_returns_belong_to_the_closure_since_6_6_16"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.16 a `return f(..)` inside a closure body (`|x| { .. }`, `|| { .. }`) is the closure's own: a fn that builds such a closure is no longer treated as returning a `: stack` pair, refused 'bind both' at its callers, or warned for its own plain return — and a closure that mixes a pair return with a plain one is warned as its own fn"
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc 6.6.0 - 6.6.15, wrong diagnostics: with `fn h(x) { return Ok(x); }`,

    fn mk(b) { var g = |x| { return h(x + b); }; return g; }   # returns ONE value: the closure
    fn main() { var g = mk(41); return 0; }                    # error: "bind both"

was refused, because the two post-parse scans that find pair-returning fns
(`_pair_prescan`, `_warn_mixed_pair_returns` in src/frontend/parse.cyr) skipped a nested
body only at a `fn` token, and a closure literal has none — so the closure's
`return h(..)` was booked to `mk`. Consequences: every single-variable bind of `mk(..)`
inside a fn was a hard error (top level: a warning), the flag propagated through
forwarding wrappers, `mk`'s own `return g;` drew "returns a `: stack` pair on another path
but a SINGLE value here", a real pair fn whose closure said `return 0;` was warned, and a
real mixed-return fn was warned at the closure's return instead of its own.
⛔ Following the error's hint was WRONG CODE: `var t, v = mk(41);` compiled (warning only)
and `v` was a stale rdx — `fncall1(v, 1)` exited 139. The workaround hisab shipped (3.3.1+)
is still valid: bind both halves inside the closure and `return 0;`.

SINCE 6.6.16: both scans skip a closure body — a `{` immediately after `|` or `||` (exact:
closure params are untyped with no return annotation, and `{` never starts an expression).
The closure's OWN pair-ness is untouched: its body parses as its own fn, so
`var t, v = fncall1(g, 1)` on a closure that returns `Ok(..)` gets both halves. A brace-less
closure (`|x| h(x)`) never had the problem (an expression has no `return`).

A CLOSURE IS A FN FOR THE MIXED-RETURN RULE. Each closure body is judged as its own unit, at
any depth: if its own body (nested closures and fns skipped) says `return f(..)` or `f(..)?`
with f a pair fn, every other return must be a variant too, or the first plain one is warned
"a closure in `mk` returns a `: stack` pair on another path but a SINGLE value here" ("a
closure returns ..." at top level / in an impl method). So

    var g = |x| { if (x > 0) { return h(x); } return 0; };    # warned at `return 0;`
    var t, v = fncall1(g, 0);                                   # v would be a stale rdx

Return `Err(..)` (or `None()` for an Option) on that path. `return None();` beside
`return Some(x);` is a whole value and stays silent, as in a fn.

⚠ For compiler work: (1) skipping a nested body is HALF the job — whatever skips a unit must
also judge it as its own, or the skip silently deletes the only diagnostic that unit had (the
first cut of this fix did exactly that; the gate's mutation ledger row c pins it). (2) If
closure params or a return type ever gain annotations (`|x: i64|: R { .. }`),
`_tok_is_closure_body` must scan the param list instead of looking one token back — the
closure parser (parse_expr.cyr) carries a comment saying so.
Gate: tests/gates/frontend/stack_enum_closure_return_scope.sh. CHANGELOG [6.6.16]

[[entries]]
name = "pair_callee_any_spelling_6_6_16"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.16 an explicit generic call `g<T>(..)` and a method call `p.m(..)` are the same call as `f(..)` for a `: stack` pair: `?` propagates it, `var a, b = ..` destructures it, and a single bind / assignment / store of it is refused. Before that they were not recognised: `?` exited 139 and a single bind dropped the payload silently."
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc 6.6.15 and older, x86 and aarch64 (Result is the `: stack` value form since 6.6.0):

    fn gdiv<T>(n: T, d: T) { if (d == 0) { return Err(99); } return Ok(n / d); }
    struct Pt { x; y; }
    fn Pt_div(self: Pt, d) { if (d == 0) { return Err(99); } return Ok(self.x / d); }

    fn a(n, d) { var v = gdiv<i32>(n, d)?; return Ok(v); }   # rc 139
    fn b(p: Pt, d) { var v = p.div(d)?; return Ok(v); }      # rc 139
    fn c(n, d) { var v = gdiv<i32>(n, d); return v; }        # compiled; Ok(3) read 0 (payload gone)
    fn e(n, d) { var t, v = gdiv<i32>(n, d); }               # "multi-value destructure needs a call"
    fn g(p: Pt, d) { p.div(d)?; return Ok(0); }              # "expected ';', got unknown"

The plain spellings `pdiv(n, d)?` / `Pt_div(p, d)?` were right, and `var v = pdiv(n, d);` was refused.

WHY: every place that asks "does this call return a pair" — `?`'s lowering choice, the lossy-bind refusals,
`return`'s wrapper propagation, the destructure, and the forward-reference prescan — tested for `IDENT (`
only. `g<i32>(` has a `<` there and `p.div(` a `.`, so all of them answered "no pair": `?` took the BOXED
lowering, which dereferences its operand, and read the tag (0 or 1) as a pointer.

SINCE 6.6.16 one resolver answers for every asker, in the three spellings: `name(`, `name<..>(` (the generic
template carries the pair flag) and `x.m(` with x a single name whose struct is known (a local, a closure's
capture, or a struct global — the order the method call itself resolves it in). So:

    var v = gdiv<i32>(n, d)?;     var v = p.div(d)?;     p.div(d)?;      # propagate both halves
    var t, v = gdiv<i32>(n, d);   var t, v = p.div(d);                   # in a fn, a closure, top level
    var v = gdiv<i32>(n, d);      x = p.div(d);   store64(&s, p.div(d)); # refused: bind both

A wrapper `fn w(..) { return p.div(d); }` forwards the pair, before or after its caller. `return g<T>(..)` /
`return p.m(..)` beside `return Ok(..)` is a whole variant (no mixed-return warning); the same spellings
returning ONE value still warn there.

FORWARD WRAPPERS: a caller parsed ABOVE its wrapper learns that the wrapper forwards a pair from a token
pass that runs before the wrapper is parsed (no locals exist yet). That pass finds `p` BY SCOPE (walking
back from the call; a block closed before it is skipped, and a closure looks through to the fn whose locals
it captures) and types it from a `: T` annotation (a param's `: *T` too), a struct literal, a
struct-returning call, or a generic's `: T` return bound at that call. A receiver typed only per instance (a
generic fn's own `p: T`) resolves when every struct's `_m` agrees on the pair flag; otherwise the call is
unknown — no flag, and no mixed-return warning. The first cut took the lexically LAST `p: T` and refused a
correct one-value bind; a wrong answer is worse than "unknown", so the pass returns "unknown" rather than
guess.

NOT COVERED at 6.6.16 (no call path typed these receivers either): `a.b.m()`, `f().m()`, an untyped impl `self`. (This
entry said a top-level `var v = f();` is not refused — it is since 6.7.6; and since 6.7.7 every one-slot store of
a pair is: stack_pair_store_refused_everywhere_v677.)

6.7.16 — "ANY SPELLING" INCLUDES A METHOD ENDING A CHAIN, ON ANY RECEIVER (SB-1, SB-1r – SB-1r4). `w.p.rs(3)`,
`(*pw).p.rs(3)`, `f(&w).p.rs(3)`, `callptr(&g, ..).p.rs(3)`, a generic step (`w.p.idv(q).rs(3)`) and a generic call head
(`gw(2).p.rs(3)`) are the method call to every pair lookahead: `?` propagates, `var a, b = ..` destructures — and code
written against 6.7.15 that bound such a chain's pair ONE-WIDE (`var v = w.p.rs(3);`, `x = ..`, a field, `store64`)
compiled and silently kept the tag; it is refused "bind both" now. A forwarder below its caller that returns such a
chain is a pair too (`var v = fwd(w);` refused). The old workaround — `var p = w.p; var c, d = p.m2();` — can go. With
an `impl T` written above `struct T`, an untyped `self` is a `*T` since 6.7.16, as with the struct first (6.7.16's D6,
GS-13: generics_since_6_7_16).
⚠ A `: stack` pair fn through `callptr(&f, ..)` / `fncallN(&f, ..)` is still no pair (staged for the user's decision):
parser_syntax.cyml parentheses_pair_calls_and_callptr_forms_v6713. receives_since_6_7_16. CHANGELOG [6.7.16] SB-1,
GS-13.

ECOSYSTEM: a new refusal in a consumer is a payload that was being dropped there. Fix it the way the message
says — `var tag, val = p.m(..);` — or propagate with `?`. Tests: tests/tcyr/crossos/method_pair_result.tcyr,
generic_struct_inference.tcyr (SQ rows); gates stack_enum_lossy_context.sh (axis 14),
stack_enum_mixed_return_warning.sh. See CHANGELOG [6.6.16].

[[entries]]
name = "typed_pointer_local_slot_v6616"
description = "A local `*i8` / `*i16` / `*i32` truncated the address it held (fixed 6.6.16); the per-site `p + n` step it documented was replaced by `sizeof(T)` everywhere at 6.6.17; since 6.7.8 `*p` through a narrow `*T` reads and writes the ELEMENT (it was always the 8-byte word), and `p[i]` works; since 6.7.16 `*(&x)` is x for every typed x — a narrow integer, an f64 / f32 / bool, an inline `Str` (D21, D70, D71)"
category = "cyrius"
---
⛔ BEFORE 6.6.16: `var buf: i64[4]; var p: *i8 = &buf;` — the `*T` ladder gave
*i8/*i16/*i32 pointer scale 1/2/4, PARSE_VAR wrote it as the local's SLTYPE,
and a positive SLTYPE below 8 is a narrow scalar's WIDTH. The slot stored
1/2/4 bytes of the address: `p == &buf` false, `load8(p + 1)` SIGSEGV, on
every backend (a Windows stack can sit below 4 GiB, which hid *i32 there).
Globals and params never had it (8-byte slots). Fix: `_sl_ptr_type` — a
`*T` local is SLTYPE 0 for those three (a plain 8-byte word), SLTYPE 8 for
every other *T, as before. Test: tests/tcyr/crossos/typed_pointer_slot_width.tcyr.
The 8-byte slot keeps its POINTER-ness for the expression type, so
`var q: *i16 = p;` / `= p + 1` are not "assigning non-pointer to typed
pointer". No emitted byte changed for the 8-byte case. (A `*T` PARAM, and a
`*T` local captured by a closure, still draw that warning when copied into a
typed pointer — pre-existing at 6.6.15.)

⟳ SUPERSEDED 6.6.17 — `p + n` now steps `sizeof(T)` at EVERY site (local, param,
capture, global in either zone, `*T` field, `: *T` result), `q - p` of two `*T` is the
element count, and a `*T` param / capture loads as a pointer (the warning above is gone).
The per-site table that stood here (local *i8/*i16/*i32 +1, other locals +8, params +1,
declaration-block globals +8, ...) is history: see language/features.cyml
`typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617`. (`*p` still loaded 8 bytes then — see 6.7.8 below.)
⛔ And `p - base` with an UNTYPED `base` holding an address is `p - n` (base is read as
an element count: a garbage address, no diagnostic), while `p - &buf` is refused: for
bytes bind both sides to plain vars, for elements subtract two `*T` (same entry).

⟳ 6.7.8 — `*p` AND `p[i]` WORK ON THE ELEMENT (an approved change to what compiling code
computes; language/features.cyml `pointer_subscripts_v678`). Before 6.7.8 every `*p` read
8 bytes and every `*p = v` / `*p OP= v` wrote 8, whatever T was:

    var a: i32[2]; a[0] = 7; a[1] = 9;
    var q: *i32 = &a;
    var x = *q;      # 6.7.7: 7 | 9 << 32 (both elements)      6.7.8: 7
    *q = 5;          # 6.7.7: wrote 5 AND zeroed a[1]          6.7.8: a = {5, 9}
    var p: *u8 = &a;
    *p += 1;         # 6.7.8: one byte, wrapping at 255

Through `*u8`..`*u32`, `*i8`..`*i32`, `*f32`, `*p` is `p[0]`: sign- / zero-extended, an
f32 typed f32 and rounded on store, with `p[0] = v`'s warnings. The operand must be KNOWN to
be that pointer: a name, a `*T` field, a `: *T` call, `p ± n`, `n + p` (`*(1 + p)` on a
`*u8` is one byte too — but `1 + p` is still no typed pointer for anything else: `var q:
*u8 = 1 + p;` warns, `(1 + p8) - q32` is a byte difference), parentheses, an if-expression
of such — and since 6.7.13 `*pp` of a `**T` (so `**pp` on a narrow T reads at its width).
Everything else is still the 8-byte WORD: an untyped name (`var w = p; *w` is the
explicit word access), `*i64` / `*u64` / `*f64` / `*bool` (`p[0]` is their TYPED spelling —
`*p * 2.0` through a `*f64` still warns), `m & p`, `-p`, a call result's untyped
field. To keep the old word through a typed pointer: `load64(p)` / `store64(p, v)`.
`p[i]` on a name, a field (`h.p[i]`) or a call (`f()[i]`) declared `*T` works since 6.7.8;
`var q = p; q[0]` is refused (q is an untyped word). CHANGELOG [6.7.8].

⟳ 6.7.15 / 6.7.16 — `*(&x)` IS x, FOR EVERY x THE COMPILER TYPES. `*(&p)` of a typed pointer keeps p's type since 6.7.15
(6.7.15's D21), and since 6.7.16 `*(&pi) OP= n` steps elements as `pi OP= n` does (ST-10; it stepped bytes). Since
6.7.16 the rest is x too: a narrow integer local or packed global reads and writes ITS width (6.7.16's D21 — `*(&a)` of
an i32 -1 is -1, `*(&GQ) = 7` writes 4 bytes; it was the 8-byte word, which zeroed the next global), an `f64` / `f32` /
`bool` keeps its type (D70: `*(&f) + 1.0` is a float add), an inline `Str` is t (D71), on the read, the store and as a
dyn source (SS-19); a narrow PARAMETER's `*(&y) = v` writes its width since its read narrows (D72). For the 8-byte word
say `load64(&x)` / `store64(&x, v)`. Still the word: a closure's capture (its environment copy); `&x + n` and `*(&x +
0)` are byte-pointer reads. struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_16. CHANGELOG [6.7.16] ST-10, ST-11, SS-13, SS-16,
SS-17, SS-19.

[[entries]]
name = "a_tuple_is_a_value_only_as_a_tuple_v677"
description = "cycc 6.7.7 tuples (B4): a whole tuple is not one value — `var u = t;`, `t == u`, `if (t)`, `print(t)` are refused (annotate the copy); `var x = f();` still keeps a multi-value call's FIRST value while `var t: (i64, i64) = f();` keeps them all; a multi-value call is bound before it is stored into a tuple field or passed as a tuple argument; generics and const fns take no tuple."
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc 6.7.7 (language/features.cyml `tuples_v677`).

A WHOLE TUPLE IS NOT A VALUE. Under the bare struct rule `t == u` would compare first words and `var x = t;`
copy one; both are refused by name:

    var t = (1, 2);
    var x = t;              # error: tuple 't' used as a value - take an element (`t.0`) or copy it whole
                            #        into a tuple
    var u: (i64, i64) = t;  # ok: a copy, annotated
    if (t.0 == u.0) { .. }  # compare elements

The same for `t + 1`, `!t`, `if (t)`, `print(t)`, an argument to an untyped parameter, and a whole tuple FIELD
(`h.p == 5`). Copy into a tuple place instead: a tuple-annotated var, `u = t;`, a tuple field, a tuple
parameter.

ONE NAME IS STILL THE FIRST VALUE. A multi-value call bound to one name is its first value (the documented ret2
/ rethi() idiom, unchanged):

    var x = divmod(20, 3);                 # 6 — the quotient only
    var t: (i64, i64) = divmod(20, 3);     # (6, 2) — declare a tuple to keep every value
    q, r = divmod(20, 3);                  # or re-assign two existing variables

Through 6.7.13 `t = (f());` was a value, not a capture (the destructure refused `(f())` too); since 6.7.14 parentheses
around the WHOLE call are transparent to both (6.7.14's D45): `t = (f());`, `var t: (i64, i64) = ((f()));` capture and
`var a, b = (f(7));` destructures — `(f(7)) + 1` is still no call. `h.p = f();` and `take(f())` into
a tuple field / parameter are refused — bind it first, `var t: (i64, i64) = f();`, then store or pass `t`. A
struct-returning callee is never captured or re-assigned: a struct return is not multiple values.

GENERICS AND CONSTS TAKE NO TUPLE (yet). A tuple binds no type parameter — neither `first<(i64, i64)>(t)` nor
`first(t)` — and a generic fn or struct holds no tuple type: pass an element (`first(t.0)`), or write the fn for
the tuple type, `fn f(t: (i64, i64))`. A const fn takes no tuple parameter and a constant holds no tuple
(`const C = (1, 2);` is refused): a compile-time value is one value. Gate: tests/gates/frontend/tuple_checked.sh
(R11, R12, R16, R4, R5 rows). See CHANGELOG [6.7.7].

[[entries]]
name = "stack_pair_store_refused_everywhere_v677"
description = "Since cycc 6.7.7 every one-slot store of a `: stack` pair is refused — a field (whatever its type), an element, `*p`, any `OP=`, a tuple-literal element — not only `var r = f();` / `r = f();` / `store64`: each kept the TAG and dropped the payload. Destructure, re-assign both, or `?` to store the payload."
category = "cyrius"
---
A `: stack` Result / Option / Either is two values (tag, payload). Before 6.7.7 these compiled clean and kept the
TAG — for an `Err`, the dropped half is the error code:

    h.n = f(7);      # any field type — a struct-typed field took the tag as its first word; a slice's .len
    a[0] = f(7);
    *p = f(7);
    x += f(7);       # every OP=, on a variable, a field, an element, *p, a u128

Since 6.7.7 (user, 2026-10-09 — the guide had said so all along) each is

    error: a `: stack` enum returns two values — bind both: `var tag, val = f();`

at the callee, in a for step, at top level, for every call spelling (pair_callee_any_spelling_6_6_16), and
under `--syntax-only` (`cyrius lint`) for a field the file cannot see; a tuple-literal element `(f(7), 3)`
likewise. WRITE INSTEAD:

    var t, v = f(7);  h.n = v;      # bind both, store what you need
    t, v = f(7);                    # re-assign both existing variables (6.7.7)
    h.n = f(7)?;                    # store the payload and propagate the Err

A boxed enum, a nullary variant and a one-value call are untouched. A plain argument `g(f(7))` keeps the tag (by
design since 6.7.11's D22); a struct-literal element `P { f(7), 3 }` is refused "bind both" since 6.7.11. Since 6.7.14
(6.7.14's D46) a store whose WHOLE value is parenthesised and starts with the pair call — `var v = (f(7) + 1);`,
`h.a = ((f(7)) + 1);` — is refused as its unwrapped spelling is (receives_and_multi_value_since_6_7_14). Gate:
stack_enum_lossy_context.sh axis 15. See CHANGELOG [6.7.7].

[[entries]]
name = "a_default_belongs_to_the_definition_v677"
description = "cycc 6.7.7 default arguments (B6): a default is a compile-time constant of the DEFINING file, evaluated once — a caller's locals never change it and `b = a * 2` is refused; a float default needs a typed parameter and an integer default on an f64 is its bit pattern; fncallN / callptr pass every argument, by position; a `return f(..)` that fills or names is not a tail call; named arguments run in the order written."
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc 6.7.7 (language/features.cyml `default_and_named_arguments_v677`).

IT IS A CONSTANT OF THE DEFINITION, NOT THE CALL. A default is evaluated once, at compile time, in the
top-level scope of the file that defines the fn: a caller's local `const K` never changes `b = K`, and a default
naming a parameter of its own fn is refused —

    fn g(a, b = a * 2): i64 { return b; }
    # error: a default must be a compile-time constant: 'a' is a parameter of 'g'

— compute it in the body from a sentinel instead. A float default needs a typed parameter (`x: f64 = 1.5`;
untyped, a float is only its bits — ADR-002); on an f64 an integer default is its bit pattern, so write `1.0`
("… an integer default is its bit pattern - write 1.0").

FNCALLN / CALLPTR PASS EVERY ARGUMENT, BY POSITION. Defaults fill only direct calls: `fncall1(&f, 1)` to
`fn f(a, b = 1, c = 2)` is refused ("a call through a fn pointer passes every argument: 'f' takes 3 (defaults
fill only direct calls)"), and a `name:` there is refused. Through a pointer held in a variable nothing is
checked (as for any fn): pass all of its parameters.

A FILLED OR NAMED `return f(..)` IS NOT A TAIL CALL. It is an ordinary call (like any `return f(..)` in a fn
with a defer). Write deep recursion with every argument by position: that form keeps its jmp.

NAMED ARGUMENTS RUN IN THE ORDER WRITTEN. `g(c: h(), a: k())` runs h() before k() — side effects follow the
text, not the parameter list. Gate: tests/gates/frontend/default_named_args_checked.sh. See CHANGELOG [6.7.7].

[[entries]]
name = "a_dyn_holds_an_address_or_a_value_v678"
description = "cycc 6.7.8 checked `dyn`: a dyn formed from `&p` holds the ADDRESS (one formed from `&local` and returned dangles); a scalar impl's dyn holds the VALUE (`&n` and a `*i64` are refused — bind the value); an untyped name is refused (it may hide a pointer); a dyn result goes only into a dyn; `var o: dyn Tr;` with no value is refused; a call through a dyn returns its member's declared value class."
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc 6.7.8 (language/features.cyml `dyn_v678`).

A DYN HOLDS `&p`, NOT A COPY. Forming from a pointer stores the pointer:

    fn mk(): dyn Area { var q = Sq { 3 }; return &q; }   # compiles — and dangles, as `return &q;` does
    var o: dyn Area = &q;                                # o sees every later write to q

Form from a record that outlives the dyn (a global, a caller's local, a heap record).

A SCALAR'S DYN HOLDS THE VALUE. For `impl Area for i64` the data word IS the number:

    var n: i64 = 5;
    var b: dyn Area = n;       # ok — the value 5
    var c: dyn Area = 5;       # ok — a literal (`5` / `'A'` an i64, `2.5` an f64, `true` a bool)
    var d: dyn Area = &n;      # error: cannot form dyn Area from `&n` - a dyn of a scalar holds its value:
                               #        write `n`
    var e = 5;
    var f: dyn Area = e;       # error: cannot form dyn Area from 'e' - an untyped name may hold a pointer
                               #        (ADR-002): pass `&e` for a struct, or bind it to a declared type

An untyped name is refused because under ADR-002 an i64 may hide a pointer; a declared scalar (`n: i64`, `u:
u64`, `b: bool`, an enum, `cstring`, f64 / f32) says which. There is no implicit `&`: `var o: dyn Area = q;` with
`q` a struct is refused ("… - no implicit `&`: write `&q` (the dyn holds its address)").

A DYN RESULT GOES ONLY INTO A DYN. `x = mk();` into an i64 or untyped variable, `var y: u8 = mk();`, `mk() + 1`, a
top-level `var G = mk();` after the first statement and a tail `return mk();` from a fn of another type are
refused ("a dyn Area result used as a value - call one of its methods, or copy it whole into a dyn"): each kept
the data word alone. `var o: dyn Area;` with no value is refused — struct locals are not zeroed and a vtable is
formed, never left to the frame; a struct local HOLDING a dyn likewise (globals are zeroed).

THE CALL'S VALUE CLASS IS THE MEMBER'S DECLARATION. A member declared `: Result` / `: Option` returns its pair —
`o.div(d)?` propagates, `var t, v = o.div(d);` binds both, `var v = o.div(d);` is refused "bind both"; any other
member returns one value and a destructure of it is refused. An impl method that returns a `: stack` pair under a
member declaring one value is refused when the pair is first formed: declare the return as the enum, in the trait
and the impl. Gate: tests/gates/frontend/dyn_checked.sh. See CHANGELOG [6.7.8].

[[entries]]
name = "a_slice_is_a_fn_local_and_reads_signed_elements_signed_v678"
description = "cycc 6.7.8 slices: `s[i] = v` / `s[i] OP= v` work and are bounds-checked in EVERY build (exit 134); `s[i]` on an `[i8]` / `[i16]` / `[i32]` is the SIGNED value (it was 0..255 / 0..65535 / 0..2^32-1); a slice is a fn-local — a parameter, return, field or declaration-zone global is refused by name; `s[i] OP= v` on an f64 / f32 / bool element is refused. Since 6.7.16 a generic struct instance is an element (`[Box<i64>]`, D89), and `s[i]` reads 1-, 2-, 4-, 8- or 16-byte elements only."
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc 6.7.8 (language/features.cyml `slice_type_v58x` § SINCE 6.7.8).

A SIGNED ELEMENT READS SIGNED (an approved value change):

    var buf: i8[4];
    var s: [i8] = 0;
    slice_set(&s, &buf, 4);
    s[0] = 0 - 3;
    if (s[0] < 0) { .. }      # 6.7.8: taken (-3)      6.7.7: never — s[0] read 253

The same bytes in an `i8[N]` array, through a `*i8` and in an `i8` field always read -3; the slice getters
zero-extended. `s[i] & 0xFF` is the unsigned read, or declare `[u8]`. `slice_unchecked_get_1/2/4` still
zero-extend.

A STORE IS CHECKED IN EVERY BUILD. `s[i] = v` (new in 6.7.8; it was "cannot subscript 's'") checks i against the
length exactly as a read does — out of range or negative is "slice bounds violation", exit 134, with or without
the bounds-checked mode. `store8(slice_ptr(&s) + i, v)` is the unchecked spelling. `s[i] += 0.5` on an `[f64]` is
refused: the READ is an untyped word, so a float `OP=` would disagree with `s[i] = s[i] + 0.5` — store the
element whole, `s[i] = v`. A closure that captured a slice cannot subscript it (pass `slice_ptr(&s)` /
`slice_len(&s)` in).

A SLICE IS A FN-LOCAL. Pass `&s` to a fn and read `slice_ptr` / `slice_len` there:

    fn f(s: [u8]): i64 { .. }     # error: a slice cannot be a parameter (6.7.8: slices are fn-locals) - pass &s
                                  #        and read slice_ptr / slice_len, or the pointer and the length
    var GS: [u8] = 0;             # in the declaration zone: refused; AFTER the first top-level statement it is a
                                  # 16-byte global used through slice_ptr(&GS) / slice_len(&GS) / slice_set

A return type, a struct field, `*[T]`, a slice of slices and a type argument are refused the same way (each was a
misleading parse error before). An UNTYPED parameter is never a slice: `y[0]` / `y.len` on one compiled only by
accident (a stale width from an earlier fn's slice in the same slot) and are refused since 6.7.8. Gates:
slice_position_refused.sh, slice_signed_read.sh. See CHANGELOG [6.7.8].

6.7.16 — A GENERIC STRUCT INSTANCE IS AN ELEMENT; A SUBSCRIPT READS FIVE WIDTHS (6.7.16's D89; FF-48, FF-48r).
`var e: [Box<i64>] = 0;`, `slice<Box<i64>>` and `[*Box<i64>]` declare, as `[Pt]` does — the element is the instance, its
width the instance's size (they were "expected ']', got '<'"). But `s[i]` reads only 1-, 2-, 4-, 8- or 16-byte elements:
a slice of a 24-byte struct (`[Box<Pt>]`) declares and is refused by name at the subscript — read any other width at
`slice_ptr(&s) + i * sizeof(T)`. CHANGELOG [6.7.16] FF-48, FF-48r.

[[entries]]
name = "struct_handles_and_values_since_6_7_10"
description = "cycc 6.7.10: a struct local or global of ANY size declared from something that is not a struct value of its type is a HANDLE — so `var q: S8 = 0;` is a NULL handle, `var a: Num = 1;` no longer dispatches, and a struct declared from an UNTYPED carrier of a small struct's word (`vec_get`, `load64`, `map_get`, `fncall1`, a `?` payload, `*x`) is a handle to the address that word spells; u8 / u16 / u32 / f32 fields are packed at their widths; `w.s + 1` without `S_add` no longer compiles; an untyped `var c = a + b;` of a small struct is that struct."
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc 6.7.10 (language/features.cyml struct_handle_line_and_struct_values_at_every_size_v6710). Each of these
compiled through 6.7.9 and means something else now — most of them silently.

AN INTEGER INITIALISER IS A HANDLE. `var q: S8 = 0;` is a NULL handle — `q.v = 5` FAULTS (it was a zeroed inline
S8). `var a: Num = 1;` is a handle to address 1, so `a + b` is pointer arithmetic with no `Num_add`. For a zeroed
inline struct write `var q: S8;` or `S8 { 0 }`; for an operator operand declare it from a struct value
(`Num { 1 }`, `mkn(1)`). The same for globals (`var G = S8 { 0 };`).

⚠ AN UNTYPED CARRIER OF A SMALL STRUCT'S WORD IS NOW A HANDLE. A struct of 8 B or less IS one word, so code
carried it in untyped values — and a struct declared from an untyped value is a handle to the address that word
spells:

    vec_push(v, s);                    # s: S8 — pushes s's one word
    var q: S8 = vec_get(v, 0);         # 6.7.9: an inline S8 holding the word (q.v read 4, q.v + 5 gave 9)
                                       # 6.7.10: a handle to ADDRESS 4 — q.v faults (measured SIGSEGV)

The same for `= load64(p)`, `= map_get(m, k)`, `= fncall1(f, x)` / a closure's result, a `?` / pair / `Result` /
`Option` payload (`var q: S8 = mk()?;`) and `= *x` with `x` untyped. Keep such a word untyped; or store the
struct's ADDRESS in the container (`vec_push(v, &s)`) and declare `var q: *S8 = vec_get(v, 0);`; or type the
carrier as a pointer and dereference it (`var p: *S8 = vec_get(v, 0); var q: S8 = *p;` — a dereference of a
`*T`, in any spelling, is a struct value and copies; one of an UNTYPED value is not).

A HANDLE'S WRITES REACH THE OBJECT. `var q: S8 = alloc(8);` (or `= &s`, `= gp()` with `fn gp(): *S8`) holds the
address and `q.v = 5` writes the object; through 6.7.9 q was an inline S8 whose field HELD the address and its
writes stayed in the frame. Code that read `q.v` to get the address back: `load64(&q)`, or keep it untyped. A
declaration from another handle BINDS (`var c: S8 = h;` aliases h's object).

COPIES THROUGH A `*T` AT EVERY SIZE. `l = p` with `p: *S8` copies the struct (it stored the POINTER into `l`'s
first field), and so do `l = GP`, `l = gp()`, `l = w.p` and `l = p + 1` (over 8 B too). Code that used a struct's
first word to carry an address: keep it in a `*S` (`var p: *S = GP;`) or `store64(&l, GP)`. A pointer into a
small BY-VALUE parameter (`bs1(&s)`, `bs1(sp)`) passes the struct now — the callee read the address as its fields.

PACKED NARROW FIELDS. `struct C4 { r: u8; g: u8; b: u8; a: u8; }` is 4 bytes (32 through 6.7.9); u8..u32 read
ZERO-extended and wrap at their width (`c.r = 250; c.r += 10;` is 4). An 8-byte load at a narrow field's offset
(`load64(&s + off)`) reads the NEIGHBOURS too — and `&s.f` itself is not an expression (refused by name since 6.7.13,
D3 (b): write `&s + off`, or reach the field through a `*T`); a hand-written offset (`load64(p + 8)` for the second field), a literal size or a wire / file
format written with `sizeof` must be re-checked. Packed is UNALIGNED: a u32 after a u8 sits at offset 1 — an
atomic or futex word needs its natural alignment (declare it first or pad). A struct that shrinks to 8 B or less
joins the one-register by-value class: refusals keyed on "over 8 B" stop applying, and `dyn` from one whose
method takes `self: T` is newly refused.

A FIELD OR DEREFERENCE LEFT OPERAND DISPATCHES. `w.s + a`, `*p + a` call `S_add` (they added first words), so
`w.s + 1` on a struct-typed field with no `S_add` no longer compiles (*refusing to emit binary with 1 reachable
undefined function(s)*): read the word explicitly — `w.s.v + 1` (`&w.s` is no expression); for a dereference
`p.v + 1` or `load64(p) + 1`. A `*T` field (`w.p + 1` steps it), a `Str` field and `-w.s + a` are unchanged.
`takeh(*pu)` with a `*U` of another struct no longer compiles (it crashed).

AN UNTYPED BINDING OF A SMALL STRUCT'S OPERATOR RESULT IS THAT STRUCT. `var c = a + b;` with `S_add` returning a
struct of 8 B or less declares `c` that struct (it was an untyped word): `c + 1`, `c * 2` now DISPATCH `S_add` /
`S_mul` (or are refused when there is none) — read the field (`c.v + 1`) or `load64(&c)`; `c & 0xFF`, `c | m`,
`c ^ 1`, `c << 1`, `c % n` still act on the word. `var c = mk8(4) * b;` takes `S8_mul`'s return type, not mk8's.
An integer-returning operator and an untyped GLOBAL are unchanged.

See CHANGELOG [6.7.10] and docs/development/ecosystem-migration.md § 6.7.10.

[[entries]]
name = "multi_value_returns_and_struct_words_since_6_7_11"
description = "cycc 6.7.11 (the returns lane): a 9-16 B struct's two words are its return registers and bind as two names; binding fewer names than an undeclared fn returns is refused; a fn declared `: (T1, T2)` must `return (a, b);`; a multi-value call is never a struct; a `: stack` pair as a plain argument is its TAG; `var x: i64 = mkp();` from a big struct is refused; struct literals are a declaration-initialiser form only; an async fn returns one i64."
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc 6.7.11 (language/features.cyml ret2_rethi § SINCE 6.7.11). Each of these compiled through 6.7.10 and read a
stale register, a wrong address or the tag.

A STRUCT'S WORDS ARE ITS RETURN REGISTERS. `var a, b = f();` from a 9-16 B struct gives its two words; three names,
a struct over 16 B (it comes back through a hidden pointer — there is no second word), or two names from an 8 B
struct / `Str` are refused. Bind the struct and read its fields.

BIND EVERY VALUE. Binding FEWER names than an undeclared fn returns is refused (`var q, r = h3(..)` from a 3-value
`h3` whose every return is `(a, b, c)`), as with a declared return type.

IN A DECLARED MULTI-VALUE FN, WRITE `return (a, b);`. `return x?;`, `return (5);`, `return;`, `return p;` of a struct
and `return one(x);` are refused ("'f' returns 2 values - return (a, b)"): they filled only the first register. A
forwarded call of the same arity, a tuple, or a callee the compiler cannot judge (`callptr(fp, ..)`) is fine.

A MULTI-VALUE CALL IS NEVER A STRUCT. `take(mk(..))`, `var p: P = mk(..);`, `h.p = mk(..);`, `p = mk(..);` are refused
at every struct size — `var a, b = mk(..); var p = P { a, b };`. (Into a `*P`, a `Str` or a scalar it is still the
first value.)

A `: stack` PAIR AS A PLAIN ARGUMENT RECEIVES THE TAG (`is_ok(f())`, `g(f())`), and an operand reads the tag — by
design (the user's choice). In a struct-literal element it is refused ("bind both"); `P { f(7)?, 3 }` stores the
payload. A PARENTHESISED pair call (`var v = (f(7));`, `P { (f(7)), 3 }`) escaped every pair check and `(f(7))?`
crashed through 6.7.12; since 6.7.13 the wrap is transparent (6.7.13's D19), and since 6.7.14 `var v = (f(7) + 1);`
is refused "bind both" as unwrapped (6.7.14's D46; it kept the tag) and `var a, b = (f(7));` destructures (6.7.14's
D45) — parser_syntax.cyml parentheses_pair_calls_and_callptr_forms_v6713. A pair method INHERITED from a trait's
default body may sit below its caller since 6.7.13 (it needed the trait and impl ABOVE every caller), and since 6.7.14
so may a fn whose only pair route is a `?` (6.7.14's RC-15).

`var x: i64 = mkp();` FROM A STRUCT OVER 8 B is refused for a free or generic call ("cannot initialise 'x: i64' from
'mkp', which returns the struct 'P2' - declare 'x: P2' or bind a field"); since 6.7.13 a METHOD or OPERATOR call into
a scalar annotation is refused the same way (D13 — it took the first word, narrowed); bind a field explicitly
(`mkp().a`, `b.mk().a`). Since 6.7.14 the ASSIGNMENT `x = mkp();` into a non-struct annotation is refused the same
way, "cannot assign 'x: i64' from 'mkp', …" (6.7.14's D27). `var x = (mk());` is a struct (over 8 B), and since 6.7.13
so is `var x = (mk1());` with an 8 B struct (it was an untyped word) — struct_values_and_receives_since_6_7_13.

STRUCT LITERALS ARE A DECLARATION-INITIALISER FORM ONLY: `take(P { .. })`, `return P { .. };`, `l = P { .. };` and a
top-level `q = P { .. };` were "undefined variable 'P'" plus up to four invented errors through 6.7.13; since 6.7.14 each
is ONE error at the struct's name (6.7.14's D12 (a)): "a struct literal is an initializer: declare `var t: P = P
{ .. };` and assign or pass t" — an assignment, an argument, an operand, a nested element, `return`, a statement, a
generic head (`Box<i64> { .. }`). Bind the literal first. Parentheses around the WHOLE initializer are transparent:
`var q: P = (P { 5, 6 });` is the literal (it was two errors). CHANGELOG [6.7.14] FE-24.

AN `async fn` RETURNS ONE i64: for several values return a pointer to a struct (or a heap block), or split the fn —
a multi-value async fn is refused by name.

See CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (RET-1 .. RET-10) and docs/development/ecosystem-migration.md.

[[entries]]
name = "call_arguments_and_fn_pointer_results_since_6_7_11"
description = "cycc 6.7.11 (the call-arguments lane): a call through a fn pointer knows its callee only when written `&f`; a `p: *f64v2` parameter is a pointer; a struct POINTER converts into any struct parameter but its DEREFERENCE does not; an integer constant into an f64 / f32 argument keeps its bits and warns; `n + p` is the pointer for a one-byte struct pointer; a generic carrier binds T through `(w)` and `*wp`."
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc 6.7.11 (language/features.cyml struct_value_operands_and_parentheses_v676, typed_simd_value_type_abi,
struct_argument_type_checked_v673).

A CALL THROUGH A FN POINTER KNOWS ITS CALLEE ONLY WHEN WRITTEN `&f`. `fncall1(&mk, 5)` / `callptr(&mk, 5)` return the
struct (and the vector) `mk` returns — and since 6.7.13 a parenthesised `(&mk)` too (RC-1). Through a VARIABLE
(`callptr(fp, 5)`, or `callptr((fp), 5)`) the result is one word, and `var p: P3 = callptr(fp, 5);` is a HANDLE to
whatever that word addresses — it faults for a struct returned by value. Give a variable callee an out-pointer, or
receive a 9-16 B struct's two words. Since 6.7.13 the known callee's result also takes `.field` / `.method()`
(`fncall1(&mk3, 5).b`, D16) and is a dyn source (D15); a variable callee followed by `.name` is refused by name.
SINCE 6.7.14 A CALL THROUGH `&f` IS TYPED AS `f(..)` IS, whatever the spelling: a `: f64` callee's result is an f64
whatever its arguments (6.7.14's D25; 6.7.6's last-argument rule is gone — `var a = fncall0(&f); a + 1.5` added
integers), and a `: *T` callee's is a `*T` (6.7.14's D26: `fncall0(&gp) + 1` steps one element, it stepped one byte;
`var w: *P2 = fncall0(&gp);` no longer warns). For byte arithmetic copy it untyped (`var q = fncall0(&gp);`). And
`callptr(&gp, 5).b = 40;` / `+= 40` store through a `: *T` result as statements, as the `fncall1` spelling did (it was
"expected ';', got '='" — 6.7.14's RC-8). A callee held in a variable is still one word.

A `p: *f64v2` PARAMETER IS A POINTER. It was a value-form vector until 6.7.11 (`rd(&b)` refused, `rd(b)` crashed);
now pass `rd(&b)` or `rd(b)` (its address). Since 6.7.12 (D27) a vector VALUE passed to `*V` is an address —
`rd(mkv(..))`, `rd(fncall2(&mkv, ..))`, `rd(*pv)` — and since 6.7.13 a method or explicit-instance result too (VC-5),
`rd(*&b)` (passes `&b`) and `rd(**ppb)` (passes `*ppb`). At top level a vector call result into `*V` is refused (no
frame to hold it). And `var x: i64 = fncall1(&mk, 5);` with a struct over 8 B is refused as the direct call is (D28).
THE VECTOR ARGUMENT RULES AFTER 6.7.13: a vector value goes ONLY to a `*V` of its own type (its address) or to a
value-form parameter of its type (a local of the type or a call declared to return it — D30). Every other pointer
kind (`*Pt` / `*i64` / `*u8` / `**T`), a by-value struct parameter ("a vector is not a struct"), a struct into a
`*V` (D33 — pass `&s`) and a captured vector of the wrong kind are refused by name (D31 – D33). SINCE 6.7.14 a
value-form parameter also takes a closure's CAPTURED vector, a dereference `*p` of a pointer to its type and either
inside parentheses around the whole argument — on every target, Windows included (6.7.14's D31 / D32, its VC-2 –
VC-5); a struct FIELD into a `*V` and every `*V` dereference into another pointer kind are refused by name (6.7.14's
D34, its VC-10) — vector_values_since_6_7_14. (The value-form vector local rule is in vector_value_return_binding_forms.)

A STRUCT POINTER CONVERTS INTO ANY STRUCT PARAMETER (`take8(pu)` copies the pointee); its DEREFERENCE does not —
`take8(*pu)` and `bs1((t))` of ANOTHER struct are refused at every size since 6.7.11: `*pu` and `(t)` are VALUES of
their own struct.

AN INTEGER CONSTANT INTO AN `f64` / `f32` KEEPS ITS BITS (ADR-002): `f(3)` passes a subnormal, about 1.5e-323 — and
since 6.7.11 it WARNS at an argument too (a call, a method, a tail call, `#inline`, a named argument, an operator's
right operand). Write `3.0`, or `f64_from(n)`. (float_builtin_results_are_float_operands_since_6_6_10.)

`n + p` IS THE POINTER `p` FOR A ONE-BYTE `*T` OF ANY KIND, a one-byte STRUCT included (6.7.11): `var r: *B1 = 1 + pb;`
is silent, and `(1 + pb) - q32` is refused as a difference of pointers with different element sizes.

A GENERIC STRUCT CARRIER BINDS T THROUGH `(w)` AND `*wp` as through `w` / `wp` (6.7.11); through 6.7.10 the 8 B
spellings ran the i64 base with the BASE's layout (a negative `i32` field tested non-negative). A tuple literal
`((b, 3))` binds nothing.

A PARAMETER'S TYPE takes the trait-signature grammar (parser_syntax.cyml a_parameter_type_is_read_whole_v6711).
See CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (ARG-1 .. ARG-9).

[[entries]]
name = "struct_copies_and_operator_results_since_6_7_11"
description = "cycc 6.7.11 (the struct lane): `l = *p` and `q = *p` copy; a `*S` destination copies whoever owns the pointer (`GP = l` writes `*GP`); `*p = v` is a struct copy of exactly sizeof(S) bytes; a struct return is checked by its struct and a `*S` returns its pointee; `return (a, b);` is not a `: P`; `n - s` and `-s` are word arithmetic; an untyped binding is the struct of any whole struct result; a captured name is not assignable in a closure."
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc 6.7.11 (language/features.cyml struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711). Each compiled through 6.7.10
and means something else now.

THE DECLARATION AND THE ASSIGNMENT FROM `*p` AGREE — BOTH COPY. `l = *p` copies the struct; `q = *p` into a `*S` writes
q's POINTEE (it used to rebind q to the struct's first word): to load a pointer stored in the first field write
`q = p.first`.

A `*S` DESTINATION COPIES, WHOEVER OWNS THE POINTER. A `*S` local, parameter and (6.7.11) GLOBAL all copy a struct value
into the pointee (`GP = l` writes `*GP`); only `*S` / handle / untyped sources rebind. A struct-typed field, a name and
a declaration take the same `*T` sources (a `*S` global, a `: *S` call, a `*S` field, a generic `: *T` call);
`o.s = GP` used to keep the address.

`*p = v` THROUGH A `*S` IS A STRUCT COPY. It writes exactly sizeof(S) bytes (it wrote 8, overrunning a small struct);
for a raw word use `store64(p, x)`; `*p += 1` is refused — write the field.

A STRUCT RETURN IS CHECKED BY ITS STRUCT, AND A POINTER RETURNS THE STRUCT IT POINTS AT, AT EVERY SIZE. `return u;` of
another struct from an 8 B fn is refused (it returned u's bytes); `return p;` / `return GP;` / `return gp();` with a
`*S` return the POINTEE (from an 8 B fn they returned the address).

A TUPLE LITERAL IS NOT THE STRUCT A FN RETURNS: `return (a, b);` from `: P` is refused; build a `P`
(`var r: P = P { a, b }; return r;`).

`n - s` AND `-s` ON A STRUCT ARE WORD ARITHMETIC WITH AN INTEGER RESULT: `-s + t` adds integers (it called `S_add` with
the minus dropped); `-` never dispatches; `var r: S = -s - t;` is a handle holding an integer.

AN UNTYPED BINDING IS THE STRUCT OF ANY WHOLE STRUCT RESULT — operator, method, chain, if-expression, wrapped — so
`c + 1` dispatches `S_add` (or is refused); write `c.v + 1`, or annotate `: i64` to keep the word. A leading call types
it only when it is the WHOLE initialiser (`var r = mk3(4) - s;` with an integer `P3_sub` is untyped).

A CLOSURE CAPTURES BY VALUE AND A CAPTURED NAME IS NOT ASSIGNABLE IN IT — copy it into a local (`var y = x;`); the
message now says so ("'x' is captured by value; a captured name is not assignable in a closure" — it said *undefined
variable*). A captured struct's FIELD (`h.n += d`) is the closure's own copy. (Since 6.7.13 the fix-it reads
`var x_l = x;`; since 6.7.14 the write is refused even when a top-level global has the same name — through 6.7.13 the
global silently took it, 6.7.14's RC-1.)

`T_from_json_str` (the scanner) decodes a NESTED derived struct since 6.7.11 (it lost every field after one), and since
6.7.13 a SCALAR field holding an object, an array or a mismatched string is walked whole (ST-4) — the scanner and the
pairs form agree.
See CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (ST-1 .. ST-21).

[[entries]]
name = "generics_and_the_frontend_since_6_7_11"
description = "cycc 6.7.11 (the generics + frontend lane): a top-level `return` is an error; `Vec<T>`'s element is checked; a struct named `slice` takes the word everywhere; a generic method called with a scalar where its body uses T as a struct is refused; a captured slice is a copy; `await f` is a plain word; the overflow operators are integer-only on a pointer; a narrow local is an integer; a `private` const fn is private in a const context; on cx, `fn main` runs."
category = "cyrius"
---
cycc 6.7.11 (language/features.cyml generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310, slice_type_v58x, bare_return_in_fn,
typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617, const_and_const_fn_v672, auto_call_main_v59x).

A TOP-LEVEL `return` IS AN ERROR (`return outside a fn body`): exit with `syscall(60, v)`, fall off the end, or return
from `fn main`. Before 6.7.11 it compiled and did not return (x86_64 / PE ran on past it; aarch64 / cx hung).

`Vec<T>`'S ELEMENT IS CHECKED: through 6.7.10 a `var v: Vec<T>` / parameter / `sizeof(Vec<T>)` element was unread text
(`Vec<nosuchtype>` compiled); now it must be a type the field form takes. A POINTER element is refused — write
`Vec<i64>` (the handle holds words either way).

A STRUCT NAMED `slice` TAKES THE WORD EVERYWHERE: with your own `struct slice` declared, spell the built-in slice `[T]`
— `var s: slice<u8>` is your struct and has no `.len`.

A GENERIC METHOD CALLED WITH A SCALAR WHERE ITS BODY USES T AS A STRUCT IS REFUSED, as the free call always was:
`p.pick(9)` returned 0 silently through 6.7.10. (generic_using_t_as_a_struct_has_no_i64_instance.)

IN A CLOSURE, A CAPTURED SLICE IS A COPY: `s.len` / `s.ptr` and `slice_len(&s)` / `slice_ptr(&s)` read it; `s[i]` and a
store into `s.len` / `s.ptr` are refused by name. Declare the slice in the closure to subscript or re-point it.
AN UNTYPED NAME COPY OF A STRUCT VALUE IS A COPY (6.7.12, D29): `var q = p;` binds `p`'s struct — `q.y` works in a fn and
in a closure, `take(q)` passes it, and `q + n` now DISPATCHES `P_add` (refused when there is none) where it added to the
first word. A `*T` or handle source and an untyped global stay words; since 6.7.13 (D6 (b)) an INLINE struct global
named in a fn is copied too (`var q = G;`) — at top level it is still the word. A bare `q` value (`return q;`,
`q == 3`) is still the first word.

THE VALUE OF `await f` IS A PLAIN WORD whatever `f` is declared.

THE OVERFLOW OPERATORS ARE INTEGER-ONLY ON A POINTER: `(q +| 1) + 1` with `q: *i32` is `q` + 2 bytes (it was + 5); use
`p + n` for a step.

A NARROW LOCAL (`u8` .. `i32`) IS AN INTEGER, and `n + &x` / `&x + n` are both byte pointers: `w + p` steps
`w * sizeof(T)`, `var r: *T = w;` warns, `w - q` compiles.

A `private` CONST FN IS PRIVATE IN A CONST CONTEXT TOO: `const X = pk();` from another file is refused, as the run-time
call always was — since 6.7.14 in a const context's SKIPPED arm too (a short-circuit's right operand, an
if-expression's untaken arm: 6.7.14's FE-4).

ON cx, `fn main` RUNS: a cx program that defines `main` and also calls it at top level without an explicit exit runs it
TWICE, as on every other target (fn_main_is_not_the_entry_point).

AN EXPLICIT INSTANCE IS AS GOOD AS AN INFERRED ONE FOR A VECTOR RESULT (6.7.13, VC-3): `var v: f64v2 = mkg<i64>(..);`
receives the vector, as `mkg(..)` does — through 6.7.12 the declaration silently dropped its annotation and `v` was one
word.

6.7.14's generics changes (type arguments, signatures, `p.m<T>(x)`, what a pointer binds, one error per mistake) are in
generics_since_6_7_14.
See CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (GF-1 .. GF-20).

[[entries]]
name = "a_write_length_is_bytes_not_characters_v6711"
description = "A `sys_write` / `syscall(SYS_WRITE, ..)` length is the literal's BYTES as the lexer stores them, not its characters: `\"a — b\\n\"` is 8, `\"\\x1b[0m\"` is 4, `\"é\"` is 2. One short silently drops the last byte; one long writes the bytes after the literal. cyrlint flags a wrong literal length since 6.7.11."
category = "cyrius"
---
    sys_write(1, "a — b\n", 8);      # the em dash is 3 UTF-8 bytes: 8, not 6
    sys_write(1, "\x1b[0m", 4);      # \xHH is ONE byte: 4, not 7
    sys_write(1, "é", 2);

Count bytes as `src/frontend/lex.cyr`'s string arm STORES them: a raw byte is one byte (never decoded — an em dash is
3), a two-character escape (`\n`, `\t`, …) one byte, `\xHH` one byte, `\u####` / `\u{…}` its UTF-8 length. A length one SHORT
silently drops the last byte (the shadow_pam SKIP line lost its `s\n`); one LONG writes whatever follows the literal.
Since 6.7.11 `cyrius lint` warns on a `syscall(SYS_WRITE|1, …)` / `sys_write` / `_aw_write` whose literal length is
wrong (`#skip-lint` silences it). Prefer a length the compiler computes (`strlen`, a `Str`) wherever the literal can
change. CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (TL-3).

[[entries]]
name = "a_same_value_redeclaration_is_silent_by_design_v6712"
description = "Two libraries that pick the same private-looking global name with the SAME initial value share ONE global with no diagnostic (the 6.6.6 two-files-one-global rule); since 6.7.12 a differing value warns even when the first was `= 0` (D30), since 6.7.14 a later value that folds to another constant (`= 2 + 1`, `= -5`) warns too, while integer vs `bool` is an error, and since 6.7.15 a const- or enum-NAMED constant of another value warns as well. Only a census sees the same-value case — give module-private globals a module prefix."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "v6.7.14", "v6.7.15", "globals", "redeclaration", "duplicate symbol", "last definition wins", "folds", "const", "enum", "D30", "bool", "D9"]
---
    # a.cyr                      # b.cyr
    var _shared = 0;             var _shared = 5;    # 6.7.12: warns "duplicate symbol '_shared' redefined with
                                                     #   conflicting value (last definition wins)"; silent before
    var _err = 0;                var _err = 0;       # silent BY DESIGN: one global, both files write it

A top-level `var` repeated across files is ONE global (the 6.6.6 rule — "last definition wins"). Since 6.7.12 a
DIFFERING constant value warns even when the first was `= 0` (D30; through 6.7.11 a recorded 0 read as "no constant",
so the pair was silent). A SAME value stays silent: two libraries that pick the same private-looking name with the same
initial value share one cell, and each sees the other's writes. Only a census sees that case — cyrius's own folds are
held to it by `fold_namespace_collisions.sh` axis 4 (sankoch's `_bre_err` was niyama's global until sankoch 2.8.7). In a
consumer, give module-private globals a module prefix. Since 6.7.14 a later initializer that FOLDS to a different
constant warns too, as an exact `= NUM ;` always did: `var a = 5; var a = 2 + 1;`, `= -5`, `= (7)`, and `true` after
`false` (they fold to 1 / 0) — a warning only, the value and the exit code unchanged (FE-18). Not compared: a computed
prior. A NAMED constant on either side (`var a = 3; var a = C;`, `var a = C; var a = 3;`, `= E.B`, `var a = 0; var a
= C;`) was silent through 6.7.14 and warns since 6.7.15 (6.7.15's FE-14), once, at the later declaration; a chain is
compared pair by pair, each later constant with the last one before it (`3`, `C`, `4` warns twice). Which value the
global HAS between two such declarations is a_zone_redeclaration_is_its_last_constant_from_program_start_v6715. And
since 6.7.14 a global declared once as an integer (untyped, an integer type, a destructure target) and once as `bool` is
an ERROR, "global 'G' redeclared with a different type or size (a global has one definition; rename one)" (6.7.14's D9
(a), FE-19: `var G = 5; var G: bool = G;` built a bool holding 5); two `bool` declarations still fold. Since 6.7.15 that
refusal is the one error (6.7.15's FE-12): a reader between the two declarations reads the FIRST one, and the mirror
`var G = 5; var X: bool = G; var G: bool = true;` also reports X's own error (X reads the integer G, as the source
says).
language/agents.cyml § NO `var x` REDECL; field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
vector_addresses_typed_copies_and_zero_as_none_v6712 #4. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] FX-6, [6.7.14] FE-18, FE-19, [6.7.15]
FE-12, FE-14.

[[entries]]
name = "a_stack_enum_cannot_be_its_own_fns_return_annotation_v6712"
description = "`fn f(x): E { return Va(x); }` with the file's own `enum E: stack` is refused (`type 'E' cannot be a fn return type`) — annotate `: Tagged` (which compiles and returns the pair) or nothing. And whatever a fn's annotation says, `return Ok(x);` makes it return TWO values: `fn f(x): i64 { return Ok(x); }` refuses `var z = f(3);` ('bind both')."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", ": stack", "enum", "Result", "Tagged", "return type", "pair", "cyrius header", "D23"]
---
    enum E: stack { Va(v); Vb(); }
    fn f(x): E { return Va(x); }          # refused: type 'E' cannot be a fn return type
    fn g(x): Tagged { return Va(x); }     # compiles; returns the (tag, payload) pair
    fn h(x): i64 { return Ok(x); }        # compiles AND returns two values — the annotation does not narrow it
    var z = h(3);                         # refused: "bind both" (var t, v = h(3);)

A `: stack` enum's own name is not in the return-type set; `: Tagged` (or `: Result` / `: Option`, or no annotation)
is. Pair-ness propagates through a fn's body whatever its annotation says, so a `: i64` on a fn that returns `Ok(x)` is
a promise the compiler does not enforce at the definition — it surfaces at the caller's single bind. `cyrius header`
refuses such a PUBLIC fn by name since 6.7.12 (D23 — TL-22), so a C caller is never told it gets a word. On aarch64 the
pair is x0:x2, not AAPCS64's x0:x1 (field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml tools_lane_signals_pairs_paths_and_the_lock_v6712 #2).
CHANGELOG [6.7.12] TL-22.

[[entries]]
name = "struct_values_and_receives_since_6_7_13"
description = "cycc 6.7.13 (the structs and receives + closures lanes): `&h` of a handle is its slot; `&x.f` is not an expression; an untyped name copy is a struct copy for every struct value, an inline global in a fn included; an inline `Str` binds, it does not copy; a struct value is never a word pointer; `*pp + n` steps elements; a bare `Vec` field depends on where your `struct Vec` is; `{ *p }` copies only at 8 B or less; `gp().n = 5;` stores; integer-result operators type their own result; parentheses are transparent at four more places; D24 holds for the declaration, not the assignment; the typed-pointer warning is everywhere and quiet on 0; closures read the enclosing name in their own declaration. Each compiled through 6.7.12 and means something else now; most of what was filed is fixed in 6.7.14 (struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_14, receives_and_multi_value_since_6_7_14)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.13", "v6.7.14", "v6.7.16", "structs", "handle", "&x.f", "Str", "**T", "Vec", "if expression", "6.7.11 D24", "receives", "parentheses", "typed pointer", "integer operators", "+|", "closures", "capture", "D104"]
---
cycc 6.7.13 (language/features.cyml struct_handle_line_and_struct_values_at_every_size_v6710,
struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711, struct_value_operands_and_parentheses_v676, closures_v637_v638,
typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617, struct_parameter_is_a_copy_v6616; language/core.cyml type_names_v6616).

`&h` OF A HANDLE (AND OF A `*T`) IS ITS SLOT. `*(&h)` is the slot's word; only an INLINE struct's `&x` is the struct
(`*(&x)`, `return &x;`, `bs(&x)`). `return &h;` of a handle in a `: S` fn returned the slot's address through 6.7.13;
since 6.7.14 it is refused by name at every size, for a handle, a `*T` or an untyped `self` (6.7.14's D19) — write
`return h;`. (ST-5, ST-10.)

`&x.f` AND `&a[i]` ARE NOT EXPRESSIONS. The address of a field is `&x + offset`, or reach the field through a `*T`
(`var p: *S = &x; p.f`). (D3 (b), ST-8.)

AN UNTYPED NAME COPY IS A STRUCT COPY FOR EVERY STRUCT VALUE — a local, a by-value parameter, a capture and, since
6.7.13, an INLINE GLOBAL named in a fn (`var q = G;` copies G) — but a plain WORD for an address holder (a handle, a
`*T`, an untyped `self`) and at top level. (D6 (b), ST-13.)

AN INLINE `Str` BINDS, IT DOES NOT COPY. With `var t: Str = Str { .. }`, `var h: Str = t;` / `var h = t;` / `h = t`
make h a second handle to t's HEADER — a write through h changes t; to copy, `Str { t.data, t.len }` or
`str_new(t.data, t.len)`. `f(t)` into a `s: Str` parameter now passes the header's address (it passed the data
pointer, read as a header). Never return or store a handle bound to a frame's inline `Str` (`return t;` itself is
refused "would dangle"; a handle bound to t is `&t` and dangles with the frame — documented, not refused, 6.7.14's D23
(b)). Since 6.7.14 a bare `var t: Str;` is an inline `Str` too (6.7.14's D20 (a); it was the old pun), and `h.eq(t)`
reads t (the stdlib's `Str` methods take a `Str`, 6.7.14's D18 (c)); the UNTYPED positions (`fncallN`, `callptr`,
`vec_push`, a tuple element) still take the data word — pass `&t` there. (D7 (1), ST-14;
struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_14.)

A STRUCT VALUE IS NEVER A WORD POINTER. `ri(s)` into `*i64` / `*u8` / `**T` is refused — pass `&s`. A handle, a `*T`
and a `Str` already hold the address. Since 6.7.14 a struct-typed FIELD, a by-value call, a method / operator result
and a `*S` / `**S` dereference are refused too, "… - a struct value is no address" (6.7.14's ST-8). (D5 (a), ST-12.)

`*pp + n` STEPS ELEMENTS NOW. With `pp: **T`, `*pp` is a `*T`: `*pp + 1` is the next element, `**pp` on a narrow T is
the element at its width, `*pp - &x` is refused (different element sizes) — copy untyped (`var a = *pp;`) for byte
arithmetic. A program may name at most 16383 pointee types of a `**` since 6.7.14 (126 in 6.7.13; each struct
counts — 6.7.14's VC-1). (D9 (a), ST-19.)

A BARE `Vec` FIELD DEPENDS ON WHERE YOUR `struct Vec` IS — above the field: that struct; below: refused; none (or an
enum): lib's 8-byte handle. Write `Vec<T>` to mean lib's handle explicitly. (D10 (b), ST-16.)

`if (c) { *p } else { *q }` COPIES THE STRUCT AT 8 B OR LESS; over 8 B it is refused — branch on the pointers (`if (c)
{ p } else { q }`) and copy from the result. `(*ps, ..)` is not a tuple element — use `ps.a` or `ps`. (D1 (a), ST-6;
D2 (a), ST-7.)

`gp().n = 5;` STORES THROUGH A `: *T` CALL'S POINTER (and `gp().m += 5;` calls once); a by-value call's field is still
a temporary. Through a fn pointer: `fncall1(&gp, 5).b = 40;` stores, and since 6.7.14 so does `callptr(&gp, 5).b =
40;` (it was "expected ';', got '='" — 6.7.14's RC-8) — but a callee held in a VARIABLE has no fields: its result is
one word. (D8 (a), ST-15; D16.)

AN UNTYPED VALUE THROUGH A `*S` IS THE STRUCT'S WORD, AT MOST 8 BYTES: `*p = 0` on a 16-byte struct clears the first 8
only (D11 (a), ST-18; unchanged, now confirmed).

AN INTEGER-RESULT OPERATOR TYPES ITS OWN RESULT. Binary-operator typing follows the LEFT operand, but a comparison,
`&&` / `||` and `& | ^ % << >> >>>` give an integer whatever their right operand: `(n < x) * 3` is 3 with `x: f64`
(it was an f64 multiply). `+ - * /` with an f64 right operand stay f64-typed (kind 2) — `(n % x) * 2.0` is an integer
`%` then a kind-2 mix; write the float side with `f64_from`. A leading `: f64` call types an untyped binding only
while the whole initialiser stays f64: `var c = f() < 1.0;` is 0 / 1 (it was f64, so `-c` flipped the sign bit).
(D12 (a), RC-4, RC-5.) Since 6.7.16 the saturating / checked `+| +? -| -? *| *?` are integer operators too (6.7.16's
D104, FB-4): their result is an integer whatever their right operand — `(n +| x) * 3` with `x: f64` is an integer
multiply (it took the f64 arm). There is no saturating f64 arithmetic: use `+ - * /` on f64 operands for float results.

PARENTHESES ARE TRANSPARENT AT FOUR MORE PLACES: a `&f` callee (`callptr((&mk), ..)` is the known callee); an untyped
8 B receive (`var x = (mk1());` is the struct — so `x + 1` now dispatches `P1_add`, and is refused without one;
annotate `: i64` for the word); a 9-16 B method / operator literal element; a dyn literal element. The four shapes
filed here are fixed in 6.7.14: in a fn `var s = (mks());` with `: Str` is a `Str`, `var a = (f()) * 2.0;` is an f64,
`(s).a` / `(mk1()).a` read the field (6.7.14's D24) and `HE { 7, (q0), 8 }` is the element. A TOP-LEVEL `var s =
(mks());` stayed a word the overloads did not route through 6.7.14; since 6.7.15 it is routed as `var s = mks();` is
(`println(s)` reaches `println_str`; 6.7.15's RC-4). (RC-1, RC-2, RC-3, RC-10.)

6.7.11's D24 HOLDS FOR THE DECLARATION, NOT THE ASSIGNMENT. `var x: i64 = b.mk();` / `= a + c;` / `= mkp();` over 8 B is
refused (in a fn and at top level); the ASSIGNMENT `x = b.mk();` into a scalar stored the struct's first word
silently through 6.7.13, and is refused with the same words since 6.7.14 (6.7.14's D27) — write the field (`x =
b.mk().a;`). `var p: *T = f();` of a by-value struct over 8 B is refused in a fn, and since 6.7.14 at top level too, in
both zones (6.7.14's D28; it compiled under "assigning non-pointer to typed pointer" and faulted at `p.b`) — declare
the struct and take `&`. An UNTYPED destination (`var x = 0; x = mkp();`) and a `*T` local of another type took the
first word through 6.7.14; both are refused by name since 6.7.15 (6.7.15's D32 / D33; receives_since_6_7_15). (D13,
D14, ST-11, RC-6, RC-7.)

THE TYPED-POINTER WARNING IS EVERYWHERE NOW, AND QUIET ON `0`. Expect it in the declaration zone and on `p = x;` into a
scalar `*T` local or parameter; `= 0` is a null pointer and never warns (`0 - 0` and `(0)` do). A string literal into a
`*u8` warned too, wrongly, in 6.7.13; since 6.7.14 a string literal alone into a `*u8` / `*i8` never warns (6.7.14's
D53). (D18 (a), RC-11.) SINCE 6.7.16: `(0)` / `((0))` is the null pointer too (FE-17; `(0) + 0`, `(1)` still warn); a
`: Str` call, a `: Str` field or name, and either wrapped whole, into a `*Str` declaration, global or local are quiet
(FE-18, FF-22, SS-12: each holds a header's address); and two sites newly warn — an assignment into a `*T` local or
parameter whose value is another struct's call or a word (`p = str_from(..)` into a `*P` bound a `Str` header to a `*P`
silently; FF-21), and a classic-for step `p = 7` into a `*T` (FF-24). A handle (`var a: P3`) takes any struct call
quietly — that is a rebind. Warnings only: no binary changes.

CLOSURES: a captured NARROW local is its value (a captured `i8` -3 reads -3, not 253); `var a = a + n;` in a closure
reads the ENCLOSING `a` (and needs lib/alloc.cyr, as any capture); a nested literal's parameters are its own; the
captured-name refusal's fix-it reads `var x_l = x;`. With a same-named GLOBAL, `y += d;` on a captured `y` compiled
and wrote the GLOBAL in 6.7.13; since 6.7.14 it is refused as any write to a capture is (6.7.14's RC-1). (D35 A,
RC-12 .. RC-15.)
See CHANGELOG [6.7.13] (ST-1 .. ST-20, RC-1 .. RC-15) and docs/development/ecosystem-migration.md § 6.7.13.

[[entries]]
name = "multi_value_returns_since_6_7_13"
description = "cycc 6.7.13 (the multi-value lane): `ret2(a, b)` is not an escape hatch from the return rules — it is refused where `return (a, b);` is (in a vector fn too since 6.7.14); a declared multi-value fn that runs off its end returns 0s, silently; a fn declared ONE type may return two values, but say `: (T1, T2)`; a tuple parameter takes a tuple, or an untyped word as its address."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.13", "v6.7.14", "v6.7.15", "multi-value", "ret2", "fall-off", "D20", "D21", "D22", "D23", "D28", "tuple parameter"]
---
cycc 6.7.13 (language/features.cyml ret2_rethi, struct_byval_int_pair_return_abi, tuples_v677).

`ret2` IS NOT AN ESCAPE HATCH FROM THE RETURN RULES. `ret2(a, b)` is `return (a, b);` spelled as a builtin, and it is
refused where the literal is: OUTSIDE a fn (it never returned — x86_64 / PE ran on past it, aarch64 / cx hung), in a
THREE-value fn (the third value was stale), in a STRUCT fn of any size (0s over 16 B, the second value dropped at 8 B,
right words only by coincidence at 9-16 B) and in an async fn. A struct fn builds the struct and returns it: `var p: S
= S { a, b }; return p;`. In a VECTOR-returning fn it compiled and returned no vector in 6.7.13; since 6.7.14 it is
refused by the vector-return text, "… — got `ret2`" (6.7.14's D43). (D20 – D22.)

A DECLARED MULTI-VALUE FN THAT RUNS OFF ITS END RETURNS 0s, SILENTLY. `fn f(x): (i64, i64) { if (x > 0) { return (1,
2); } }` gives `f(0)` = (0, 0) — the pair to a bool fn's `false`. There is no diagnostic for the missing `return`. If 0
is a valid value, return explicitly on every path. (D28 C; before 6.7.13 the caller read stale registers.)

A FN DECLARED ONE TYPE MAY RETURN TWO VALUES — BUT SAY `: (T1, T2)`. `fn g2(): i64 { return (1, 2); }` compiles. Each
value takes the declared type (`: f64` makes both f64), and `var a = g2();` silently keeps the first. With `ret2(..)`
in the body the value count was not checked in 6.7.13 (`var a, b, c = g2();` read a stale third value); since 6.7.14 a
fn left only through `ret2(..)` / `return (a, b);` is two values to every count, and three names, a 3-tuple or a
struct argument are refused (6.7.14's D42). Declaring `: (T1, T2)` names both types and checks every return and every
bind. (D23 B, kept by the user.)

A TUPLE PARAMETER TAKES A TUPLE. A multi-value call into it is refused "bind it first", for a declared or a counted
callee (6.7.13, MV-1). In 6.7.13 any other word was passed as the tuple's ADDRESS and crashed with no diagnostic; since
6.7.14 (6.7.14's D44) an integer literal (`take(5)`) is refused "cannot pass an integer to tuple parameter …" and a
scalar-declared fn returning two values by the "bind it first" words, while an untyped word is the tuple's address BY
DESIGN (a tuple has no `*T` spelling: `take(&t)` works) and a one-value call (`take(one(3))`) is that word — your
address, or a crash. `take(-5)` / `take(true)` compiled (and crashed) through 6.7.14; since 6.7.15 an argument made
only of literals and operators is refused by name (6.7.15's D36, RC-3). Build or bind a tuple variable and pass that.
See CHANGELOG [6.7.13] (MV-1 .. MV-19).

[[entries]]
name = "vector_pointers_step_vectors_and_windows_value_form_v6713"
description = "cycc 6.7.13 (the vectors lane): `p + n` on a `*f64v2` steps n VECTORS; `&b + 8` is lane 1 but `*&b` is the vector; on Windows a value-form vector parameter is passed by address, so `lanes(w)` is the one spelling every target takes (since 6.7.14 every target takes the same sources); a captured vector is a local for a value-form parameter since 6.7.14; a struct named like a vector type is the struct everywhere since 6.7.14; a `v: f32v8` parameter now arrives in two vector registers; a missing fn in a vector initialiser is reported as missing."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.13", "v6.7.14", "SIMD", "vectors", "f64v2", "f32v8", "*V", "value-form", "Windows", "Win64", "closures", "D30", "D32"]
---
cycc 6.7.13 (language/features.cyml typed_simd_value_type_abi, f32v8_avx2_256bit_simd, closures_v637_v638).

`p + n` ON A `*f64v2` STEPS n VECTORS. `load64(p + 8)` is 128 bytes on, not lane 1 — read lane 1 with `var q = p;
load64(q + 8)` (an untyped copy steps bytes) or `load64(&b + 8)`. (The 6.7.13 triage itself filed this as a
wrong-address bug in closures; it is the typed-pointer step, by design.)

`&b + 8` IS LANE 1, BUT `*&b` IS THE VECTOR. `&x` is a byte pointer (6.7.11); a dereference of it names `b`, so
`rd(*&b)` passes `&b` and `var v: f64v2 = *&w;` copies `w`. (VC-11.)

ON WINDOWS A VALUE-FORM VECTOR PARAMETER IS PASSED BY ADDRESS. That is why, in 6.7.13, `lanes(&w)`, a parenthesised
argument and a struct local compiled there and were refused elsewhere, and why a pointer word (`lanes(pv)`) used to
RUN there — it is refused since 6.7.13 (kept by the user, 6.7.14's D51). Since 6.7.14 every target takes the same
sources: `lanes(&w)` and a struct local are refused on Windows too (6.7.14's D33), and a parenthesised argument passes
everywhere. `lanes(w)` remains the one spelling to write. (D30, VC-6; 6.7.14's VC-2, VC-3, VC-6.)

INSIDE A CLOSURE, A CAPTURED VECTOR WAS NOT A LOCAL FOR A VALUE-FORM PARAMETER in 6.7.13: `|k| lanes(b)` was refused on
every target (it ran on Windows through 6.7.12). Since 6.7.14 it passes as the local does (6.7.14's D31) — the copy
(`var w: f64v2 = b; lanes(w)`) is no longer needed; a capture of another class is refused with the local's words. Into
a `*V` of its type a capture passes the closure's COPY — a write through it does not reach the enclosing local. (D32,
VC-8; 6.7.14's VC-4.)

A STRUCT NAMED LIKE A VECTOR TYPE (`struct f32v8 { .. }`) was the vector as a parameter type and a struct as a local
in 6.7.13. Since 6.7.14 it is that struct EVERYWHERE in the program — a by-value parameter, and `lib/simd.cyr`'s fns of
that name (6.7.14's D41: a vector type's name is not reserved). Keep the old advice only for a program that also wants
`lib/simd.cyr`'s vector of that name: then do not name a struct after `f64v2` / `f64v4` / `f32v4` / `f32v8`.

A `v: f32v8` PARAMETER FROM C now arrives in two vector registers (by pointer on Win64), like `f64v4`; through 6.7.12
it was one integer register (lane 0's bits; every read past it 0). (VC-2.)

A MISSING FN IN A VECTOR INITIALISER IS REPORTED AS MISSING (`undefined function 'f64v2_splat'` — add `include
"lib/simd.cyr"`); through 6.7.12 the vector refusal named it a refused source first. Wrapped, in a `return` or as a
value-form argument it still did in 6.7.13; since 6.7.14 the missing fn is named at every vector position (6.7.14's
VC-7). (VC-12.)
See CHANGELOG [6.7.13] (VC-1 .. VC-12) and field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
vector_values_receives_arguments_and_dereferences_v6713; 6.7.14's vector changes: vector_values_since_6_7_14.

[[entries]]
name = "generics_since_6_7_14"
description = "cycc 6.7.14 (the generics lane): a type argument is a TYPE — never a type parameter given arguments, an unknown `Name<..>`, the built-in `slice<..>`, a number or `u128`; a two-parameter instance name can carry brackets; a signature's type arguments are checked even when nothing calls the fn; every `Vec<..>` is checked; `p.m<T>(x)` is `P_m<T>(&p, x)`; the parameter's shape decides what a pointer argument binds; a forward to a bounded generic without an i64 impl compiles at its other types; a `: T` generic is not a handle source; one error per mistake in a generic body. Cross-linked from generics_and_the_frontend_since_6_7_11."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "generics", "type arguments", "instance names", "signatures", "Vec", "method type arguments", "*T", "trait bounds", "diagnostics", "D1", "D2", "D3", "D4", "D5", "D6", "D7", "D8"]
---
cycc 6.7.14 (language/features.cyml generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310, nested_generic_type_arguments_v6617,
trait_bounds_v671, slice_type_v58x). The decisions are 6.7.14's (the user, 2026-10-16). The 6.7.11 – 6.7.13 generics
notes are in generics_and_the_frontend_since_6_7_11 and generic_using_t_as_a_struct_has_no_i64_instance.

A TYPE ARGUMENT IS A TYPE, NEVER A TYPE CONSTRUCTOR OR A VALUE (D2 (a), D3 (a); GN-7, GN-9). It is a struct, i8 .. i64,
f64, a type parameter in scope, or a generic struct instance `G<..>`. Everything else is refused once, at its token:

    g<Nonexist<u8>>(0)       # unknown type 'Nonexist' as a type argument (...)
    h<T<u8>>(0)              # (in a generic) a type parameter takes no type arguments: 'T'
    g<slice<u8>>(0)          # a slice cannot be a type argument (6.7.8: slices are fn-locals)
    var b: Box<1 + 2>;       # a type argument is a type name, got number 1
    id<u128>(3)              # u128 cannot be a type argument (a type argument is a struct, i8/i16/i32/i64, f64, or ...)

Through 6.7.13 the spelling with arguments fell through to the i64 base silently (`g<Nonexist<u8>>(0)` ran, exit 11),
and a number whose value happened to equal a type name's offset in the name pool compiled as that type
(`sizeof(Box<7>)` was 32). A refused explicit argument mints no instance, and the call's arguments are not judged
against one (GN-8). A program's own `struct slice<T>` still takes the word. A NON-generic struct or a scalar given
arguments (`Pt<i64>`) was accepted with the arguments ignored through 6.7.14; since 6.7.15 it is refused at the name
(6.7.15's D6, GN-3), and in the positions 6.7.15 left since 6.7.16 (6.7.16's D7: generics_since_6_7_16).

TWO-PARAMETER INSTANCE NAMES CAN CARRY BRACKETS (D1 (a), GN-1). `g2<P<R>, i32>` is the instance `g2$<P$R>$i32` and
`g2<P, R<i32>>` is `g2$P$<R$i32>` — through 6.7.13 both were `g2$P$R$i32`, and the second call silently ran the first's
body. These are the names the coverage dump and `cyrius syms` show: a script that greps instance names must allow `<`
`>` in them. Every other instance name is unchanged (`g2$i32$P`, `Two$i64$i32`, `Box$Pair$i32`). A call-site diagnostic
spells the instance as the source does, `g2<P<R>, i32>` (diagnostics_caps.cyml
a_diagnostic_names_a_method_and_an_instance_as_written_v6714).

A SIGNATURE'S TYPE ARGUMENTS ARE CHECKED EVEN WHEN NOTHING CALLS THE FN (D6 (a), GN-11). `fn gm(m: Mp<K, V>, b)` with
`K` / `V` declared nowhere, `m: Box<Zork>` and `m: Box<u8>` are refused at the definition ("unknown type 'K' as a type
argument (...)") — through 6.7.13 `m: Box<Zork>` silently took a `Box<i64>`. If the name was meant generic, make it the
fn's own type parameter: `fn gm<K, V>(m: Mp<K, V>)`. A struct declared below the fn is fine. Unread through 6.7.14,
read at the definition since 6.7.15 (6.7.15's D7, GN-9): a multi-value element, a trait member's signature, a `*fn(..)`
parameter type — and a pointer field's `*G<..>` (6.7.15's D8, GN-7).

`Vec<..>` IS CHECKED EVERYWHERE (D4 (a), D5 (a), GN-10): a field's nested `Vec<Vec<..>>`, a trait member's parameter and
`: Vec<..>` return, a multi-value element, `Box<Vec<..>>`, `idv<Vec<..>>(c)`, `sizeof(Box<Vec<..>>)`. A generic
element's own list is read as that struct's annotation reads it: `Vec<Box<nosuch>>` is refused, and a struct FIELD
`Vec<Box<Later>>` needs `Later` declared ABOVE the struct, as a field `Box<Later>` always did (a local's or a parameter's
does not). A non-generic element's list (`Vec<Pt<..>>`) was skipped through 6.7.14 and is refused at the name since
6.7.15 (6.7.15's D6, GN-3); a Vec element's generic struct is held to its bounds since 6.7.15 too (D10, GN-13).

`p.m<T>(x)` IS THE METHOD SPELLING OF `P_m<T>(&p, x)` (D7 (a), GN-16): `p.id<f64>(h)` calls `P_id$f64`, and `var a =
p.id<f64>(h);` is an f64 — on a chain, a `*P` receiver, a statement, a struct receive / return, a bounded member, and
`p.pick<T>(t)` forwarding T in a generic. It is a call only when `m` is a generic method and NO field of the receiver's
struct: a field of that name wins, so `p.n < k` stays a comparison. `var a, b = p.mm<i64>(3);` did not destructure in
6.7.14; since 6.7.15 the explicit spelling is a method call to every lookahead, exactly as `p.mm(3)` (GN-21).

THE PARAMETER'S SHAPE DECIDES WHAT A POINTER ARGUMENT BINDS (D8 (a), GN-17). Through `fn g<T>(p: *T)` every struct
pointer binds its pointee — `&s`, `&G`, a `*S` local, parameter (an untyped impl `self`), capture or global, a `*S`
field, a `: *S` call (a generic one at its instance); `&p` of a pointer binds nothing. Through 6.7.13 only a `*S` LOCAL
bound S, and every other spelling ran the i64 base (`sizeof(T)` read 8 for a 24-byte S). Through `fn g<T>(x: T)` a
pointer is an i64 — a `*S` local bound S there since 6.6.17, against the guide — so a struct-using `g(q)` with `q: *S`
is now refused: write `g<S>(q)`. A SCALAR pointer (`*i32`) through `p: *T` still bound i64 in 6.7.14 (pointer
arithmetic inside stepped 8 bytes); since 6.7.15 it binds its pointee — i8 / i16 / i32 / f64 (6.7.15's D11, GN-14).

A FORWARD TO A BOUNDED GENERIC WITHOUT AN i64 IMPL COMPILES AT ITS OTHER TYPES (GN-14). `fn o<T>(x: T) { return gb(x);
}` with `gb<T: Show>` and no `impl Show for i64`: `o(h)` with `h: f64` calls `o$f64`; only `o(3)` / `&o` / a tail call
are refused, "generic 'o' has no i64 instance: it forwards its type parameter to 'gb'", with one note naming the impl
the chain's end needs. A chain of more than 64 such forwards was refused at a definition in 6.7.14; since 6.7.15 any
depth is judged, a CALLED chain held to the nesting bound (about 120 forwards; GN-20; ~300 since 6.7.16, GB-2). A
bounded method call on a carrier's T field (`b.v.show()`, `b: Box<T>`) is the bound's call since 6.7.14, as on a
T-typed name (GN-15).

A `: T` GENERIC IS NOT A HANDLE SOURCE; A `: *T` ONE IS (GN-5, GN-5r, GN-6). `var h: S = gpg<S>();` from a generic
declared `: *T` (or one returning an untyped value) keeps its annotation: a handle to the struct (the annotation was
dropped). A generic returning its own `: T` was not such a source in 6.7.14: `var a: P3 = gid(5);` (a scalar instance)
was refused at `a.z` — or, with no field use, compiled with the annotation dropped. Since 6.7.15 it is a handle, as
`var a: P3 = wid(5);` is (6.7.15's D13 (a), GN-25; generics_since_6_7_15). `fn mkp<T>(x: T): *Box<T>` compiles.

ONE ERROR PER MISTAKE IN A GENERIC BODY (GN-18). A refused generic body prints each error and its notes once, however
many instances parse it (a generic struct's field refusal gave 1 error + 4 notes). In 6.7.14 an arity error and the
other direct error writers still printed once per instance, and so did every warning; since 6.7.15 both print once per
site (GN-22, GN-23).
See CHANGELOG [6.7.14] (GN-1 .. GN-18, GN-5r, GN-14r, GN-18r) and docs/development/ecosystem-migration.md § 6.7.14;
6.7.15's generics changes: generics_since_6_7_15.

[[entries]]
name = "struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_14"
description = "cycc 6.7.14 (the structs lane): your own `struct Result` / `Option` / `Tagged` is a plain struct; `n + &x` is the pointer `&x + n` is; `**pp` through a `**S` is the struct (but `*pp` as a whole copy source is not yet); a struct value is never a word pointer, a field, a call result or a dereference included; a `*V` dereference is no if-expression branch; `*(&s) += v` is refused as `s += v`; `return &q;` of a handle or a pointer is refused; an inline `Str` is a value and binding is not copying; the stdlib's `Str` methods take a `Str`; annotate a top-level `Str` declared from an inline global; a top-level `mk().n = 5;` is one error."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "structs", "Result", "Option", "Tagged", "**T", "Str", "inline Str", "handle", "&q", "if expression", "compound assignment", "D17", "D18", "D19", "D20", "D21", "D22", "D23"]
---
cycc 6.7.14 (language/features.cyml struct_handle_line_and_struct_values_at_every_size_v6710,
struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711, struct_parameter_is_a_copy_v6616, typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617,
if_expression_v674; language/core.cyml type_names_v6616). The decisions are 6.7.14's (the user, 2026-10-16); 6.7.13's
are named with their release.

YOUR OWN `struct Result` / `Option` / `Tagged` IS A PLAIN STRUCT (ST-5). As a PARAMETER type those three names were the
stdlib's one-word handles whatever the program declared, so a by-value `x: Result` of your own struct received its
first field and faulted. Now it is passed as a COPY, as every struct is — so a heap handle of it passed to `x: Result`
no longer aliases the caller's object (a callee's write no longer reaches it). Declare `x: *Result` to share. `Str`
stays a handle by name whatever declares it.

`n + &x` IS THE POINTER `&x + n` IS, INTO A BY-VALUE STRUCT TOO (ST-6). With a narrow `n` (`i8` / `i16` / `i32` / `u8`),
`bs(n + &x)` passes the struct at `&x + n` (it passed the address's bits as the struct); `n + 5` is still the value.

`**pp` THROUGH A `**S` IS THE STRUCT (ST-7). `var t: S = **pp;` copies (it bound a handle to the first field and
faulted), `**pp = u` copies `sizeof(S)` bytes, `l = **pp` copies, `**pp + a` dispatches `S_add`, a 1..7-byte S is read
byte-exact, and `return **pp;` / `return *pp;` from a fn returning S return the struct; an if-expression branch
`{ **pp }` copies at 8 B or less and is refused over 8. `**pp += v` is refused: write the field through `var p: *S =
*pp; p.x += v`. In 6.7.14 `*pp` as the WHOLE source of a copy was not the struct yet: `l = *pp` / `G = *pp` / `*q =
*pp` stored the pointer word into the first field, and `var t: S = *pp;` aliased (a handle). Since 6.7.15 each copies
in a fn (6.7.15's ST-2); at top level and into a struct field since 6.7.16 (6.7.16's D17 (a), ZN-11; ST-1 —
struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_16).

A STRUCT VALUE IS NEVER A WORD POINTER — A FIELD, A CALL RESULT OR A DEREFERENCE INCLUDED (ST-8). `ri(w.s)`, `ri(mk())`,
`ri(o.dup())`, `ri(a + b)`, `ri(*pp)` / `ri(**pp)` into a `*i64` / `*u8` / `**T` (or any `*T` that records no struct)
are refused once: "cannot pass 's' to a parameter that is not a pointer to its struct type in a call to 'ri' - a struct
value is no address". Through 6.7.13 each pushed the value's first word as the address. Pass an address: `&w + offset`,
the pointer, or `&t` of a local holding the call's result. An address, a `*T` field, a `: *T` call and a `Str` field
still pass.

A `*V` DEREFERENCE IS A VECTOR, SO IT IS NO IF-EXPRESSION BRANCH (ST-9). `if (c) { *pa } else { *pb }` (and `(*pa)`,
`*gpv()`, `*&a`, `**ppa`, a `*V` field, `*(pa + 0)`) is refused "an if expression branch cannot be a vector, u128 or
slice value - branch on a pointer (&x)" — through 6.7.13 it was lane 0's word, silently. Branch on the pointers, then
dereference.

`*(&s) += v` IS `s += v`, SO IT IS REFUSED (D17 (a), ST-10): "compound assignment to struct 's' is refused - a struct
value is not an integer or a float (write `a = a + b`)" — a local, a by-value parameter, a global, a capture, a for
step, `*&s`. Through 6.7.13 it was the word op on s's first field. Write the field (`s.x += v`) or `s = s + b`. A
handle's `*(&h) += v` and a scalar's `*(&n) += v` keep the word op (`&h` is the slot).

`return &q;` OF A HANDLE OR A POINTER IN A FN RETURNING A STRUCT IS REFUSED (D19 (a), ST-11), at every size: "a fn
returning the struct `S8` takes a value of that struct, or a pointer to one — `&q` is the address of q's slot, not a
struct — return q". `&q` is the slot (6.7.10's handle line); a fn of 8 B or less returned the slot's address as the
struct's word. Write `return q;` (for a pointer or a handle of ANOTHER struct, `return q;` then meets the
another-struct refusal). `return &s;` of an inline struct still returns the struct (6.7.13's D4).

AN INLINE `Str` IS A VALUE, AND BINDING IS NOT COPYING (D18 (c), D20 (a), D23 (b); ST-12, ST-13, ST-16).
  - `t = a` into an inline `Str` from a `Str` HANDLE (a handle local / parameter / global, a `: Str` call or method, a
    `Str` field) COPIES the 16-byte header; it stored a's address into t.data and left t.len stale. An untyped source
    keeps the one-word store.
  - A bare `var t: Str;` (no initialiser) is an inline `Str` too: its name is its header's address, and it is never
    returned or stored ("would dangle"). To keep 6.6.16's stand-in-handle pun, declare it `var t = a;`.
  - `var h: Str = t;`, `var h = t;`, `h = t` and `var p: *Str = t;` BIND to t's header: a write through them changes t.
    Such a handle is `&t` and dangles when the frame returns — the compiler does not refuse it (D23 (b)). Return
    `str_new(t.data, t.len)`.
  - The stdlib's `Str` methods take a `Str` operand (D18 (c)): `h.eq(t)` with an inline t, and `h.eq("lit")` /
    `h.contains("lit")` / `h.cat("xy")`, compute what `str_eq` / `str_cat` do — through 6.7.13 they read garbage (a
    literal's own bytes read as a header). A cstring VARIABLE is still a word: wrap it with `str_from`.
  - An UNTYPED position — `fncallN`, `callptr`, `vec_push`, a tuple element, an untyped if-expression branch — still
    takes an inline `Str`'s data word: pass `&t` there.

AT TOP LEVEL, ANNOTATE A `Str` DECLARED FROM AN INLINE GLOBAL (D21 (a), D22 (b); ST-14, ST-15). `var H: Str = gt;` is a
handle to gt's static header (an alias, 8 bytes; it was a 16-byte copy): give H its own literal for a copy. The untyped
`var H = gt;` keeps gt's FIRST WORD, so `f(H)` reads garbage — in a fn `var h = gt;` is the handle. A gt declared below
H was refused in 6.7.14; since 6.7.15 H binds to its header (6.7.15's D17, ST-5).

A TOP-LEVEL `mk().n = 5;` (A STRUCT RETURNED BY VALUE) IS ONE ERROR, "'mk' returns a struct by value, and a struct
result needs storage in a fn's frame — call it inside a fn" — `mkp().p[0] = 5;` and `o.mk().n = 5;` too (ST-2, ST-3;
`o.mk().p[0] = 5;` reported two in 6.7.14, and since 6.7.15 it and `var x = mk().p[0];` are one error each — 6.7.15's
ST-19, ST-20). Call it inside a fn. A classic-`for` step stores through a `: *T` call's field since 6.7.14 (`gp().n +=
1`, ST-4: parser_syntax.cyml a_subscript_base_is_a_name_field_or_call_declared_star_t_v678).
See CHANGELOG [6.7.14] (ST-1 .. ST-16, ST-7r) and docs/development/ecosystem-migration.md § 6.7.14; 6.7.15's struct
changes: struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_15.

[[entries]]
name = "receives_and_multi_value_since_6_7_14"
description = "cycc 6.7.14 (the receives + multi-value lane): inside a closure an enclosing local's name is the capture for writes too — a same-named global never takes them; parentheses around a whole value never change its rule or its type; a struct result over 8 B never becomes a scalar or a pointer silently, an assignment and a top-level `*T` included; a top-level `var x = mk1();` is the struct; a call through `&f` is typed as `f(..)`; a string literal into a `*u8` never warns; `ret2(a, b)` is `return (a, b);` to every count; a tuple parameter takes an address; a fn that propagates a pair with `?` is a pair wherever it is defined."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "receives", "multi-value", "closures", "capture", "parentheses", "6.7.11 D24", "*T", "fncallN", "callptr", "typed pointer", "ret2", "tuple parameter", ": stack", "?", "D24", "D25", "D26", "D27", "D28", "D29", "D30", "D42", "D43", "D44", "D45", "D46", "D53"]
---
cycc 6.7.14 (language/features.cyml closures_v637_v638, struct_value_operands_and_parentheses_v676,
struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711, typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617, ret2_rethi, tuples_v677,
stack_enum_unboxed_payload_variants_v6555). The decisions are 6.7.14's (the user, 2026-10-16); earlier releases' carry
their release. Each closes a "filed" line of struct_values_and_receives_since_6_7_13 or multi_value_returns_since_6_7_13.

INSIDE A CLOSURE AN ENCLOSING LOCAL'S NAME IS THE CAPTURE, FOR WRITES TOO (RC-1). With a same-named top-level global,
`y += d`, `y = 9`, a for init / step or `a, y = f()` on a captured `y` compiled and wrote the GLOBAL while the read
beside it saw the capture; now each is refused "'y' is captured by value; a captured name is not assignable in a
closure — copy it into a local (`var y_l = y;`)". A closure that sees no enclosing `y` still writes the global. And a
closure's parameters are untyped: `|m: i64|` is refused at the `:`, "a closure's parameters are untyped — drop `: T`"
(RC-4).

PARENTHESES AROUND A WHOLE VALUE NEVER CHANGE ITS RULE OR ITS TYPE (D24, D45, D46; RC-5 – RC-7, RC-11, RC-20, RC-21). In
a fn `var s = (mks());` is a `Str`, `var a = (f()) * 2.0;` an f64, `(s).a` / `(*p).a` / `(mk1()).a` / `(s).m()` read as
without them, `HE { 7, (q0), 8 }` is the element, `var a, b = (f(7));` destructures and `var t: (i64, i64) =
(two(3));` captures. A store whose WHOLE value is parenthesised and starts with a `: stack` pair call — `var v = (f(7) +
1);`, `h.a = ((f(7)) + 1);` — is refused "bind both" as unwrapped (it kept the tag); `x = (3 + f(7));` keeps the tag as
`x = 3 + f(7);` does. Refused by name: `(x + 1).a` ("… the value in these parentheses is not a struct (or a pointer to
one)") and a store through the wrap, `(s).a = v;` ("cannot assign to a field after parentheses - write the store
without them (`s.a = v`)"). `(s).a;` as a statement was a discarded read in 6.7.14 only (the unwrapped `s.a;` is
refused); since 6.7.15 it is refused as `s.a;` is (6.7.15's D60, ST-16). The one exception left in 6.7.14, a TOP-LEVEL
`var s = (mks());`, is routed as the unwrapped spelling since 6.7.15 (6.7.15's RC-4).

A STRUCT RESULT OVER 8 B NEVER BECOMES A SCALAR OR A POINTER SILENTLY (D27, D28; RC-9, RC-9r, RC-12). Not in a
declaration (6.7.11's D24), not in an ASSIGNMENT — `x = mkp();`, `x = b.mk();`, `x = a + c;`, `x = fncall0(&mkp);` into a
variable, parameter or global declared `: i64` / `u8` / `f64` / `f32` / `i32` / `cstring` / an enum / `u128` (a
classic-`for` header's `var x: u8` in the loop body too): "cannot assign 'x: i64' from 'mkp', which returns the struct
'Q' - declare 'x: Q' or bind a field" — and not into a `*T` at top level, in both zones: "cannot initialise 'X: *P2' from
'mkp', which returns the struct 'P2' - declare 'X: P2' and take `&X`" (through 6.7.13 it stored the first word as the
pointer and `X.b` faulted). Bind a field (`x = mkp().a;`) or declare the struct. 8 B and `Str` keep the word. An
UNTYPED destination (`var x = 0; x = mkp();`) and a `*T` local of another type (`p = mkp();`) still took the first
word in 6.7.14; both are refused by name since 6.7.15 (6.7.15's D33 / D32; receives_since_6_7_15).

A TOP-LEVEL `var x = mk1();` IS THE STRUCT (D29, D30; RC-14). An untyped top-level declaration of one whole free call
binds the struct it returns: of 8 B or less x is that struct (`x.a` reads the field; `x + 1` calls `P1_add`, refused
without one — it added to the word); of 9 – 16 B it is refused, "'mk2' returns a struct by value, and a struct result
needs storage in a fn's frame — call it inside a fn" (x held the first word). Annotate `: i64` for the word. In 6.7.14
a method / operator / `&f` result and a zone `var x = mkg1(..);` whose generic callee returns a generic struct INSTANCE
kept the word; since 6.7.15 each binds its struct the same way (6.7.15's D34 / D35, RC-13, RC-14). A `Str` call and a
name keep the word at top level.

A CALL THROUGH `&f` IS TYPED AS `f(..)` (D25, D26; RC-8, RC-10). `fncallN(&f, ..)` / `callptr(&f, ..)` of a `: f64`
callee is an f64 whatever its arguments (6.7.6's last-argument rule is gone), and of a `: *T` callee a `*T`
(`fncall0(&gp) + 1` steps one element; it stepped one byte). Through a callee held in a VARIABLE it is a word. For byte
arithmetic, copy the result untyped (`var q = fncall0(&gp); q + 1`). `callptr(&gp, 5).b = 40;` stores, as the
`fncall1` statement did.

A STRING LITERAL IS A `*u8` / `*i8` VALUE; `0` IS A NULL POINTER (D53; RC-13). Both are exempt from "assigning
non-pointer to typed pointer" at every site (a declaration, an assignment, a parameter, a global, the zone) — 6.7.13's
D18 had spread the warning onto `t = "xyz";`. In 6.7.14 a string literal into a `*i64` (or a one-byte struct's `*B1`)
and a string EXPRESSION (`"abc" + 1`, `("xyz")`) still warned; since 6.7.15 a string literal alone, ± n or wrapped into
ANY `*T` never warns (6.7.15's D59, RC-6, RC-7). Anything else without a pointer scale still warns. `p = &a[1];` is
refused without the warning beside it.

`ret2(a, b)` IS `return (a, b);` TO EVERY COUNT (D42, D43; RC-16, RC-17). A fn left only through `ret2(..);` / `return
ret2(..);` / `return (a, b);` (a final one, or an if / elif / else of them) is a 2-value fn: `var a, b, c = g2();`, a
3-tuple capture, a by-value struct argument and a 3-value forward are refused (the third value read a stale register).
⚠ The if / else end counts with no `ret2` too: three names from `if (x > 0) { return (x, 1); } else { return (x, 2); }`
are refused (they compiled). `ret2` is refused wherever the fn's declared return is not two words — a 3-value fn, a
named struct (6.7.13), and since 6.7.14 a vector ("a vector-returning fn carries its result in a fixed register pair,
so `return` takes … — got `ret2`"). A closure's or an `#inline` replay's `ret2` is that fn's own.

A TUPLE PARAMETER TAKES AN ADDRESS (D44; RC-18, RC-19). An untyped word passes as the tuple's address (`take(&t)`, a
variable holding `&t`, a `: i64` call returning one — a tuple has no `*T` spelling); an integer literal (`take(5)`) is
refused "cannot pass an integer to tuple parameter 't' (i64, i64) of 'take' - it takes a tuple", and a several-value
call from a fn declared ONE scalar by "a multi-value call 'g2' is not a tuple argument - bind it first". A one-value
call (`take(one(3))`) is your word — and crashes if it is not an address. `take(-5)` / `take(true)` still compiled in
6.7.14; since 6.7.15 any literal-only argument is refused by name (6.7.15's D36, RC-3). `var t: (i64, i64) =
callptr(fp, ..);` captures since 6.7.14 (its count is not checked).

A FN THAT PROPAGATES A PAIR WITH `?` RETURNS A PAIR, WHEREVER IT IS DEFINED (RC-15, RC-22). `fn qb(x) { var v =
parse(x)?; return v; }` (an impl method, an inherited trait default, a dyn slot too) is a pair to a caller ABOVE it, as
to one below: `qb(x)?` there propagates (it dereferenced the tag: 139), and `var v = qb(x);` is refused "bind both" (it
kept the Err tag). Bind both or `?` it. A closure's own `?` in it (`|a| parse(a)?`) is the closure's.
See CHANGELOG [6.7.14] (RC-1 .. RC-22, RC-9r) and docs/development/ecosystem-migration.md § 6.7.14; 6.7.15's receives
changes: receives_since_6_7_15.

[[entries]]
name = "vector_values_since_6_7_14"
description = "cycc 6.7.14 (the vectors lane): a missing fn in any vector position is reported as missing; an untyped `var v = *p;` or `var v = s.mv();` is now the vector; a 16-byte value-form `x: f64v2` takes any 16-byte vector; a generic's `pp: **T` binds T from a `**S` argument but not through parentheses or arithmetic; `&` is a pointer conversion, never a value-form argument, on Windows too; a struct field is no `*V`; a `*V` dereference into a word pointer is refused however it is spelled."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "SIMD", "vectors", "f64v2", "f32v4", "*V", "**T", "value-form", "Windows", "Win64", "generics", "undefined function", "D31", "D32", "D33", "D34", "D35", "D36", "D37", "D38", "D39", "D40", "D41"]
---
cycc 6.7.14 (language/features.cyml typed_simd_value_type_abi, overload_dispatch_pointer_vs_value, closures_v637_v638,
generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310). The decisions D31 – D41 are 6.7.14's (the user, 2026-10-16); 6.7.13's D29 – D34
are other decisions of the same numbers. The value-form argument rule after 6.7.14 (a local of its type, a call declared
to return it, a closure's capture, a `*p` of a pointer to its type, any of them wrapped — the same on every target) is
in call_arguments_and_fn_pointer_results_since_6_7_11; the 6.7.13 notes in
vector_pointers_step_vectors_and_windows_value_form_v6713.

A MISSING FN IN ANY VECTOR POSITION IS REPORTED AS MISSING (VC-7). In a local's initialiser or assignment (wrapped or
not), a `return` from a vector fn or a value-form argument, a call to a fn that does not exist (a missing `include
"lib/simd.cyr"`) is "undefined function 'f64v2_splat'" + "refusing to emit binary". Through 6.7.13 the wrapped, the
`return` and the argument positions reported it as a refused source ("… got `f64v2_splat`"), and the argument never
named the missing include. So a "got `..`" refusal there names a call that EXISTS. (A vector fn returning a missing fn's
call that is never called now builds with the undefined-function warning, as the unwrapped local always did.)

AN UNTYPED `var v = *p;` OR `var v = s.mv();` IS NOW THE VECTOR (D36, D37; VC-13, VC-14). With `p: *f64v2` / `fn
S_mv(self): f64v2` each bound ONE word through 6.7.13, and its value differed per target. Code that meant the word
annotates it: `var x: i64 = load64(p);`. `return *p;` and `return s.mv();` return the vector (D35, D39), and a method's
vector into a scalar, struct or pointer local is refused "vector return type does not match the declared type" (D38).
Only the WHOLE initialiser counts, as for `var v = mkv(..)`: in 6.7.14 a wrapped `var x = (mk2(..))` / `(s.m2())`, a
TOP-LEVEL `var w = mkv(..)` and a chained receiver `var v = w.s.mv();` still bound one word silently. Since 6.7.15 the
wrapped and the chained forms are the vector and the top-level one is refused (6.7.15's D28 / D26; VC-4, VC-7, VC-9).

A 16-BYTE VALUE-FORM PARAMETER `x: f64v2` TAKES ANY 16-BYTE VECTOR (6.4.4's class). It takes an f32v4 or
integer-vector local, capture or `*p` too, and reads its bytes as the f64v2's lanes. Only `v: f32v4` and the 32-byte
types check the exact type. Annotate the parameter with the type you mean.

A GENERIC'S `pp: **T` BINDS T FROM A `**S` ARGUMENT — BUT NOT THROUGH PARENTHESES OR ARITHMETIC (D40; VC-15). A T that
no `x: T` / `p: *T` parameter and no generic struct carries is carried by the first parameter declared exactly `pp:
**T`: `st(pps)` runs `st$P3` (a `**S` local, parameter, capture, global, field chain, `: **S` call, `&p` / `&G` of a
`*S`) — through 6.7.13 it ran the i64 base, so `*pp + 1` stepped 8. In 6.7.14 `st((pps))` and `st(pps + 0)` still ran
the i64 base, so `tg((pps))`'s `take(**pp)` faulted (139); since 6.7.15 a wrapped or offset pointer argument binds as
the pointer does (6.7.15's D22 / D23, VC-14), a `**i32` binds i32 (6.7.15's D11, GN-14) and a `***S` through `ppp:
***T` binds S (6.7.15's D24, VC-15). A `*S` through `pp: **T` binds nothing.

`&` IS A POINTER CONVERSION, NEVER A VALUE (D33; VC-6). `lanes(&w)` into a value-form parameter is refused on every
target since 6.7.14 — Windows ran it. In a receive, `f64v2_add(&a, &b)` still means `f64v2_add_ptr`, on Windows too
(its own-call receives route there now, as a call does). Your own value-form fn called with addresses needs a `_ptr`
sibling, or pass the locals.

A STRUCT FIELD IS NO `*V` (D34; VC-12). `rd(w.s)` into `p: *f64v2` is refused by name. Pass `&w` (plus an offset) if the
bytes are meant; `&w.s` is no spelling.

A `*V` DEREFERENCE INTO A WORD POINTER IS REFUSED HOWEVER IT IS SPELLED (VC-10). `ri(**ppb)`, `ri(*gp(pb))`,
`ri(*h.pv)`, `ri(*GPV)`, `ri(*(pb + 0))`, `ri(*&b)` into a `*i64` / `*u8` / `**T` or a small by-value struct pushed lane
0 as the address through 6.7.13. In 6.7.14 the refusal quoted the operand's LAST name — `ri(*gp(pb))` printed '*pb'
and `ri(*&b)` '*b'; since 6.7.15 it quotes the argument as written, '*gp(pb)' / '*&b' (6.7.15's VC-12).

A VECTOR TYPE'S NAME IS NOT RESERVED (D41; VC-16): a program's own `struct f32v4 { .. }` is that struct everywhere
(vector_pointers_step_vectors_and_windows_value_form_v6713). And a program may name 16383 `**` pointee types (126 in
6.7.13; VC-1).
See CHANGELOG [6.7.14] (VC-1 .. VC-17, VC-4r) and docs/development/ecosystem-migration.md § 6.7.14; 6.7.15's vector
changes: vector_values_since_6_7_15.

[[entries]]
name = "a_u128_is_not_a_parameter_return_or_field_type_yet_v6714"
description = "Since cycc 6.7.14 a bare `u128` parameter, return or field type is refused by name (a parameter took the LOW word and compared it signed: `big(0)` with `v > 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF` was true) — pass `*u128` (`f(&b)`) or the two halves; since 6.7.15 a trait member's `: u128` return too, and `*pu` of a typed `*u128` is refused. A u128 captured by a closure reads its full value since 6.7.14 (it read the env copy's address), and two `#inline` calls with u128 arguments in one expression compute right since 6.7.15."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "v6.7.15", "u128", "parameter", "return type", "field", "trait", "closures", "capture", "*u128", "#inline", "D10", "D3", "D20"]
---
    fn big(v: u128): i64 { .. }     # 6.7.14: `u128` is not supported as a parameter type yet - pass `*u128`
    fn mk(): u128 { .. }            # `u128` is not supported as a fn return type yet
    struct S { w: u128; }           # `u128` is not supported as a field type yet
    fn big(v: *u128): i64 { .. }    # write this, and call big(&b)

A `v: u128` parameter was one 8-byte slot holding the LOW word, compared signed (`v > 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF` was true for
0, so `big(0)` was 1), and `&v + 8` read the next frame slot. Since 6.7.14 it is refused by name (D10 (a)), a trait
member's parameter too; a return or field type says the same in its own words (it said "expected identifier, got
reserved keyword 'u128'"). Pass `*u128` or the two halves until u128 is carried as a 16-byte value (the roadmap_6
arc). `*u128` and a `*fn(..)`'s own list are unchanged. A trait member's `: u128` RETURN still compiled in 6.7.14; since
6.7.15 it is refused by name, as a fn's is (6.7.15's D3, GN-11: declare `: *u128`, and its impls with it). A default on
a bare `u128` parameter (`fn f(b: u128 = 3)`) is the type's one error since 6.7.15 (FE-4). And `*pu` of a typed
`*u128` is refused since 6.7.15, as `pu[0]` is (6.7.15's D20, ST-11): read the halves with `load64` at offsets 0 and 8
through an untyped address.

A u128 CAPTURED BY A CLOSURE IS ITS VALUE since 6.7.14: `|q| { c == 5 }` was false for `c = 5`, and `c + 0` added the
env copy's address; `==`, `<`, `>`, `+` / `-` with carry, a truth test, `!`, `match` and a nested closure's read now see
all 128 bits, on every target. Open in 6.7.14 (High): two `#inline` calls in one expression with u128 arguments
(`id(a) + id(a + 1)`) read a wrong value (84 for 83 on x86_64 / aarch64 / PE) — a frame temporary taken while an inline
fn's arguments ran lay past the frame. Fixed in 6.7.15 (VC-1; vector_values_since_6_7_15). language/features.cyml
f32_writes_round_and_u128_arithmetic_v676. CHANGELOG [6.7.14] FE-26; [6.7.15] FE-4, GN-11, ST-11, VC-1.

[[entries]]
name = "last_definition_wins_reaches_the_stdlibs_own_callers_v6714"
description = "A fn or enum constant you define with a stdlib name replaces the stdlib's for EVERY caller when yours binds last — the stdlib's own callers too: a test stub after `include \"lib/ws_server.cyr\"` ran inside ws_server's handshake, and a program's `SYS_POLL = 39` makes `fd_wait_ready` call getpid. The other direction holds too: a later stdlib definition replaces YOURS for every use, above it as well (6.7.16: the agnos cycc_cx's `SYS_*`). And since 6.7.16 every call of a redefined fn is typed by its last definition. The `duplicate fn` / `duplicate symbol` warning is the only signal: read it, and rename yours."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "v6.7.16", "last definition wins", "duplicate fn", "duplicate symbol", "stdlib", "SYS_POLL", "SYS_EPOLL_WAIT", "SYS_OPEN", "epoll_event_set", "ws_server", "io.cyr", "process.cyr", "stubs", "agnos", "cycc_cx"]
---
    include "lib/ws_server.cyr"
    fn sandhi_server_find_header(..) { .. }   # warning: duplicate fn 'sandhi_server_find_header' (last definition wins;
                                              #   first defined in …) — ws_server's own handshake now runs THIS
    enum { SYS_POLL = 39; }                   # warning: duplicate symbol 'SYS_POLL' redefined with conflicting value
                                              #   (last definition wins) — fd_wait_ready(999, 0, 0) runs getpid: 1, not -9

A top-level name is ONE definition program-wide, and the LAST one binds (the 6.6.6 rule; for a global's value see
a_same_value_redeclaration_is_silent_by_design_v6712). So a fn or enum constant you define with a stdlib name replaces
the stdlib's for every caller — the stdlib's own callers included:
  - a test stand-in for `sandhi_server_find_header` placed AFTER `include "lib/ws_server.cyr"` made ws_server's
    handshake run the stub (`sock_send_all_short_write.tcyr`, until 6.7.14 — LIB-3); a stand-in also outlives the day
    the module starts including the real fn;
  - a program's own `epoll_event_set` (new in the stdlib in 6.7.14) makes `epoll_event_new` and `lib/async.cyr`'s
    registrations run it (measured: `epoll_event_new(1, 5)` returned the program's 77 — LIB-4);
  - the stdlib declares `SYS_POLL` (7) in every peer and `SYS_EPOLL_WAIT` (232) on aarch64 since 6.7.14 (PL-6: so
    `fd_wait_ready` and `sys_epoll_wait` spell their x86 numbers by name). A program enum of the same name with another
    value warns, and then the other number is issued: AFTER the include the stdlib's `fd_wait_ready` issues yours;
    BEFORE it your own `syscall(SYS_POLL)` issues 7. The same value changes nothing. Do not declare your own `SYS_*`
    names — rename yours, or use the stdlib's.
The `duplicate fn '…' (last definition wins; first defined in …)` / `duplicate symbol '…' redefined with conflicting value
(last definition wins)` warning is the signal: read it, don't scroll past it, and rename the private fn or constant (a
module prefix).

6.7.16 — BOTH DIRECTIONS, EVERY USE, EVERY CALL:
  - THE INVERSE: a LATER stdlib definition replaces YOURS — for every use, the ones above it too (TT-7, TT-10). The
    agnos `cycc_cx`'s driver declared `enum Sys { … SYS_OPEN = 2; … }` (Linux's numbers), and in an agnos build
    lib/alloc.cyr's lib/syscalls.cyr, included later, redefined `SYS_EXIT` / `SYS_READ` / `SYS_CLOSE` / `SYS_OPEN` with
    agnos's — so every use in the driver, those above the second definition included, read agnos's values (four true
    warnings). Probe: `enum A { K = 3; } fn f(): i64 { return K; } enum B { K = 9; } syscall(60, f() * 10 + K);` warns
    once and exits 99 — `f()` reads 9. Declare per-target values under the target's `#ifdef` yourself (what the drivers
    now do), or take the stdlib's and declare none; never rely on which definition a use sits next to.
  - EVERY CALL OF A REDEFINED FN IS TYPED BY ITS LAST DEFINITION (SS-3, SS-4) — above, between and after the
    definitions: its `*T` step (with `fn gp(): *H` then `fn gp(): i64`, `gp() + 1` stepped by sizeof(H)) and its `bool`
    return and parameters. A program carrying the `duplicate fn` warning changes value when the definitions disagreed;
    `gp().b = 5` through a winner returning no pointer stops compiling. Delete the earlier definition.
  - lib/process.cyr includes lib/io.cyr on Linux and macOS since 6.7.16 (for `env_block`, TL-11r): a fn of your own with
    one of io.cyr's names beside process.cyr now draws `duplicate fn '<name>' (last definition wins; first defined in
    lib/io.cyr)` — yours still wins, for io.cyr's own callers too. Rename it.
CHANGELOG [6.7.14] LIB-3, LIB-4, PL-6; [6.7.16] TT-7, TT-10, SS-3, SS-4, TL-11r.

[[entries]]
name = "generics_since_6_7_15"
description = "cycc 6.7.15 (the generics lane): a `>>` before a `,` in a type-argument list no longer drops the next argument; type arguments on a type that takes none are refused (D6); a pointer field's, a multi-value element's, a trait member's and a `*fn(..)` type's arguments are read (D7, D8); a trait member returns no `Vec` / `u128` and an impl's return must match its member's (D3, D4, D9); a Vec element meets its bounds (D10); `p: *T` binds a scalar pointee (D11) and a wrapped / dereferenced / captured struct binds as its plain spelling (D12); only the bound's methods on a carrier's field (D5); `x.m<..>(..)` is `x.m(..)`; a `: T` word instance into a struct local is a handle (D13); errors and warnings print once per site. Cross-linked from generics_since_6_7_14."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.15", "generics", "type arguments", ">>", "trait bounds", "traits", "impl", "Vec", "*T", "**T", "scalar pointee", "parentheses", "handle", "diagnostics", "D3", "D4", "D5", "D6", "D7", "D8", "D9", "D10", "D11", "D12", "D13"]
---
cycc 6.7.15 (language/features.cyml generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310, nested_generic_type_arguments_v6617,
traits_checked_v670, trait_bounds_v671). The decisions D3 – D13 are 6.7.15's (the user, 2026-10-18); a "6.7.14's Dn" is
the 6.7.14 open's. The 6.7.14 notes are generics_since_6_7_14.

A `>>` BEFORE A `,` NO LONGER DROPS THE NEXT TYPE ARGUMENT (GN-1, a High). A nested argument closed by `>>` / `>>>` and
followed by `,` swallowed the `,` and the argument after it: `g2<P<P<R>>, i16>` ran with B = i64, and `Two<P<P<R>>,
i16>` was 24 bytes. Since 6.7.15 it binds as written (B = i16; `Two<..>` is 18 B, its `b` an i16) — a program whose
numbers came from the dropped argument changes — and a signature with such a parameter before another compiles.

NO TYPE ARGUMENTS ON A TYPE THAT TAKES NONE (D6, GN-3). `Pt<i64>` of a non-generic struct, `i64<u8>`, and `slice<u8>` of
your own non-generic `struct slice` are refused once, at the name: "'Pt' is not a generic struct: it takes no type
arguments". The list was skipped unread (and a name inside it never checked). Delete the `<..>`. A few positions still
accepted it silently in 6.7.15 — a pointer field `*Pt<i64>`, a multi-value element, a trait member's parameter,
`*fn(Pt<i64>)`, `**Pt<i64>`, `*i32<u8>` — refused since 6.7.16 (6.7.16's D7: generics_since_6_7_16). And
`Box<i64>` written out is the base `Box` everywhere, an explicit type argument included (GN-4): an instance over it is
named `gx$Box` (it was `gx$Box$i64`) in the coverage dump, `cyrius syms` and a diagnostic (`'gx<Box>'`).

EVERY SIGNATURE POSITION READS ITS TYPE ARGUMENTS (D7, D8; GN-7, GN-9). A pointer field `p: *Box<Zork>` is refused AT
THE FIELD, even if the field is never used; a multi-value element, a trait member's signature and a `*fn(..)` type are
read at the definition. A name there that names no type was ignored (the base struct ran) and now stops the build.

TRAIT MEMBERS (D3, D4, D9; GN-10 – GN-12). A member returns no `: Vec<..>` / `: Vec` / `: u128` — return `*Vec<T>` /
`*u128` (no impl of such a member could compile). An impl method's return must agree with its member's: an OMITTED
return is i64, so a member with no return implemented `: bool` (or `: i32`, or a multi-value) is refused at the impl,
by name. A return that only spells the same type differently agrees (GN-12r) — and since 6.7.16 a dyn forms from such
an impl too (GS-2). Parameter types are not compared.

A Vec ELEMENT MEETS ITS BOUNDS (D10, GN-13). `Vec<Box<Pt>>` with `struct Box<T: Show>` needs `impl Show for Pt`, in any
annotation, a field or a nested Vec, and is refused at the element otherwise. Add the impl, or drop the bound.

WHAT AN ARGUMENT BINDS (D11, D12; GN-14, GN-15). Each is a language change: `sizeof(T)`, the step and a `: T` result
change for code that compiled, and the explicit `g<i64>(..)` keeps the old base.
  - A SCALAR pointee binds through `p: *T` / `pp: **T`, as a struct does: `second(pa)` with `pa: *i32` is the i32
    instance — `p + 1` steps 4, `*p` reads 4, a `: T` result is sign-extended (it ran the i64 base: an 8-byte step over
    an i32 array). `*i8` / `*i16` / `*f64` likewise, from a local, a parameter, a capture, a global, a `: *T` call, a
    field chain or `&h`. `*u8` .. `*u64`, `*f32`, `*bool` and `*i64` bind i64, and so does a narrow PARAMETER's `&i`.
    ⚠ An untyped `var q = p + 1` inside the generic is an i64 word whose `*q` reads 8 bytes even in the i32 instance:
    name it `var q: *T = p + 1`.
  - Parentheses around a whole argument are transparent: `gs((l))` is `gs(l)`, and `g((h))` with an f64 is the f64
    instance; `gs(*p)` of a `*S` binds S, and a captured struct binds S. A captured f64 through `x: T` still bound i64
    in 6.7.15; it binds f64 since 6.7.16 (6.7.16's D3, GB-11).
  A wrapped or offset POINTER argument and `***T`: vector_values_since_6_7_15.

ONLY THE BOUND'S METHODS ON A CARRIER'S FIELD (D5, GN-18). Under `fn g<T: Show>(b: Box<T>)`, `b.v.other()` with
`other` declared by no trait of the bound is refused at the generic's definition, called or not. Add a trait declaring
it to the bound, or drop the bound (an unbounded T resolves per instance). The explicit spelling `b.v.other<i64>(1)`
slipped past the contract in 6.7.15; it is refused at the definition since 6.7.16 (6.7.16's D4, GB-4).

`x.m<..>(..)` BEHAVES EXACTLY AS `x.m(..)` (GN-21). A `: stack` pair result must be bound to two names or propagated
with `?` (one name kept the tag), a vector result is refused into a scalar, a multi-value one into a struct field — and
`x.m<..>(..)?` propagates (it crashed).

A `: T` GENERIC INTO A STRUCT-ANNOTATED LOCAL IS A HANDLE (D13 (a), GN-25). `var a: S = gid(5)` — a `: T` generic
reaching a word instance — is a handle, as `var a: S = wid(5)` is: `a.f` reads at the returned word plus the field's
offset (address 5 + offset: a fault), passing `a` where ANOTHER struct is expected is refused, and `a = 7;` warns
"assigning non-pointer to typed pointer". Declare the local untyped to keep a plain word.

A FORWARDING CHAIN MAY BE ANY LENGTH (GN-20). The judgement of a chain of forwards to a bounded generic is memoised, and
the 64-forward cap is gone; a CALLED chain was still held to the instance-nesting bound in 6.7.15 — about 120 forwards,
then "nesting too deep (over 256 levels)"; since 6.7.16 the compiler's stack bounds it, ~300 links (GB-2:
diagnostics_caps.cyml live_compiler_capacity_ceilings). A T-typed value passed whole into a stub's carrier position
makes the forwarder a stub (GN-17).

ONCE PER SITE. Errors, notes and warnings inside a generic or `#inline` body print once per site, not once per instance
(GN-22, GN-23), and a generic METHOD's refusals name it `P.pick`, as the call spells it (GN-24;
diagnostics_caps.cyml a_diagnostic_names_a_method_and_an_instance_as_written_v6714). A build log carries fewer lines.
See CHANGELOG [6.7.15] (GN-1 .. GN-25, GN-12r, GN-15r, GN-20r, GN-25r) and docs/development/ecosystem-migration.md
§ 6.7.15; 6.7.16's generics changes: generics_since_6_7_16.

[[entries]]
name = "struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_15"
description = "cycc 6.7.15 (the structs lane): a forward call into a fn whose signature names a struct declared below reads the real signature, so include order is harmless; `*pp` of a `**S` copies in a fn, not yet at top level or into a field; a dereference argument is judged by its parameter; an inline `Str` copies from every TYPED `Str` source (D14), a `*Str` takes an inline `Str`'s header (D16) and a zone `Str` binds a global declared below (D17); a `Str` field's method acts on the string; `*pu` of a `*u128` is refused (D20); `return &n;` of a non-struct in a struct fn is refused at every size (D18); `(s).a;` and a parenthesised destination are refused (D60, D19); an undefined fn's `.field` is an error even under `--allow-undef`. Cross-linked from struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_14."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.15", "structs", "forward call", "include order", "Str", "inline Str", "*Str", "**T", "*pp", "u128", "return &", "parentheses", "--allow-undef", "cyrius lint", "D14", "D16", "D17", "D18", "D19", "D20", "D58", "D60"]
---
cycc 6.7.15 (language/features.cyml struct_handle_line_and_struct_values_at_every_size_v6710,
struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711, typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617; language/core.cyml
type_names_v6616). The decisions are 6.7.15's (the user, 2026-10-18); a "6.7.14's Dn" is the 6.7.14 open's. The 6.7.14
notes are struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_14.

INCLUDE ORDER CAN PUT A STRUCT BELOW THE FNS THAT NAME IT (ST-1). A call above a fn whose signature names a struct
declared below THAT fn read a wrong signature: by-value arguments and `: S` / `: *S` returns faulted. Since 6.7.15 the
forward call reads the signature the definition reads, and passing ANOTHER struct to such a parameter is refused by
name ("cannot pass 'p' to a parameter of a different struct type in a call to 'g'" — it passed the wrong layout).
Including `lib/tls_native.cyr` (→ sigil → bayan), or ws, before `lib/str.cyr` puts `struct Str` below bayan's fns: that
is harmless now, and their forward `: Str` results are `Str`-typed.

`*pp` COPIES IN A FN — NOT AT TOP LEVEL, AND NOT INTO A FIELD YET (ST-2). With `pp: **S`, `var t: S = *pp;` (and
`(*pp)`) COPIES the struct — it aliased, so a write through t changed the original — and `l = *pp`, `G = *pp`, `*q =
*pp`, `**qq = *pp` copy the whole struct (they stored the pointer into the first field). `l = *qq` of ANOTHER struct's
`**Q` is refused by name. In 6.7.15 a top-level `var G: S = *GPP;` still bound a handle, and `o.s = *pp` into a struct
field still stored the pointer word — both copy since 6.7.16 (6.7.16's D17 (a), ZN-11; ST-1:
struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_16). Spell the alias `var t: *S = *pp;`.

A DEREFERENCE ARGUMENT IS JUDGED BY THE PARAMETER (ST-7). Into a `Str` parameter (`str_len`, `str_eq`, a `Str` method's
operand, your own fn) `*p` of a `*Str` passes the header p points at — p itself; into an untyped parameter it is the
word `*p` reads. ⚠ `var p: *Str = &h;` with h a `Str` HANDLE types the handle's SLOT as a `*Str` (`&h` is the slot), so
`str_len(*p)` now reads the slot as a header (0; it gave h's length): write `var p: *Str = h;`.

AN INLINE `Str` COPIES FROM EVERY TYPED `Str` SOURCE, AND KEEPS ONE WORD FROM AN UNTYPED ONE (D14 – D17; ST-5, ST-6,
ST-8, ST-9).
  - `t = p` / `t = *p` with p a `*Str` copy the header p points at into the inline t (D14; t.data took the pointer and
    t.len stayed stale), as a handle source has since 6.7.14 — a later write through p no longer shows in t.
  - `p = t` into a `*Str` takes t's header address (D16), as `var p: *Str = t;` did (it stored t's data word); a fn's
    own inline `Str` into a `*Str` GLOBAL (`GP = t`) is refused — the header would dangle.
  - A declaration-zone `var H: Str = gt;` with gt declared BELOW binds H to gt's header (D17; refused in 6.7.14).
  - An if-expression is untyped and keeps the one-word store (D15, kept and documented): assign in each branch.

A METHOD ON A `Str` FIELD ACTS ON THE STRING, NOT THE STRUCT (ST-10). `w.s.len()` / `w.s.setl(2);` (refused through
6.7.14) take the header the field holds as `self`, so a write lands in the string w.s names; on a nested INLINE struct
field, `self` is the field itself.

`*pu` OF A TYPED `*u128` IS REFUSED (D20, ST-11), as `pu[0]` is — a read, a store and `OP=`. Read the halves with
`load64` / `store64` at offsets 0 and 8 through an untyped address (`var w = pu; load64(w + 8)`): `pu + 8` on the typed
pointer is 128 bytes on.

IN A STRUCT-RETURNING FN, `return &x;` IS `return x;` ONLY FOR AN INLINE STRUCT OF THE FN'S OWN STRUCT (D18, D58; ST-13,
ST-14). Every other `&name` — a handle, a `*T`, a scalar, a `**S`, an array, a `Str` handle — is a slot's address and is
refused by name at every size: at 8 B or less `&n` of a non-struct compiled and the caller read a dead frame's address
as the struct's field. An inline `Str` or a tuple is refused as another struct. A generic's i64 base was not judged in
6.7.15; since 6.7.16 `return &n;` of a T value makes the generic struct-only (a scalar call refused, 6.7.16's D1) and of
a name no T types is refused at the definition, called or not (D2) — generics_since_6_7_16.

A WRAPPED FIELD READ IS A STATEMENT ONLY WHEN IT CALLS SOMETHING; A WRAPPED DESTINATION NEVER IS (D60, D19; ST-15,
ST-16). `(s).a;` is refused as `s.a;` is (it compiled in 6.7.14 only, and did nothing); `(mk1()).a;` and `(s).m();` keep
the statement. `(*p) += 1;` / `(n) = 7;` are refused naming the statement to write ("an assignment's destination is not
parenthesised - write `*p += 1`"). For a struct the quoted `*(&s) -= 1` is itself refused (6.7.14's D17): operate on a
field, or `s = s - b` with an operator fn.

AN UNDEFINED FN'S `.field` IS AN ERROR EVEN UNDER `--allow-undef` (ST-17, ST-18). `undefd().n = 1;` names the fn, once,
and refuses the build. `cyrius lint` accepts the same shape when the fn lives in a sibling module (`gp().n = 5;`) —
the cx compiler's `--syntax-only` too since 6.7.16 (TL-10), and a `: *Foo` fn defined here with Foo a sibling's struct
since 6.7.16 (ST-9). A top-level `o.mk().p[0] = 5;` / `var x = mk().p[0];` of a struct returned by value is one error
each (ST-19, ST-20).

THROUGH `pp: **Box<T>` (ST-4). The parameter compiles since 6.7.15, but in 6.7.15 `nb(&q)` with `q: *Box<Pt>` inferred
no T and silently ran the i64 base (`nb<Pt>(&q)` was the spelling); since 6.7.16 it infers T = Pt (GB-15).
See CHANGELOG [6.7.15] (ST-1 .. ST-20, ST-2r, ST-7r) and docs/development/ecosystem-migration.md § 6.7.15; 6.7.16's
struct changes: struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_16.

[[entries]]
name = "receives_since_6_7_15"
description = "cycc 6.7.15 (the receives lane): a known `&f` callee is the direct call all the way — its struct, f64, `*T` step AND element, and bool (D30, D31); a struct result over 8 B is taken only by its struct, so an assignment to a scalar, another `*T` or an untyped variable is refused (D32, D33); an untyped top-level global from any whole struct-valued call is that struct, 9 – 16 B refused (D34, D35); a literal-only argument into a tuple parameter is refused (D36); a string literal is an address into any `*T` (D59); the every-path `: stack` rule covers methods; a body ended by an endless loop counts `ret2` (D62). Cross-linked from receives_and_multi_value_since_6_7_14."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.15", "receives", "fncallN", "callptr", "&f", "bool", "*T", "struct results", "top level", "operators", "methods", "tuple parameter", "string literal", "typed pointer", ": stack", "ret2", "Str", "D30", "D31", "D32", "D33", "D34", "D35", "D36", "D59", "D62"]
---
cycc 6.7.15 (language/features.cyml struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711,
typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617, ret2_rethi, tuples_v677, stack_enum_unboxed_payload_variants_v6555). The
decisions are 6.7.15's (the user, 2026-10-18); a "6.7.14's Dn" is the 6.7.14 open's. Each item closes a "filed" line
of receives_and_multi_value_since_6_7_14.

A KNOWN `&f` CALLEE IS THE DIRECT CALL, ALL THE WAY (D30, D31; RC-9, RC-10). `fncallN(&f, ..)` / `callptr(&f, ..)` takes
`f`'s struct, `: f64`, `: *T` step AND element — read, written and subscripted at the element's width — and `: bool` (a
boolean value). Through a variable (`fncall0(fp)`) the callee is unknown: one untyped word. ⚠ A store `*fncall0(&bp) =
v` with `fn bp(): *u8` writes ONE byte since 6.7.15 (it wrote 8); to keep a word, bind the call untyped first
(`var w = fncall0(&bp);`) and dereference that.

A STRUCT RESULT OVER 8 B IS TAKEN ONLY BY ITS STRUCT (6.7.14's D27; D32, D33; RC-11, RC-12). Assigned (`x = mkp();`), it
goes into a variable of that struct (copied), a `*S` of it (copied through) or a handle. A scalar annotation, any OTHER
`*T` (it stored a wild pointer) and an UNTYPED variable — a for-header or destructure name and a closure's parameter
included (it took the first field) — are refused by name. 8 B or less and a `Str` keep their word. Bind the field you
meant (`x = mkp().a;`), or declare `var x = mkp();` (the struct).

AN UNTYPED GLOBAL FROM ANY WHOLE STRUCT-VALUED CALL IS THAT STRUCT (6.7.14's D29 / D30; D34, D35; RC-13, RC-14). A free
call since 6.7.14, and since 6.7.15 a method, an operator, a known `&f` and — in the declaration zone too — a generic's
instance: 8 B or less is the struct (`G + 1` dispatches its operator fn, and is refused without one — annotate `var G:
i64 = …` to keep a number; a bare `G` as a value is still its word), 9 – 16 B is refused (a struct result needs a fn's
frame: move it into a fn, or bind the field, `var G = x.m().a;`), over 16 B the call's own refusal. Exceptions that keep
the word: a `Str` call, a name — and in 6.7.15 an if-expression's struct, which binds since 6.7.16 (6.7.16's D16 (a),
ZN-9; receives_since_6_7_16).

A TUPLE PARAMETER TAKES A TUPLE'S ADDRESS (6.7.14's D44; D36, RC-3). An argument made only of literals and operators — a
negated literal (`take(-5)`), `true` / `false`, a constant expression — is refused by name (it crashed at run time). Any
name (a const too), `&t` or a call is taken as the address, unchecked. Pass a tuple: `take((a, b))`, a tuple variable,
or `&t`. A float literal still slipped through in 6.7.15; it is refused at the argument since 6.7.16 (6.7.16's D23,
RV-2).

A STRING LITERAL IS AN ADDRESS EVERYWHERE (6.7.14's D53; D59, RC-6, RC-7). Into ANY `*T` — a `*i64`, a one-byte struct's
`*B1` — it never draws "assigning non-pointer to typed pointer", nor do `"abc" + n`, `"abc" - n` or a wrapped
`("xyz")`. `(0)` still warned where `0` did not in 6.7.15; it is the null pointer since 6.7.16 (FE-17).

THE EVERY-PATH `: stack` RULE COVERS METHODS (RC-8). An `impl` method or an inherited trait default that returns a pair
on one path and a single value on another warns, as a fn does — the single value is read as the tag: return a variant on
that path (`Err(x)`, `None()`). The warning names the method as written (`Pt.qm`).

PASS 1'S VALUE COUNT TREATS `ret2(..);` AS `return (a, b);` (6.7.14's D42; D62, RC-1), a body ending in an infinite loop
left only through `ret2(..);` included: three names or a 3-tuple capture are refused (the third name read a stale
register). Bind two names, or declare `: (T1, T2, T3)` and return three. Right after a `case N:` / `default:` label it
was not yet in 6.7.15; it is since 6.7.16 (RV-12).

A WRAPPED `: Str` CALL GLOBAL IS ROUTED AS THE UNWRAPPED ONE (RC-4). A top-level `var s = (mks());` passed to an
overloaded base (`println`, `strlen`, any base with a `_str` sibling) reaches the `_str` sibling, as `var s = mks();`
always did (it printed the header's bytes); the global is still a word at top level. A `: Str` METHOD global was not
routed in 6.7.15; it is since 6.7.16 (ZN-10).
See CHANGELOG [6.7.15] (RC-1 .. RC-14, RC-5r, RC-13r, RC-14r) and docs/development/ecosystem-migration.md § 6.7.15;
6.7.16's receives changes: receives_since_6_7_16.

[[entries]]
name = "vector_values_since_6_7_15"
description = "cycc 6.7.15 (the vectors lane): a vector method on a field-chain receiver is the vector; `: f64` (or `f32`, `bool`, `cstring`, an enum, `Vec`, `Result`, `Option`) is not 'no annotation' for a vector call; parentheses do not hide a vector call (D28) and a method starting an integer initialiser is refused (D29); a vector call never initialises or assigns a non-vector global (D26, D27); `*(&p)` is `p` (D21); a wrapped or offset pointer argument and `***T` pick the generic's instance (D22 – D24); refusals quote what you wrote; an `#inline` call's argument temporaries stay in the frame. Cross-linked from vector_values_since_6_7_14."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.15", "SIMD", "vectors", "f64v2", "methods", "field chain", "parentheses", "globals", "top level", "*(&p)", "typed pointer", "generics", "***T", "#inline", "u128", "diagnostics", "D21", "D22", "D23", "D24", "D26", "D27", "D28", "D29"]
---
cycc 6.7.15 (language/features.cyml typed_simd_value_type_abi, generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310,
inline_directive_v6563, typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617). The decisions D21 – D24 and D26 – D29 are 6.7.15's (the user,
2026-10-18); 6.7.14's D31 – D41 are other questions. The 6.7.14 notes are vector_values_since_6_7_14.

A METHOD ON A FIELD CHAIN IS THE VECTOR (VC-4). `var v = w.s.mv();` with `mv` declared `: f64v2` is the vector's local:
through 6.7.14 it was one word of garbage, and the annotated, assigned, argument and return forms were refused "got
`w`". `var x: i64 = w.s.mv();` / `x = w.s.mv();` (and an f64, struct or pointer destination) are refused. A head that is
`(*ps)` or a call still bound a word in 6.7.15; since 6.7.16 a vector method is the vector on any receiver (RV-4).

`: f64` IS NOT "NO ANNOTATION" (VC-6). `var x: f64 = mkv(..);` is refused "vector return type does not match the
declared type"; through 6.7.14 `x` was silently the two-lane vector under an f64 label (`load64(&x + 8)` read lane 1).
The same for `f32`, `bool`, `cstring`, an enum, `Vec`, `Result` and `Option`. Declare the vector type, and read a lane
with `load64(&v)`.

PARENTHESES DO NOT HIDE A VECTOR CALL (D28, D29; VC-5, VC-7). `var x = (mkv(..));` / `(s.mv(..))` is the vector (it held
one word), and `var x: i64 = (mkv(..));`, `x = (mkv(..));`, `x = (s.mv(..));` are refused; a wrapped call of ANOTHER
vector type into a vector local is a type mismatch. A method starting an integer initialiser is refused too — `var x:
i64 = s.mv(..) + 1;`, `var x: i64 = callptr(&f, ..) + 1;` (D29). Not yet refused in 6.7.15, and wrong: `x = s.mv() +
1;`, `var x: i64 = (mkv(..)) + 1;` and `var w = mkv(..) + 1;` — each refused since 6.7.16 (6.7.16's D24 – D26, D100: a
vector call is no operand anywhere — vector_values_since_6_7_16).

A VECTOR CALL NEVER INITIALISES OR ASSIGNS A NON-VECTOR GLOBAL (D26, D27; VC-8, VC-9). `G = mkv(..);` in a fn (a method,
`fncallN(&f, ..)` and `callptr(&f, ..)` too), a top-level `var w = mkv(..);` / `var v = s.mv(..);` and a top-level
`var x: i64 = mkv(..);` are refused — the global held the result's first word, garbage on cx and Windows — and so is
`x = fncallN(&f, ..);` into a scalar LOCAL. Make the call in a fn and store the lanes: through a vector global's
address, or as scalars. A top-level `G = mkv(..);` after the first statement still stored a word in 6.7.15; it is
refused since 6.7.16 (ZN-13).

`*(&p)` IS `p` (D21, VC-13). Through a `*u8`, `**(&pb) = 0x1FF` writes ONE byte now — it wrote 8, clobbering the bytes
after the element — and `*(&p) + 1` steps an element, not a byte; an untyped `var v = **(&pv);` of a `*V` is the vector,
and `**(&ps)` of a `*S` the struct. For the old byte arithmetic write `load64(&p) + n`. `*(&pi) += 1` still stepped one
byte in 6.7.15; it steps an element since 6.7.16 (ST-10), and `*(&x)` is x for a narrow integer, an f64 / f32 / bool and
an inline `Str` too (typed_pointer_local_slot_v6616).

A WRAPPED OR OFFSET POINTER ARGUMENT PICKS THE GENERIC'S INSTANCE (D22 / D23, VC-14). Through a `*T` / `**T` parameter,
`g((ps))`, `g(ps + 1)`, `g(n + ps)`, `g(ps - n)` and `tg(pps + 0)` run the S instance: `sizeof(T)`, the step, `*p`'s
width, a `var x: T` and a `: T` result change (they ran the i64 base, and `tg((pps))`'s `take(**pp)` faulted). Spell
`g<i64>(..)` for the old base. `&s + n` is a byte pointer and binds nothing.

`***T` CARRIES T (D24, VC-15). `s3(ppps)` with `ppps: ***P3` runs the P3 instance, as `s3<P3>(ppps)` does (an i8 / i16
/ i32 / f64 pointee its own) — through any deeper `*..*T` too; it ran the i64 base.

REFUSALS QUOTE WHAT YOU WROTE (VC-10, VC-12). A dereference argument is '*gp(pb)', not '*pb'; a method result at a
struct destination is 'Pt.dup', not 'Pt_dup'; a top-level instance is 'mkg<i32>', not 'mkg$i32'. A script grepping the
old text re-matches.

AN `#inline` CALL'S ARGUMENT TEMPORARIES STAY IN THE CALLER'S FRAME (VC-1, the release's other High). Two `#inline`
calls in one expression with u128 arguments (`id(a) + id(a + 1) + 2`) read 84 for 83 on x86_64, aarch64 and Windows (43
or 84 in a closure), and so did a `*S` dereference before a struct operator: a frame temporary taken while the inline
fn's arguments ran lay past the frame. Right since 6.7.15, and a refused `#inline` argument prints once (VC-2).
See CHANGELOG [6.7.15] (VC-1 .. VC-15, VC-1r, VC-6r) and docs/development/ecosystem-migration.md § 6.7.15; 6.7.16's
vector changes: vector_values_since_6_7_16.

[[entries]]
name = "a_zone_redeclaration_is_its_last_constant_from_program_start_v6715"
description = "Since cycc 6.7.15 (D2 (a)) a declaration-zone global redeclared with a constant is that LAST constant from program start — before its later declaration too — however either declaration spells it (`= 5`, `= C`, `= E.B`, `= 0`): a read between the two sees the later constant, and a call between them that writes the global keeps its write. Since 6.7.16 a float constant and a struct / tuple literal of constants redeclare the same way (D13 (a), D14 (a)), a byte list over a named or float constant keeps the bytes it does not list, and f64 arithmetic with an integer RIGHT operand is in the image (D15 (a)) — computed on the integer's bits, so `1.5 + 1` is 1.5. A bool keeps its startup store."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.15", "v6.7.16", "globals", "redeclaration", "declaration zone", "constant initializer", "const", "enum", "baked", "struct literal", "tuple literal", "byte list", "f64", "subnormal", "ADR-002", "kernel", "D2", "D46", "D13", "D14", "D15", "D86"]
---
    const C = 5; const D = 3; fn setg() { G = 9; }
    var a = D; var b = a; var a = C;          # 6.7.15: b == 5 (C) — through 6.7.14: b == 3 (D, declaration order)
    var G = 0; var r = setg(); var G = C;     # 6.7.15: G == 9 (setg's write kept) — through 6.7.14: G == 5

THE RULE (6.7.15's D2 (a), FE-13). In the declaration zone, a global declared twice where the later declaration is a
constant — a literal, a const or enum name, an expression of them (`A + 1`), `= 0` — is that later constant FROM PROGRAM
START, whatever the earlier declaration was: an initializer that reads the global between the two declarations reads
the later constant, and a fn called from one of them that writes the global keeps its write (no startup store resets
it). A literal pair already behaved so (`var a = 5; var b = a; var a = 7;` gives b == 7, the guide's example); through
6.7.14 a NAMED constant kept declaration order instead, and so did an earlier computed initializer before a named
constant (`var a = f(); var b = a; var a = C;` gave b == f(); f still runs, into a sink, and b is C now). A program
that reads the global between two such declarations reads a different value now. An earlier constant declaration leaves
no startup code. Two DIFFERENT constants warn: a_same_value_redeclaration_is_silent_by_design_v6712 (a named one is
compared since 6.7.15, FE-14).

EXCEPTIONS TO KNOW (6.7.15; the first two closed in 6.7.16, below):
  - A FLOAT-literal redeclaration kept declaration order in 6.7.15 (a read between saw the earlier float; a call's write
    was overwritten) and was never compared — D2 named integer constants.
  - A redeclared STRUCT / TUPLE literal of OTHER bytes kept declaration order in 6.7.15: `var p = P{1,2}; var b = p.a;
    var p = P{3,4};` gave b == 1, and `var t = (1, 2); var b = t.0; var t = (3, 4);` b == 1.
  - A `bool` global keeps its startup store (its value is judged there), and so does an initializer naming another
    file's private const (refused there).

6.7.16 — FLOATS, LITERALS AND LISTS FOLLOW THE RULE (6.7.16's D13 (a), D14 (a), D86 (a); ZN-1 – ZN-3, ZN-1r, SS-37):
  - A FLOAT constant redeclaration is the global's value from program start, as an integer's is (D13 (a)): `var G: f64 =
    1.5; var b = load64(&G); var G: f64 = 2.5;` gives b 2.5's bits; an earlier float constant leaves no startup store,
    a call between keeps its write, the constant f64 expressions 6.7.15 bakes and an f32 pair behave the same, and two
    DIFFERING float constants now warn (often two files' defaults, `= 0` and `= 1.5`). A computed float redeclaration
    still runs in order; a list before a float constant redeclaration of the slot is not laid down.
  - A STRUCT / TUPLE literal of constants that redeclares a global is its value from program start (D14 (a)): the two
    examples above give b == 3; a call that writes it before or between keeps its write (`{9, 4}`, not `{3, 4}`); every
    spelling (tuple, named form, generic literal, wrap) the same. An earlier COMPUTED initializer still runs, its result
    discarded (read with 6.7.16's D45); a later computed literal runs in declaration order. No new warning (the compare
    stays scalar).
  - A BYTE LIST OVER A CONSTANT KEEPS THE BYTES IT DOES NOT LIST (ZN-1, SS-37). The list's seed is the slot's last
    constant before it, however spelled — a literal, a const or enum name, an untyped float — so `const C = 0x0506; var
    a = C; var a[1] = {9};` reads 0x0509 (6.7.15: 0x0009), `var a = 0x0506; var a = D; var a[1] = {9};` with `D =
    0x0708` 0x0709 (6.7.15: the stale literal's 0x0509), and `var K = 1.5; var K[1] = {9};` 0x3FF8000000000009 (6.7.15:
    0x0000000000000009; D86 (a)). A computed initializer in between is passed over, and a const between two lists
    seeds the later one as a literal does (`var a[1] = {7}; var a = C; var a[1] = {9};` reads 0x0509).

A SINGLE ALL-CONSTANT LITERAL OR f64 EXPRESSION IS IN THE IMAGE FROM PROGRAM START TOO.
  - An all-constant struct / tuple literal (`var p = P { 1, 2 };`, named, nested, generic, `(1, 2)`) was baked into the
    image (6.6.16) AND stored again at startup, element by element; since 6.7.15 the stores are gone (FE-15, 6.7.14's
    D11 (b)). So an EARLIER initializer's call that writes the global keeps its write: `var r = setp(); var p = P { 1, 2
    };`, setp storing 9 into `p.a`, ends with `p.a == 9` (it was 1).
  - A zone float global whose initializer is a CONSTANT f64 expression — `var G: f64 = CF;`, `= (1.5)`, `= 1.5 + 1.0`,
    `= -CF`, an untyped `var K = CF;`, an f32's binary32 rounding — is baked as a float literal is (FE-16; 6.7.15's D46
    kept 6.7.14's bake): an earlier initializer reads its value (it read 0), and an x86 `kernel;` build reads it without
    the late-initializer warning. A call, a NaN (`0.0 / 0.0`) and an integer or enum operand (`1.5 + 1`, warned as a
    mix) stayed startup stores in 6.7.15 — late, and named, in a `kernel;` build.
  - Since 6.7.16 f64 arithmetic with an integer or enum RIGHT operand — `var M: f64 = 1.5 + 1;`, `= 1.5 * E.A`, `= 1.5 *
    A` (a bare enum member), `= 1.5 + C` (an integer const) — is in the image from program start too (6.7.16's D15 (a),
    ZN-4): an x86 `kernel;` program reads it (the filed repro exits 100, not 1), an earlier initializer sees it, an
    earlier call's write is kept. An integer LEFT operand (`1 + 1.5`) and a NaN stay startup stores (late in a `kernel;`
    build).
  ⚠ `1.5 + 1` IN f64 ARITHMETIC IS 1.5. The integer is its raw BITS (ADR-002: everything is i64), and 1 read as bits is
  the least subnormal; the bake reproduces the run time bit for bit (`1.5 * 3` is the subnormal whose bits are 4), and
  the warning "f64 arithmetic with a non-f64 right operand" is the hint — write `1.5 + 1.0`. `1 + 1.5` is integer
  arithmetic on the float's bits.
global_init_order_silent_zero (6.7.14's constant-initializer rule). CHANGELOG [6.7.15] FE-13, FE-15, FE-16; [6.7.16]
ZN-1 – ZN-4, ZN-1r, SS-37.

[[entries]]
name = "generics_since_6_7_16"
description = "cycc 6.7.16 (the generic signatures, generic binding, f16 / f16b and s16 lanes): a type-argument list no longer than its generic declares (D5), at most two type parameters (D8), `Name<..>` only on a generic struct in every position (D7, D93), a list judged by what the name declares (D88); a trait member no impl can define refused at the trait (D9), a dyn formed from an impl that spells a type another way, an untyped `self` a `*T` in either order (D6); `return &n` of a T value or a stored struct result makes a generic struct-only (D1), `return &n` of a non-T name refused at the definition (D2), the explicit `v.m<..>(..)` held to the bound (D4); a captured f64 binds f64 (D3), `pp: **Box<T>` infers T, a pointer to another instance refused at the call (D97), an untyped `var y = x;` from a T value is T (D102), a generic operator fn binds as its call does (D85 (a)). Cross-linked from generics_since_6_7_15."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.16", "generics", "type arguments", "type parameters", "traits", "impl", "dyn", "self", "trait bounds", "stub", "return &", "closures", "capture", "f64", "**T", "operators", "multi-value", "D1", "D2", "D3", "D4", "D5", "D6", "D7", "D8", "D9", "D10", "D85", "D88", "D91", "D92", "D93", "D97", "D102"]
---
cycc 6.7.16 (language/features.cyml generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310, nested_generic_type_arguments_v6617,
traits_checked_v670, trait_bounds_v671). The decisions are 6.7.16's (the user, 2026-10-19 / 2026-10-20); a "6.7.15's Dn"
is the 6.7.15 open's. The 6.7.15 notes are generics_since_6_7_15.

THE TYPE-ARGUMENT LIST (D5, D7, D8, D10, D88, D92, D93; GS-7 – GS-12, GS-14, FF-42, FF-45, FF-49):
  - LONGER THAN THE GENERIC DECLARES: refused at the first extra argument, "'Box' takes 1 type argument, 2 given" (D5 —
    the extra was dropped in silence), the line reading as if the extra were deleted.
  - A THIRD TYPE PARAMETER: refused at its declaration, a fn, a struct, a method, and a trait member's own (D8, D92) —
    through 6.7.15 C was bound to nothing (`sizeof(C)` 8 for a 24-byte struct). Pass a struct holding the rest.
  - `Name<..>` ONLY ON A GENERIC STRUCT, EVERYWHERE (D7, D93): 6.7.15's D6 left a pointer field `*Pt<i64>`, a trait
    member's parameter or return, a multi-value element, a `*fn(..)` type, `**Pt<i64>`, `*i32<u8>`, a union's by-value
    field and a slice's pointer element compiling with the list ignored; each is refused at the name, and so is a tuple
    element's scalar (`(i64<u8>, i64)`, D93). `cyrius lint` leaves the by-value forms to the compile.
  - A LIST IS JUDGED BY WHAT THE NAME DECLARES (D88): an enum without `<..>`, `Option`, `Tagged` and `cstring` take
    none; `Result<..>` is read — `fn g(): Result<i64, Pt>` compiles (diagnostics_caps.cyml type_name_refusals_v6616).
  - A BOUND ON A GENERIC STRUCT'S PARAMETER is never met by another generic struct's instance — no `impl Trait for
    Box<R>` can be written — in a `Vec` element too (D10, GS-9).

TRAITS AND IMPLS (D6, D9; GS-1 – GS-5, GS-13, FF-40, FF-41):
  - A MEMBER NO IMPL CAN DEFINE is refused at the trait, once, with a fn's words: `: u8` .. `: u64`, `: f32`, an enum
    ("type 'u8' cannot be a fn return type"), `: *fn( .. )` / `: **fn( .. )` ("a fn-pointer type `*fn( .. )` cannot be a
    fn return type") — declare `: i64` or a pointer, and its impls with it (D9). A fn's own `: *fn( .. )` return says
    the same since 6.7.16 (FF-40; it was "expected identifier, got reserved keyword 'fn'").
  - A member's own `<U>` after a `*fn(..)` parameter, a `cb: *fn(i64): u128` parameter and a bare `*fn` parameter
    compile (GS-3, GS-4; D91).
  - A DYN FORMS from an impl whose method spells a member's type another way (`Box<i64>` / `Box`, `> >` / `>>`,
    `*Box<i64>` / `*Box`) — "spell the impl's return as the member does" is no longer needed (GS-2) — and an impl's
    mismatch no longer hides the next one (GS-1).
  - WITH `impl T { .. }` ABOVE `struct T`, an untyped `self` is a `*T` (D6, GS-13): `self.b` compiles (it was "no struct
    type in scope for 'self'"), `T_m(t)` passes `&t` (it passed t's value and faulted), and arithmetic on it is refused,
    as with the struct first.

WHAT A BODY NEEDS (D1, D2; GB-19, GB-19r, GB-20, SS-42, SS-46, SS-47). A GENERIC WHOSE BODY ONLY WORKS WHEN T IS A
STRUCT HAS NO SCALAR INSTANCE, and three lines now say so — a `.field` on a T value (since 6.6.10), `return &n` of a T
value in a fn returning a struct, and a struct result over 8 B stored in a T-typed name (`var y: T = ..`, `y = ..`, `p =
..` into a `*T` / `**T`, and through one, `*p = mkp();` — 6.7.16's D99). `h(3)`, `g(5)`, `g<i32>(5)` and `&h` are
refused at the call, "generic 'h' has no i64 (or other scalar) instance", with a `note:` naming the line (every stub
refusal carries the note now); through 6.7.15 they returned a dead slot's address or kept the struct's first word. Bind
a field (`var y: T = mkp().a;`) to make the body scalar-safe. And `return &n;` of a name NOT typed by T is refused at
the generic's definition, called or not (D2), as in a plain fn.

WHAT AN ARGUMENT BINDS (D3, D85 (a), D97; GB-11, GB-15, GB-18, GB-21, GB-22, SS-41, SS-43):
  - A CAPTURED f64 binds T = f64 (D3): arithmetic on `|| gid(h)` is float arithmetic (it ran the i64 base on the
    double's bits). A captured f32 still binds i64 (f32 is no type argument).
  - `pp: **Box<T>` INFERS T from every `**Box<X>` source and from `&` of a `*Box<X>` (GB-15; it ran the i64 base,
    reading `Box<i64>`'s offsets); a `*Box<X>` given to `**Box<T>` binds nothing.
  - A generic's base reads its signature's instance: `b: Box<Pt>` given the base `Box` or a `Box<i64>` BY VALUE is
    refused (GB-18), and a POINTER to another instance — `*Box<i64>` into `b: *Box<Pt>`, in any fn — is refused at the
    argument since D97 (SS-43: struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_16).
  - `gg(*p)` with `p: *Str` at T = Str passes the header p points at (GB-21; it passed the data word).
  - A MULTI-VALUE ELEMENT TYPED BY T IS THE INSTANCE'S: `var a, b = mm2<f64>(h);` makes a an f64; capture it into `(f64,
    i64)` — `(i64, i64)` is refused now (GB-22).
  - A GENERIC OPERATOR FN IS A GENERIC CALL (D85 (a), SS-41): T binds from the operands as from the call's arguments
    (left = 0, right = 1), so `b + p` runs `P1_add$Pt` and its result is the instance's type; only a whole operand binds
    — `b + w.s` runs the base.
  - IN A GENERIC, `var y = x;` IS T-TYPED (D102; FB-2, FB-2r, FB-2r2): `var y = x; return y.b;` works at a struct T,
    through a chain of untyped copies; at a scalar T the generic has no instance (a scalar call refused by name, the
    note at `y.b`). `var y = p;` with `p: *T`, `var y = x + 1;` and `var y = x.f;` stay untyped — annotate (`var y: *T =
    p;`). An untyped local from a CARRIER of T by value (`var y = b;`, `b: Box<T>`) is that carrier (beyond D102's
    text). Shadowing is honoured: under `|x|`, `for x in` or `var q, x = f();`, `var y = x;` is the new binding's. ⚠
    Open (staged for the user's decision): a generic's own `x: T` parameter REBOUND by `|x|`, `for x in` or a
    destructure is still read as T by `x.show()` / `x.b` directly — a bounded `x.show()` there SIGSEGVs at a struct T.
    Do not reuse a type-parameter-typed parameter's name for a closure parameter, loop variable or destructure name.

THE BOUND'S CONTRACT (D4; GB-3, GB-4, GB-13). `v.other<i64>(1)` — a method no trait of the bound declares, with explicit
type arguments, on a bounded T or a carrier's T field — is refused at the definition, called or not, as `v.other(1)` is
(D4; it called the instance's own method); a bound's own generic member called so (`v.show<i64>(1)`) compiles at a
scalar T too. ⚠ `v.other<Nope>(1)` with Nope declared nowhere is no type list: an uncalled such generic compiles, and
every call is refused. A bounded generic's closure calling the bound's member on a captured T at a scalar T, and `self`
captured in a trait default for a scalar type, compile (GB-13). Every bound-contract refusal of a compile prints (GB-3).

A FORWARDING CHAIN of generics compiles to ~300 links (it was refused at ~122): diagnostics_caps.cyml
live_compiler_capacity_ceilings (GB-2).
See CHANGELOG [6.7.16] (GS-1 .. GS-18, GS-5r, GS-7r, GS-11r, GS-13r; GB-1 .. GB-24, GB-5r, GB-13r, GB-19r; FF-40 –
FF-42, FF-45, FF-49; FB-2, FB-2r, FB-2r2; SS-41 – SS-43, SS-46) and docs/development/ecosystem-migration.md § 6.7.16.

[[entries]]
name = "struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_16"
description = "cycc 6.7.16 (the structs, declaration-zone, s16, f16 and f16b lanes): `o.s = *pp` copies into a field; a `Str` handle is rebound and an inline `Str` copied from every `Str`-valued source, `*p` of a `*Str` is p in every `Str` / `*Str` slot (D20, D73, D74), a frame's inline `Str` never goes into a field or through a pointer (D19), `*(&t)` is t (D71); `*(&x)` is x for a narrow integer, f64, f32 or bool (D21, D70), a narrow parameter is narrowed (D72), a signed narrow global after the first statement reads signed; a top-level `var G: S = *GPP;` / `= P;` copies, its source declared above (D17, D18); a `*U` into an S and a pointer to another struct into a `*S` are refused (D87, D97); a `.field` needs a struct (D68); `**B` fields may point below (D103); a capture shadows a same-named global (D96). Cross-linked from struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_15."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.16", "structs", "Str", "inline Str", "*Str", "handle", "*pp", "*(&x)", "narrow integers", "parameters", "f64", "bool", "globals", "top level", "copy-init", "pointer arguments", ".field", "**T", "closures", "capture", "D17", "D18", "D19", "D20", "D21", "D68", "D70", "D71", "D72", "D73", "D74", "D87", "D96", "D97", "D103"]
---
cycc 6.7.16 (language/features.cyml struct_handle_line_and_struct_values_at_every_size_v6710,
struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711, typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617, closures_v637_v638). The
decisions are 6.7.16's (the user, 2026-10-19 / 2026-10-20). The 6.7.15 notes are
struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_15.

STRUCT COPIES (ST-1, ST-6). `o.s = *pp` (a struct field, pp a `**S`) COPIES the struct (it stored the pointer into the
field's first word); another struct's `*qq` is refused by name — for a pointer in the struct, declare a `*S` field.
`fncall0(&gp)` / `callptr(&gp)` is `gp()` everywhere: a known callee's `*T` result copies into a struct destination (it
stored the pointer word), and `var t: P2 = *fncall0(&gp);` no longer faults. Through a callee held in a variable it is
still a word.

`Str` (D19, D20, D71, D73, D74; ST-2 – ST-5, SS-8 – SS-14):
  - A `Str` HANDLE IS REBOUND, AN INLINE `Str` IS COPIED, FROM EVERY `Str`-VALUED SOURCE (D20). `h = *p` makes h the
    header p points at — a write through h shows through p; for a copy, `h = str_clone(p);` — and `t = *pp` into an
    inline t copies the header (it stored its address in t.data). `var u: Str = *pp;` is u's own copy (D74).
  - `*p` OF A `*Str` IS p, THE HEADER'S ADDRESS, wherever a `Str` handle or a `*Str` slot takes it — a `Str` field, a
    literal's `Str` field, a `: Str` return (SS-8), a `*Str` local, parameter, global, declaration, field, literal
    element, `s: *Str` argument, `return *p;` from a `: *Str` fn (D73, SS-11) — no copy, no allocation; each took the
    data word. `*q = *p` does NOT copy the header: write `q.data = p.data; q.len = p.len;`.
  - A FRAME'S INLINE `Str` NEVER GOES INTO A FIELD OR THROUGH A POINTER (D19, ST-4, SS-9, SS-14): `w.p = t` into a
    `*Str` field (a local struct's too), `*pp = t`, a `*Str` literal element (`W { t, 1 }`) and `return t;` from a `:
    *Str` fn are refused as would-dangle, as `v.s = t` into a `Str` field always was. Store `str_new(t.data, t.len)`, or
    point at an inline `Str` GLOBAL (`w.p = gt` takes gt's address).
  - `*(&t)` / `*&t` OF AN INLINE `Str` IS t (D71, SS-13): a handle on t, its header passed, a copy into an inline `Str`;
    `*(&t) = v` copies the header (it wrote one word) and `*(&t) OP= v` is refused; a frame's t keeps every would-dangle
    refusal through it. ⚠ A closure's CAPTURED inline `Str`'s `*(&t) = v` still writes one word into the capture's copy.
  - `var k = h.clone();` IN A FN IS A `Str` (ST-5): `k.len()` works, and `println(k)` / any `f` with an `f_str` overload
    takes the string overload (it printed the header's bytes). At top level annotate it: `var K: Str = H.clone();`. A
    `: Str` method's untyped global reaches the `_str` overloads (ZN-10).
  - A `Str` handle's name, a `: Str` call or field into a `*Str` no longer warns "assigning non-pointer to typed
    pointer" (SS-12, FE-18, FF-22).
  ⚠ A nested literal's first leaf, `O { *p, 1, 2 }` with `struct O { v: WS; m; }`, still carries the header's data word
  into `v.s`.

`*(&x)` IS x, AND A NARROW VALUE KEEPS ITS WIDTH (D21, D70, D72; ST-10, ST-11, SS-15 – SS-19):
  - `*(&x)` is x for every x the compiler types: a narrow integer (`*(&a)` of an i32 -1 is -1, `*(&GQ) = 7` writes 4
    bytes — it zeroed the next global), an f64 / f32 / bool (D70: `*(&f) + 1.0` is a float add), an inline `Str` (D71) —
    on the read, the store and as a dyn source; `*(&pi) += 1` steps an element (ST-10). For the 8-byte word say
    `load64(&x)` / `store64(&x, v)`. A capture keeps its env word; `*(&x + 0)` is a byte-pointer read
    (typed_pointer_local_slot_v6616).
  - A NARROW PARAMETER IS NARROWED LIKE A LOCAL (D72, SS-16): the caller's word is not the value — `fn wid(y: u8)` given
    0x1FF sees 255 (it held 511), an i16 32767 `+= 1` wraps to -32768, and `z = y` into a narrower variable warns
    "narrowing assignment". The argument still passes whole (the ABI is unchanged): the callee narrows. Declare `i64` to
    keep the word. A generic `x: T` at i8 / i16 / i32 is narrowed too. ⚠ A SUSPENDING `async fn`'s narrow parameter
    still holds the caller's word (open).
  - A SIGNED NARROW GLOBAL DECLARED AFTER THE FIRST TOP-LEVEL STATEMENT reads sign-extended (SS-15): through 6.7.15 a
    program with `main();` before `var GS: i16 = 0 - 2;` read 65534.

TOP-LEVEL STRUCT COPIES (D17 (a), D18 (a), D87 (a); ZN-11, ZN-12, SS-38, SS-39, SS-39r):
  - `var G: S = *GPP;` (GPP a `**S`) and `var G: S = P;` (P a `*S` global's name) are COPIES at every size, in the
    declaration zone and after the first statement, as in a fn — `(*GPP)`, `*gpp()`, `*W.pp`, `(P)` too. Through 6.7.15
    G held the pointer word (8 B or less: `N8.v` printed an address) or was a handle on the object (over 8 B). A write
    through the source is no longer seen through G: to alias, write `var G: *S = P;`.
  - DECLARE THE SOURCE ABOVE G, AT EVERY SIZE (SS-38): a source declared below whose value is stored at run time (a
    pointer global's `&X`, a call, a literal with a computed field) is refused by name, "cannot copy-init 'A' from a
    global declared below it (declare the source first)" — it faulted at startup or read 0. A literal of constants below
    is fine (it is in the image from program start).
  - Another struct's `**Q` (`var G: S = *GQQ;`) is refused, and a `*U` global, call, method or field into a struct `S`
    declaration is refused at every size (D87): write `var t: *U = GU;` (an alias), `var t: U = GU;` (a copy), or fix
    the type.

A POINTER TO ANOTHER STRUCT INTO A `*S` / `**S` PARAMETER IS REFUSED AT THE ARGUMENT (D97, SS-43): `nc(&q)` of another
struct, a `*Box<i32>` where `*Box<i64>` is declared, `**Qd` into `**Pt` — "cannot pass 'y' to a parameter of a different
struct type in a call to 'nc'" (they read the wrong layout silently). Pass a `*S` aimed at the field you mean (`var p:
*S = &c;`). An untyped pointer, `alloc()`, `0` and a generic's own binding still pass, and a typed pointer still
converts into a BY-VALUE struct parameter (6.7.3). ⚠ A pointer of another depth (`**Pt` into `p: *Pt`) still passes
unchecked.

A `.field` NEEDS A STRUCT (D68; FF-4, FE-5): `.field`, a field store and `.m()` on a global declared i8 .. u32, f64 or
f32 are refused with the local's words, "no struct type in scope for 'g'" (`g.d` on an i32 read the program's 4th
struct's `d`; an f64's crashed the compiler), and on a `*T` global whose T is no struct (`var x: *i64 = &g; x.a` read
through the program's first struct). Annotate the struct (`var x: *P`) or read explicitly (`load64(x)`).

`**B` FIELDS MAY POINT BELOW (D103, FB-3): `struct A { p: **B; } struct B { .. }` compiles, at any depth (`**Box<B>`
too); only a by-value field needs its struct declared above. And a `**G<..>` field mints its instance whole (GB-14):
where such a field named `G<X>` first, `sizeof(G<X>)` read a half-built size (`sizeof(Box<S16>)` 16, now 24).

CLOSURES (D96; FB-1, GB-9, GB-10, GB-13):
  - A CAPTURE SHADOWS A SAME-NAMED GLOBAL FOR `.field` / `.m()` TOO (D96): with a struct global `x`, a closure capturing
    an untyped local `x` is refused "no struct type in scope for 'x'" at `x.a` — through 6.7.15 it silently read the
    GLOBAL's field. Rename one, or type the local.
  - An untyped capture's `.field`, store or `.m()` is the untyped local's refusal (it was "undefined variable 'x'";
    GB-13); a closure's parameter is untyped whatever an earlier fn left in its frame slot (GB-9), and a capturing
    closure's first parameter no longer takes the enclosing fn's struct type (GB-10). To read fields through a closure
    parameter, name it typed in the body (`var p: *Q = x;` — it warns "assigning non-pointer to typed pointer").

A REDEFINED FN'S CALLS are typed by its last definition (SS-3, SS-4): last_definition_wins_reaches_the_stdlibs_own_
callers_v6714. Refusals quote a dereference as written ('*gps(&s)', '*h.pu', '*qq'; ST-12) and a refused `l = *pq` is
one error (ST-13): diagnostics_caps.cyml.
See CHANGELOG [6.7.16] (ST-1 .. ST-13, ST-12r; SS-3, SS-4, SS-8 – SS-19, SS-38, SS-39, SS-39r, SS-43; ZN-10 – ZN-12;
FE-5, FF-4, FF-22; FB-1, FB-3; GB-9, GB-10, GB-13, GB-14) and docs/development/ecosystem-migration.md § 6.7.16.

[[entries]]
name = "receives_since_6_7_16"
description = "cycc 6.7.16 (the receives, declaration-zone, s16, s16b, f16 and f16b lanes): a tuple parameter's refusal names the value, and a float into one is refused (D23); one error for a bad `return` value; `ret2(..);` after a `case` label counts; a struct result over 8 B never lands in a scalar or pointer slot, a store through a pointer included (D24 / D27 / D32 / D99, one rule); a multi-value method on a chain destructures, propagates and captures, and its pair bound one-wide is refused; a 16-byte struct is no multi-value element (D90); an untyped top-level global from a generic call, a generic method, an if-expression of struct branches or an f64 call is that struct / f64 (D16, D98). Cross-linked from receives_since_6_7_15."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.16", "receives", "tuple parameter", "return", "ret2", "case", "struct results", "*p", "multi-value", "chains", "methods", ": stack", "?", "globals", "top level", "declaration zone", "if expression", "f64", "D16", "D23", "D24", "D27", "D32", "D90", "D98", "D99", "D104"]
---
cycc 6.7.16 (language/features.cyml ret2_rethi, tuples_v677, struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711,
stack_enum_unboxed_payload_variants_v6555). The decisions are 6.7.16's (the user, 2026-10-19 / 2026-10-20); a "6.7.14's
Dn" is the 6.7.14 open's. The 6.7.15 notes are receives_since_6_7_15.

A TUPLE PARAMETER'S REFUSAL NAMES THE VALUE (D23; RV-1 – RV-3, RV-2r). "A bool" for a comparison, `!x`, `&&` / `||`; "an
f64" for anything the compiler types f64; "an integer" once an integer operator applies — `take(2 +| 1.5)` is "an
integer" since the saturating operators are integer (D104). A float literal is no tuple: it is refused at the argument
(it compiled and crashed). It is still not a check of names — `take(F)` with an `f64` const, or `take(x * 2.0)`, passes
the word as the address.

ONE ERROR FOR A BAD `return` VALUE (RV-10, RV-10r, RV-11, RV-15, RV-16): in a struct or vector fn, `return zz;`,
`return (zz, 1);` and `ret2(zz, 1);` report the value's own error; the class rule speaks only when the value parses.

A `ret2(..);` RIGHT AFTER A `case` / `default` LABEL IS A STATEMENT (RV-12; 6.7.14's D42): it counts toward the fn's
value count, so `var a, b, c = g2(1);` of a two-value fn is refused there too. Bind two names.

A STRUCT RESULT OVER 8 B NEVER LANDS IN A SCALAR OR POINTER SLOT, WHATEVER THE SPELLING (6.7.11's D24, 6.7.14's D27,
6.7.15's D32, 6.7.16's D99 — one rule; SS-46, SS-46r). Since 6.7.16 the store THROUGH a pointer is refused too: `*p =
mkp();` and `p[0] = mkp();` with `p: *i64` (any scalar or pointer pointee) — "cannot assign '*p: i64' from 'mkp', which
returns the struct 'Q' - store it through a '*Q' or bind a field" (it stored Q's first word). Bind the field you want
(`*p = mkp().a`) or store through a `*Q`; 8 B results and `Str` keep their word. ⚠ Still the first word, in silence:
`*p = mkp();` through an UNTYPED pointer, and `w.n = mkp();` / `buf[0] = mkp();` into a scalar field or array element
(staged for the user's decision).

A MULTI-VALUE METHOD ON A CHAIN (SB-1 – SB-3, SB-1r – SB-1r4, SB-4r, SB-4r2; pair_callee_any_spelling_6_6_16):
  - It is a destructure, re-assignment, tuple-capture and dyn source (it was refused) and its `?` propagates (it
    crashed): `var c, d = w.p.m2();`, `t = (*pw).p.m2();`, `return w.p.q16();` — the old workaround `var p = w.p; var c,
    d = p.m2();` can go.
  - A `: stack` pair on a chain bound one-wide (`var v = w.p.rs(3);`, `x = ..`, a field, `store64`) is refused "bind
    both" — it kept the tag in silence; the same after a generic step (`w.p.idv(q).rs(3)`), on a generic call head
    (`gw(2).p.rs(3)`), and through a forwarder below its caller.
  - It is not a struct: `hh.s = w.p.m2();`, `return w.p.m2();` from a `: Q8` fn, an async fn's `return w.p.m2();` and
    `take(callptr(&f, ..).p.m2())` compiled and kept the first value (or crashed); each is refused — bind `var a, b =
    w.p.m2();` and build the struct.
  - A chain's struct over 8 B returns, and passes by value from a `callptr` head (it pushed a word as the address).
  ⚠ A generic step whose argument is itself a generic call has no struct (unchanged): `q.idv(q.idv(q)).m2()` is "needs a
  call" — annotate the inner result (`var r: P = q.idv(q);`, then `q.idv(r).m2()`).

A 16-BYTE STRUCT IS NOT A MULTI-VALUE ELEMENT (D90; FF-46, FF-52, FB-5). `return (b, 5);` with `b: Pt` (16 B) is "'mv'
returns struct 'Pt' (16 bytes) as a multi-value element - return its pointer, or its fields as elements" — through
6.7.15 it returned `b.x` only, and a caller passing the binding on as a `Pt` faulted. Return `&b` (declare the element
`*Pt`) or the fields (`return (b.x, b.y, 5);`). An 8-byte struct, and a HANDLE named whole (`var q: Pt = alloc(16);`, a
pointer-mode global), are one word each and stay elements.

AN UNTYPED TOP-LEVEL GLOBAL TAKES ITS SOURCE'S TYPE (D16 (a), D98; ZN-5 – ZN-7, ZN-9, ZN-10, SS-30 – SS-35, SS-45).
6.7.15's rule — an untyped global from a whole struct-valued call is that struct — reaches, in the declaration zone too:
a generic call whatever carries its type argument (`var x = mkg1(30 + 3);`), a generic method's generic struct result
(`var y = b.mkg(P);`), an if-expression of struct branches of 8 B or less (`var G = if (..) { B1 } else { B1 };`, D16
(a); a 16-byte branch is refused naming the struct), and a `: Str` method's result (routed to the `_str` overloads). So
`x + 1` there DISPATCHES the struct's operator fn and is refused "undefined function 'G1_add'" without one — through
6.7.15 it added 1 to the word. Bind the field, or annotate `: i64`. An untyped global from an f64 call is an f64 at top
level as in a fn, the zone included, and so is a zone destructure's f64 element (D98): `Y * 2.0` is a float multiply (it
was 0, warned) and `Y + 1` adds as an f64 and warns; for the bits read `load64(&Y)` or annotate `: i64`. ⚠ A zone global
from a generic call whose return is a NESTED instance (`: G1<Box<T>>`) is still unbound in the zone ("no struct type in
scope") — make it after the first statement or in a fn.

`+| +? -| -? *| *?` give an integer whatever their right operand (D104): struct_values_and_receives_since_6_7_13. A
`: stack` pair fn through `callptr(&f, ..)` is still no pair: parser_syntax.cyml
parentheses_pair_calls_and_callptr_forms_ v6713. A struct global declared from a source below it:
struct_values_and_inline_str_since_6_7_16. See CHANGELOG [6.7.16] (RV-1 .. RV-16, RV-2r, RV-10r; SB-1 .. SB-7, SB-1r ..
SB-1r4, SB-4r, SB-4r2; SS-30 – SS-35, SS-45, SS-46, SS-46r; ZN-5 – ZN-10; FF-46, FF-52; FB-4, FB-5) and
docs/development/ecosystem-migration.md § 6.7.16.

[[entries]]
name = "vector_values_since_6_7_16"
description = "cycc 6.7.16 (the receives, declaration-zone and s16 lanes): there is no vector `+`, anywhere — a vector call is never an operand, a non-vector fn's return, a word or f64 argument, a destructure source or a non-vector global's top-level assignment (D24 – D26, D100, D101, 6.7.14's D27 (a) / D38); a vector method is the vector on any receiver; parentheses are transparent at a vector call that starts a source. Cross-linked from vector_values_since_6_7_15."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.16", "SIMD", "vectors", "f64v2", "operators", "methods", "parentheses", "return", "arguments", "destructure", "globals", "top level", "Windows", "cx", "D24", "D25", "D26", "D100", "D101"]
---
    var w = mk2(41, 7) + 1;          # 6.7.16: refused — 6.7.15: dropped the `+ 1`, or added it to one lane on cx
    var x: i64 = 1 + mk2(41, 7);     # 6.7.16: refused — 6.7.15: 1 on x86_64, 42 on cx and Windows
    var w = mk2(41, 7); var y = load64(&w) + 1;     # write this: bind the vector, compute with its lanes

cycc 6.7.16 (language/features.cyml typed_simd_value_type_abi). The decisions are 6.7.16's (the user, 2026-10-19 /
2026-10-20). The 6.7.15 notes are vector_values_since_6_7_15.

A VECTOR METHOD IS THE VECTOR ON ANY RECEIVER (RV-4): `(*p).mv()`, `(s).mv()`, `f(&w).s.mv()` and `o.get(&w).mv()`
mid-chain, not only `w.s.mv()`. `var v = (*p).mv();` is an `f64v2` local, and an integer destination is refused; a
refusal names a parenthesised source as `(...)`.

PARENTHESES ARE TRANSPARENT AT A VECTOR CALL THAT STARTS A SOURCE (D25, D26; RV-5, RV-5r): `(mk2(..)) + 1` is `mk2(..) +
1`, refused at an integer destination, and so is `(mk2(..) + 1) * 2`; a method or `callptr(&f, ..)` start is refused at
an assignment as at a declaration — one rule, 6.7.14's D27 (a).

THERE IS NO VECTOR `+`, ANYWHERE (D24, D100, D101; RV-6, RV-7, SS-49, SS-50, SS-50r, SS-50r2, SS-52, ZN-13). A vector
call is refused, at the call, once:
  - as an OPERAND anywhere — a declaration or assignment's source, one further right (`1 + mk2(..)`), a compound
    assignment's right side (`x += mk2(..)`), under a unary operator, inside an argument or a condition, an index, a
    range bound (D100); `var w = mk2(..) + 1;` at an untyped declaration and `return mk2(..) + 1;` from a vector fn too
    (D24, RV-6, RV-7: the refusal names the call's first token, "got `mk2`");
  - as a whole value stored into a field, an element or through a pointer to no vector, a struct or tuple literal's
    element, a condition (D100);
  - as the `return` of a fn that returns no vector, a closure's expression body, or an argument where a word goes — an
    integer, untyped or f64 parameter, a builtin, `callptr`, fncallN (D101: "a vector value cannot be returned as i64 -
    'mk2' returns a vector");
  - as a destructure's source, `var a, b = mk2(..);` (6.7.14's D27 (a));
  - as a top-level assignment to a global that is no vector, `G = mkv(..);` (6.7.14's D38 / D27 (a); ZN-13 — it stored
    0 on x86_64 / aarch64 and 41 on cx / Windows).
Through 6.7.15 each compiled and used one word, target-dependent: `1 + mk2(41, 7)` was 1 on x86_64 and 42 on cx, `return
mk2(..)` from a `: i64` fn faulted on Windows (the tail call passed no hidden result pointer), `rd(mk2(41, 7) + 1)` into
a `*f64v2` crashed. Bind the vector and read its lanes. Unchanged: a vector fn's own `return`, value-form and `*V`
parameters, a parameter declared the vector, a discarded call. 6.7.12's "an argument to an untyped parameter runs and
discards its vector" no longer holds. The refusals are made in sync, so the next statement keeps its own errors (SS-51).
See CHANGELOG [6.7.16] (RV-4 .. RV-7, RV-9, RV-5r, RV-6r, RV-14; SS-49 – SS-52, SS-50r, SS-50r2, SS-55; ZN-13) and
docs/development/ecosystem-migration.md § 6.7.16.
